<node id="Python">
  <data key="d0">Python</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Python is a programming language mentioned as producing a much smaller version of the argument parsing program than Java.&lt;SEP&gt;Python is a programming language mentioned as using def in the same role that LOGO uses TO.&lt;SEP&gt;Python is a programming language mentioned as not having Checked Exceptions while still allowing Robust Software.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Function Name">
  <data key="d0">Function Name</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Function Name is the name assigned to a function that should reveal what the function is or does and should not hide side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;A well-chosen name for a small function, often preferable to a comment header.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Descriptive Names">
  <data key="d0">Descriptive Names</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Descriptive Names is a software design principle stating that carefully chosen names make code more readable and allow readers to infer function behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601715</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Employee">
  <data key="d0">Employee</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Employee is a payroll-domain person or worker type whose classification determines which region-specific legal minimum vacation logic applies. In this business context, the name denotes the individual whose employment attributes drive vacation-policy decisions.

In the software descriptions, Employee is a Java class or object representing an employee record. It is used as an example in code demonstrations, including the statement employee.getName(), and it appears in an assertion example with a find method associated with employee numbers, as well as methods such as employeeNumber. The class is also referenced as the owner of a nonstatic member function in a discussion of static and nonstatic methods, and it may own a nonstatic calculatePay method. More specifically, Employee is described as an abstract Java class that implements PayrollConstants and declares abstract payroll-related methods, including isPayday, calculatePay, and deliverPay.&lt;SEP&gt;Employee is the payroll entity whose type determines which pay calculation path is used.&lt;SEP&gt;Employee is an abstract class shown in Listing 3-5 that defines the abstract methods isPayday, calculatePay, and deliverPay for employee derivatives.&lt;SEP&gt;A fictional person represented in a Java code example by flags, age, and a method for full benefits eligibility.&lt;SEP&gt;A class in a code example representing an employee object with a getPay method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abstract Factory">
  <data key="d0">Abstract Factory</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Design pattern proposed for DayDateFactory so that concrete date implementations can be created without the base class knowing derivatives.&lt;SEP&gt;Abstract Factory is a design pattern used in the example for creating shapes, represented by an interface and a concrete implementation class.&lt;SEP&gt;Abstract Factory is the design approach proposed to hide the switch statement and create appropriate Employee instances.&lt;SEP&gt;Abstract Factory is a design pattern used to give an application control over when objects are created while keeping construction details separate from application code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Polymorphism">
  <data key="d0">Polymorphism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Polymorphism is an object-oriented technique that can replace repeated switch/case or if/else chains testing the same conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymorphism is the ability of a function or method to take different forms depending on implementation, discussed in relation to static and nonstatic methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymorphism is the behavior pattern that a static method should avoid when the guideline prefers nonstatic methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymorphism is an object-oriented technique recommended by heuristic G23 as an alternative to switch statements.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymorphism is an object-oriented technique used to avoid repeating switch statements in higher-level code.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanism by which the area method can differ by shape type, allowing new shapes to be added without changing existing functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Single Responsibility Principle">
  <data key="d0">Single Responsibility Principle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Single Responsibility Principle, commonly abbreviated SRP, is an object-oriented software design principle described in depth in Agile Software Development: Principles, Patterns, and Practices. It states that a class, module, component, method, or function should have only one reason to change and one cohesive responsibility. SRP is used as a criterion for evaluating how responsibilities are assigned in code and provides a practical guideline for class size by encouraging components to remain small, focused, and separable. The principle is characterized as important, simple, and often abused.

In practice, violations of SRP can be recognized through organizational cues and through small commonality extraction, which may reveal responsibilities that should be moved to another class or object. The principle is particularly relevant in concurrent systems, where thread-aware code can be isolated from thread-ignorant code. Identified violations include a function with more than one reason to change, a method that performs both runtime processing and construction or initialization logic, CalculatePay because multiple reasons exist for it to change, error-message formatting placed in Args, the responsibility assigned to ArgsException’s errorMessage method, and server code that is said to violate the principle.

SRP is also supported when design mechanisms such as Inversion of Control move secondary responsibilities to other objects, allowing the affected class or method to retain a single focused purpose.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606445</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Open Closed Principle">
  <data key="d0">Open Closed Principle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Open Closed Principle is a software design principle specifically listed in the input.&lt;SEP&gt;The Open Closed Principle is violated by CalculatePay because the method must change whenever new employee types are added.&lt;SEP&gt;Open Closed Principle is a design principle stating that software should be open for extension but closed for modification; the discussed function violates it because new types require changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Open Closed Principle is a design principle cited for the extensibility of exception-based error handling.&lt;SEP&gt;A principle of design referenced in the excerpt and described in depth in Agile Software Development: Principles, Patterns, and Practices.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inheritance">
  <data key="d0">Inheritance</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Inheritance is an object-oriented relationship in which a class derives from another class, criticized in the text when used to expose constants through the class hierarchy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790602143</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GOF">
  <data key="d0">GOF</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">GOF is cited as a reference associated with the Template Method pattern in the vacation policy example.&lt;SEP&gt;GOF is a cited reference associated with the Decorator pattern.&lt;SEP&gt;GOF is a reference cited in a footnote next to the mention of the Template Method pattern.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
