</node>
<node id="Date">
  <data key="d0">Date</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Date is a Java date/time class, identified in several descriptions specifically as the java.util.Date type, that appears in code examples and factory-method designs. It is used as a parameter to DayDateFactory.makeDate and SpreadsheetDateFactory._makeDate, corresponding to the makeDate and _makeDate methods in the DayDateFactory design. In these factories, a Date object may be accepted by _makeDate and converted into a SpreadsheetDate through a GregorianCalendar, or it may be accepted by SpreadsheetDate.createInstance and passed to GregorianCalendar.setTime. Date is also the type returned by DayDate.toDate after Calendar.getTime is called, linking it to Java calendar and time representation.

In illustrative and testing contexts, Date is used to show that a method call such as date.add(5) does not clearly indicate whether the method mutates the existing instance or returns a new value. It also denotes calendar date values expressed through day, month, and year arguments in d calls, where those values serve as inputs and expected results in date calculation tests.

Finally, the name Date is described as a class name that is already used too often in the Java library, making it an unsuitable candidate for a new date class. This reflects the potential for confusion with the existing java.util.Date and similar date-related names.&lt;SEP&gt;Date is a type used as a parameter in the example function isPayday(Employee e, Date date).&lt;SEP&gt;Date is a calendar date that can be incremented and constrained by the last day of a month.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606383</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Money">
  <data key="d0">Money</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Money is a value object type returned by calculateWeeklyPay to represent computed pay.&lt;SEP&gt;Money is a suggested code class for representing currency using integers and handling rounding appropriately.&lt;SEP&gt;Money is a class returned by calculatePay in the HourlyEmployee example to represent an amount of pay.&lt;SEP&gt;Money is a Java class representing a monetary amount used as the result of payroll calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;Money is the return type declared for CalculatePay in the Payroll.java example.&lt;SEP&gt;Money is a type used as the return type of calculatePay and as a parameter of deliverPay in the Employee example.&lt;SEP&gt;Money is the return type of the StockExchange currentPrice method, representing the current price of a stock symbol.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Function Arguments">
  <data key="d0">Function Arguments</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A referenced section on page 40 that discusses the arguments of functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Function Arguments are a naming scope mentioned in the prefix-convention example where the prefix the may be meaningful.&lt;SEP&gt;Function Arguments is a coding guideline describing preferred argument counts, with zero arguments ideal and more than three requiring special justification.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-003</data>
