<node id="Testing">
  <data key="d0">Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Testing is a practice used to minimize risk in threaded code by exposing problems, and tests should be run frequently under different configurations and load.&lt;SEP&gt;Testing is the phase where jiggling, random thread-ordering strategies, and well-written tests are applied to find errors.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601727</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reader">
  <data key="d0">Reader</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A role in the readers-writers model where threads read a shared resource that serves as a source of information.&lt;SEP&gt;A person expected to profit from comments, but unable to do so if comments are written inattentively.&lt;SEP&gt;A reader is a person who reads code and judges the effort of an author.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="assertEquals">
  <data key="d0">assertEquals</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">assertEquals is an assertion method used in Java testing to compare an expected value with an actual value and verify that they are equal. It is invoked during test execution to validate that produced results match anticipated outcomes, serving as a standard mechanism for checking correctness in unit tests.

In the provided context, assertEquals appears in Java test files, including SerialDateTest.java and BobsSerialDateTest.java, where it is used throughout the test methods to compare expected and actual values. Its use includes verifying date-related results, such as expected dates against actual returned dates, as well as checking specific expectations such as month-code values and leap-year-count results.&lt;SEP&gt;assertEquals is a dyadic function shown as assertEquals(expected, actual), described as problematic because its two arguments lack natural ordering.&lt;SEP&gt;A commonly used assertion method whose overloads demonstrate expected/actual ordering, message confusion, and floating-point tolerance.&lt;SEP&gt;assertEquals is an assertion method used to verify that the response content type equals text/xml.&lt;SEP&gt;assertEquals is an assert method used in the refactored environment controller tests to compare the expected state string with the actual state string.&lt;SEP&gt;An assertion method called in testGetPageHieratchyAsXml to compare expected and actual values.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="expected">
  <data key="d0">expected</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The expected string value used as one side of the comparison and passed to compaction and formatting operations.&lt;SEP&gt;expected is the expected string value compared in the compaction logic.&lt;SEP&gt;expected is the first argument variable in the assertEquals example and is placed before actual by convention.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="actual">
  <data key="d0">actual</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The actual string value used as the other side of the comparison and passed to compaction and formatting operations.&lt;SEP&gt;actual is the actual string value compared in the compaction logic.&lt;SEP&gt;actual is the second argument variable in the assertEquals example and can be accidentally placed where expected should be.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="message">
  <data key="d0">message</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">The message string parameter supplied to formatCompactedComparison for constructing the returned assertion text.&lt;SEP&gt;message is a variable name described as indistinguishable from theMessage in the absence of specific naming conventions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="String.format">
  <data key="d0">String.format</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Java method called by errorMessage to build formatted error messages for missing or invalid arguments.&lt;SEP&gt;String.format is a static method used to construct schema format error and argument error messages.&lt;SEP&gt;String.format is a static String method invoked by DayDate.toString to produce a formatted date string.&lt;SEP&gt;String.format is a Java method that creates a formatted string; Day.fromInt and Day.parse call String.format to build exception messages.&lt;SEP&gt;A method used to illustrate variable arguments treated identically, equivalent to a list and therefore conceptually dyadic.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="List">
  <data key="d0">List</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">List is a Java collection type recommended over ArrayList for a less constraining declaration.&lt;SEP&gt;Java collection type proposed as a replacement for the args array to allow Iterator-based traversal.&lt;SEP&gt;List is a Java container type whose name implies a specific collection type in programmer contexts.&lt;SEP&gt;List is a programming container type with a specific meaning to programmers, making the name accountList misleading when the container is not a List.&lt;SEP&gt;A collection type that variable arguments are equivalent to when all variable arguments are treated identically.&lt;SEP&gt;Java interface used as the declared type of the collected files and the return type of findJavaFiles</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Code">
  <data key="d0">Code</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Code is digital source material and a general software artifact that is written, read, formatted, erased, retyped, checked in, changed, and evolved over time. It may appear neat or disorganized, clean or messy, and can be judged by readers much like structured prose. Code becomes easier to understand and modify when consistency, meaningful naming, and refactoring tools are applied, and it can be transformed from bad examples into good examples.

In software development and maintenance, Code is the application source code and software implementation that developers create, maintain, and defend while implementing user stories and framework-based objects. It provides the executable or source-code context in which comments, methods, and classes appear. Code can explain itself to some degree, while comments may further clarify it. When Code primarily handles Problem Domain concepts, its names should be drawn from the Problem Domain, and it can read as a structured, detailed specification that implements domain concepts.

Code also functions as a formal, detailed specification that a machine can understand and execute, representing requirements at a level where precision cannot be ignored. Because Code can become messy and break under changes, it is cleaned, refactored, and evaluated for maintainability, structure, and rot, often requiring professional defense. Within the described framework, Code is treated as the only truly accurate source of information about program behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606376</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="x">
  <data key="d0">x</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">x is a Java method that computes a bowling score using cryptic identifiers and numeric literals, serving as a negative example for descriptive naming.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601731</data>
