</node>
<node id="Temporal Coupling">
  <data key="d0">Temporal Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Temporal coupling is the hidden ordering dependency in which findCommonSuffix relies on prefixIndex being calculated by findCommonPrefix before suffix calculation occurs.&lt;SEP&gt;Temporal Coupling is a required ordering dependency between operations that should be made explicit.&lt;SEP&gt;Temporal Coupling is the dependency between functions that requires a specific call order because each function needs a result from another function.&lt;SEP&gt;Temporal Coupling is a call-order dependency in which a function may only be safely called at certain times.&lt;SEP&gt;A timing or sequencing dependency that can result from side effects in functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Side Effect">
  <data key="d0">Side Effect</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Side Effect is a behavior that a function name should not hide, such as creating an object before returning the object.&lt;SEP&gt;Side Effect is a behavior of a function that is not implied by the function name, such as checkPassword initializing a session.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="set">
  <data key="d0">set</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">set is an overloaded method in ArgumentMarshaler and its subclasses that assigns an argument value from an Iterator or a String.&lt;SEP&gt;set is a method declared in ArgumentMarshaler that parses the current argument iterator into the marshaler and throws ArgsException on failure.&lt;SEP&gt;set is a function that sets a named attribute and returns whether the operation succeeds, creating command-query ambiguity in if statements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Function Signature">
  <data key="d0">Function Signature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The declared interface of a function, including its name, parameters, return type, and thrown exceptions.&lt;SEP&gt;Function Signature is the declared signature of a function that a reader may check to determine whether an argument is an input or an output.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exception">
  <data key="d0">Exception</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Exception is a general programming construct and error-handling mechanism used to signal abnormal conditions and separate failure handling from normal processing. It is preferred over returned error codes because it keeps error handling distinct from the happy path, and it is suggested as an alternative to returning null to indicate a failed operation. In Java, Exception is also a general exception type and is referenced as the source of stack traces for Java exceptions, including in the first code sample mentioned in the descriptions.

In the provided examples, Exception functions as a Java exception base class or superclass. It is the class from which ArgsException extends and inherits, including instances where ArgsException is described as a nested class. In a Java Stack example, Exception serves as the base exception class extended by EmptyException and FullException.

Exception is also involved in specific control-flow and method-level behavior. It is caught by a getDouble method, thrown by an errorMessage method when the error code is OK, and caught inside an anonymous Runnable implementation within a process method. Additionally, it is thrown by waitForClose when the closed flag remains false after a timeout, declared as thrown by analyzeFile, and caught by startSending and related helper methods for error handling.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604780</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Duplication">
  <data key="d0">Duplication</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Duplication is described as the primary enemy of a well-designed system, representing additional work, risk, and unnecessary complexity.&lt;SEP&gt;Duplication is repeated similar code that the text treats as a problem to be reduced through extraction, reuse, and pattern-based refactoring.&lt;SEP&gt;Repeated code, algorithms, or structure that represents a missed opportunity for abstraction and should be eliminated.&lt;SEP&gt;Duplication is repeated code or data that bloats software and creates multiple modification points.&lt;SEP&gt;Repeated implementation of the same idea, considered a sign that code does not well represent a mental idea.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Structured Programming">
  <data key="d0">Structured Programming</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A programming strategy for eliminating duplication.&lt;SEP&gt;Structured Programming is a programming discipline that restricts control flow to improve code structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Structured Programming is a book by O.-J. Dahl, E. W. Dijkstra, and C. A. R. Hoare published by Academic Press in London in 1972.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Functions">
  <data key="d0">Functions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A category of code smells related to function and method design, including arguments, dead code, and related issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Software constructs that can be small or large, short or long, complicated or well organized, and are described as verbs in a domain-specific language.&lt;SEP&gt;Functions are existing program functions to which new data structures can be added, although adding new data structures to existing functions is hard.&lt;SEP&gt;Smaller code units discussed earlier, whose size was measured by physical lines and whose design informs class design.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unit Tests">
  <data key="d0">Unit Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Unit Tests are automated testing practices used to verify individual behaviors within software, often serving as expressive documentation by example that helps readers quickly understand a class. They are expected to be easy to execute, ideally runnable with a single simple command or one button click, and they can remain passing while associated code is refined, even when covering clumsy lines of code. Unit Tests are also described as capable of catching the kinds of errors that the final keyword might catch.

Unit Tests are specifically associated with an automated test suite created for the Args class. This suite verifies the behavior of the Args system and was initially a set of tests that did not require getBoolean to return false for a nonboolean argument, reflecting an early behavior specification.

Unit Tests are also used as simple tests that encode knowledge about the behavior of Log4j, providing executable records of expected behavior for that component.&lt;SEP&gt;Unit Tests are tests used to ensure programs work, including short throwaway tests in earlier practice and automated tests in later practice that cover production code and can rival the size of production code.&lt;SEP&gt;Automated tests verifying individual units of production code and supporting flexible, maintainable, reusable code.&lt;SEP&gt;Unit Tests are tests that should be written just before production code to keep production code testable.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-005</data>
