<node id="Clean Code">
  <data key="d0">Clean Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Clean Code is a value-driven standard for software craftsmanship and a code-quality concept describing source code that is well-structured, elegant, efficient, readable, robust, pleasing, focused, and maintainable. Rather than a fixed list of rules, it is an ongoing state in which code is kept simple, orderly, and free of unnecessary noise and clutter, reducing rot, misused or misunderstood behavior, and maintainability problems. Clean Code communicates intent clearly, is easy to understand, resists misuse, and is produced by developers who take care of their code. It is associated with making programmers better and happier.

In the referenced book, Clean Code is both the chapter heading for a discussion of good programming and the quality standard that the Narrator promises to maintain; the Test Obsessed Wrist Band reminds the Narrator to pursue it. The chapter explains how to distinguish good code from bad code, write good code, and transform bad code into good code. Clean Code is described as the state indicated by Ward's Principle, in which each routine behaves pretty much as the reader expected. It is not treated as something that appears perfect at first; instead, it emerges through successive drafts, with developers first writing dirty code and then refining it into a more elegant form.

Clean Code is also a design approach that helps achieve cleanliness at lower levels of abstraction and provides a basis for staying clean at the system level. It is characterized by straightforward logic, minimal dependencies, complete error handling treated as a separate concern, meaningful structure, tests, close-to-optimal performance, a single-minded purpose, and doing one thing well. It can be understood as code that has been taken care of and is so well written that reading it requires little effort: its content is simple, obvious, compelling, and predictable, with each module setting the stage for the next. Clean Code is also described as a quality produced by Test-Driven Development and refactoring, serving as a discipline for improving software quality, maintainability, and craftsmanship.&lt;SEP&gt;A quality goal for code that requires attention to higher levels of code organization, including clean classes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-07.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-08.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bad Code">
  <data key="d0">Bad Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bad Code is poorly structured code that can rot, ferment, and have a profound long-term degrading effect on projects and teams.&lt;SEP&gt;Confusing, disorganized, messy code that often motivates unnecessary comments; the text recommends rewriting or cleaning bad code instead of commenting it.&lt;SEP&gt;Bad code is messy, tangled, buggy, and difficult-to-understand code that can slow releases, increase crashes, and impede programmers.&lt;SEP&gt;Bad Code is a concept for code that tempts mess to grow, tries to do too much, has muddled intent, and may include abbreviated error handling, memory leaks, race conditions, and inconsistent naming.&lt;SEP&gt;Code presented as bad in the book and transformed into good code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Programming">
  <data key="d0">Programming</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Programming is the activity of writing programs and is described in the input as a craft rather than merely a science.&lt;SEP&gt;Programming is an activity described as social in the input text.&lt;SEP&gt;Programming is the activity of specifying requirements with sufficient detail and formality that a machine can execute them, and the Clean Code chapter equates such specification with code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Requirements">
  <data key="d0">Requirements</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Requirements are the specifications whose unclear details create ambiguity that can be expressed by an ignored or commented-out test.&lt;SEP&gt;Requirements are the detailed behavior and intent that software must satisfy, and requirements ultimately need to be expressed in formal code.&lt;SEP&gt;Requirements are project conditions that managers defend with passion.&lt;SEP&gt;The requirements that changed in ways that thwart the original design.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Implementation Patterns">
  <data key="d0">Implementation Patterns</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Implementation Patterns is a book by Kent Beck cited as another source for the guideline about using explanatory variables.&lt;SEP&gt;Implementation Patterns is a book by Kent Beck published by Addison-Wesley in 2008, listed in the bibliography.&lt;SEP&gt;Implementation Patterns is a book by Kent Beck whose preface is referenced in the Clean Code chapter and contains the premise that good code matters.&lt;SEP&gt;A 2007 book authored by Kent Beck and published by Addison-Wesley.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Machine">
  <data key="d0">Machine</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A computer system used to run tests; different machines may require different tuning values to demonstrate a threading failure.&lt;SEP&gt;A machine is a system that can understand and execute formal, detailed specifications or programs, and the Clean Code chapter references machines as the target of code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Professionalism">
  <data key="d0">Professionalism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Professionalism is an outcome that comes from values that drive disciplines.&lt;SEP&gt;The professional quality expected of developers, including defending code with professional honor and making reality-based commitments.&lt;SEP&gt;A professional standard including continuous improvement.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Code Rot">
  <data key="d0">Code Rot</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Code Rot is the process by which bad code degrades, modules intertwine, and dependencies become hidden and tangled.&lt;SEP&gt;Degradation of code over time; the excerpt describes an active role in preventing the degradation.&lt;SEP&gt;Code Rot is degradation of code that occurs when slow tests delay problem detection or when tests are left to rot.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606383</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dependencies">
  <data key="d0">Dependencies</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dependencies are hidden and tangled relationships among modules that make code difficult to clean up.&lt;SEP&gt;Dependencies are connections in code that should be minimal to ease maintenance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Software Design">
  <data key="d0">Software Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Software Design is the practice of designing software; much of the practice is described as being about partitioning code into appropriate places.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601517</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Andy Hunt">
  <data key="d0">Andy Hunt</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">An author who called the rule against duplication the DRY principle.&lt;SEP&gt;Andy Hunt is a collaborator with Dave Thomas and is associated with the broken windows metaphor about code decay.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Readability">
  <data key="d0">Readability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Readability is a quality attribute of software code that describes the ease with which its purpose can be understood. It is considered a property of clean code and a factor that supports better communication among developers. Readability is also emphasized by Grady Booch and Dave Thomas as an essential characteristic of code that can be read in a prose-like manner and that allows other developers to understand, maintain, and enhance the software more effectively.

Readability is strongly influenced by carefully chosen names, which are described as roughly ninety percent of what makes software readable. It can be improved through practices such as renaming variables to express intent and removing unnecessary if statements that complicate logic. In addition, readability is affected by code layout and organization, including vertical openness, vertical density, and visual grouping, which help readers perceive the structure and intent of the program more clearly.

Beyond immediate comprehension, readability has a lasting effect on later changes to code, because clearer code reduces the risk of errors during modification. It is also a code quality that makes tests easier to understand, and it is especially important for unit tests, where clear naming and organization help verify behavior and support long-term maintainability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-07.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-08.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606391</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tests">
  <data key="d0">Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tests refer to test practices, routines, suites, and executions used to validate software behavior and guide development. In the Simple Design philosophy, Tests are considered more important than maintaining low function and class counts, emphasizing behavioral assurance over structural minimization. They can also be test routines written to demonstrate failure conditions and compared under different operating system environments, highlighting their role in exposing defects and verifying cross-platform behavior.

In software refactoring contexts, Tests denote validation runs, test executions, and automated test suites used to confirm that the program remains correct while code is changed. They pass after extraneous if statements are removed from compactString, verify that incremental changes to Args keep the program running and passing, and serve as the test suite ensuring argument parsing behavior remains correct during marshaler refactoring. Tests are also repeated test runs that pass after refactoring changes to the Args class, automated test suites passed after each small refactoring step and kept running while code is moved around, and test executions reported to pass after the set method is deployed to BooleanArgumentMarshaler. Collectively, Tests provide continuous, repeatable assurance that modifications preserve expected functionality.&lt;SEP&gt;Software tests considered necessary for clean code; absence of tests makes code unclean.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-15.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Acceptance Tests">
  <data key="d0">Acceptance Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Acceptance Tests are automated tests created for the Args class to verify higher-level behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Acceptance Tests is the suite of automated tests created for Args that verifies specified system behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Acceptance Tests is a set of tests written in FitNesse that required getBoolean to return false when called on a nonboolean argument.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601516</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="API">
  <data key="d0">API</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">API is the public interface of a class or object that can be changed to be multithread-aware.&lt;SEP&gt;API is the external application programming interface that learning tests help a team understand and that third-party packages provide.&lt;SEP&gt;API is a well-known programming interface, where over-engineered APIs can compromise separation of concerns and good APIs help teams focus on user stories.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-08.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Class">
  <data key="d0">Class</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Class is a software unit that exposes an interface, contains methods and variables, and is discussed in relation to keeping interfaces small and reducing coupling.&lt;SEP&gt;Class is a code structure whose scope contains private functions and whose methods should use the variables and functions of that class.&lt;SEP&gt;A class is a named software type, and a class name should reveal the intent of the class.&lt;SEP&gt;A class is a software structure in Java whose size is closely related to the size of the containing source file.&lt;SEP&gt;A software construct such as SuperDashboard or Version. The discussion covers naming, size, responsibilities, methods, and cohesion of classes.&lt;SEP&gt;A unit of code organization discussed as needing clean design, small size, and careful arrangement of members.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Module">
  <data key="d0">Module</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Module is a software container or unit of code intended to separate concepts at different abstraction levels and expose a small, purposeful interface. In system design, a module is a program component whose coupling to another module should serve a direct purpose, and in discussions such as Make Logical Dependencies Physical it can depend on another component. It may also be referenced as a possible dependent of a subsystem in a code example.

A module is also a unit of code or system being read or examined, and its clarity matters: modules can be puzzling and complicated, but in clean code each module sets the stage for the next. A module’s start may accumulate journal comments, and its code can become obfuscated by long logs. Under the Law of Demeter, a module should not know the innards of the objects it manipulates. When evaluated, a module is examined to determine whether it is pretty much what was expected.&lt;SEP&gt;A Module is a software unit mentioned alongside a class in the definition of the Single Responsibility Principle. A module should have one and only one reason to change.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Extract Method">
  <data key="d0">Extract Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Extract Method is a refactoring technique used to move a conditional or expression into a named method that explains its intent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601517</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Object">
  <data key="d0">Object</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Object is the generic return type required for the deployed get method so that boolean, string, and integer results can be cast to their specific types.&lt;SEP&gt;Object is the Java Object return type used by the abstract get method, requiring typed getters to cast the returned value.&lt;SEP&gt;An entity that hides data behind abstractions and exposes functions that operate on that data.&lt;SEP&gt;A software structure that exposes behavior and hides internal data; it is easy to add new kinds of objects without changing existing behaviors but hard to add new behaviors to existing objects.&lt;SEP&gt;A general Java type that can be stored in or retrieved from a Java.util.Map when specific type constraints are not enforced.&lt;SEP&gt;Object is a software element that may need to be split when the object performs more than one thing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-08.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Method">
  <data key="d0">Method</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Method is a class operation that should use the variables and functions of the class to which the method belongs rather than other classes.&lt;SEP&gt;Method is a software element that may be improved by Extract Method Refactoring when the method performs more than one thing.&lt;SEP&gt;A software routine inside a class. SuperDashboard and Version expose public methods, and cohesion concerns the instance variables manipulated by a method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uncle Bob">
  <data key="d0">Uncle Bob</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A person mentioned in the input as the author of an email about calculating possible execution orderings.&lt;SEP&gt;Uncle Bob is a named person mentioned as having used a naming practice in C++ before giving up the practice because modern IDEs make the practice unnecessary.&lt;SEP&gt;Uncle Bob is the person named in the input text as the owner of a set of personal formatting rules.&lt;SEP&gt;Uncle Bob is the authorial voice associated with the Object Mentor School of Clean Code and with opinions about clean code presented as absolutes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Javadoc">
  <data key="d0">Javadoc</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A documentation format used for Java code, including method signatures, parameters, return values, and exceptions.&lt;SEP&gt;Javadoc is a documentation mechanism used in SerialDate source comments, and the source is criticized for mixing Java, English, Javadoc, and HTML formatting.&lt;SEP&gt;Javadoc is documentation text that is considered clutter when the documentation adds little beyond the method signature and is deleted when the documentation becomes wrong after the Day enumeration change.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java documentation comment system cited as a source of redundant and mandated comments.&lt;SEP&gt;Javadoc is a Java documentation convention or tool referenced as a required function-level comment source.&lt;SEP&gt;A comment-extraction tool mentioned as responsible for adding appropriate HTML when publishing comments to a Web page.&lt;SEP&gt;Javadoc is a documentation mechanism containing an @author field that identifies the authors of code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Variable">
  <data key="d0">Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Variable is a declared name for data that should be placed in an appropriate location.&lt;SEP&gt;Variable is a programming concept whose word should not be redundantly embedded in variable names.&lt;SEP&gt;A variable is a named piece of software data, and a variable name should reveal the purpose, measurement, and usage of the variable.&lt;SEP&gt;A named value that can replace an explanatory comment by making code self-explanatory.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boy Scout Rule">
  <data key="d0">Boy Scout Rule</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Boy Scout Rule is a refactoring principle stating that code should be left cleaner than found, cited in the input as motivation for improving the ComparisonCompactor code.&lt;SEP&gt;Boy Scout Rule is the principle that a developer should leave code cleaner than the code was before making changes, exemplified by improving the ComparisonCompactor module.&lt;SEP&gt;The Boy Scout Rule is a software practice in which code is checked in cleaner than the code is checked out.&lt;SEP&gt;A rule stating that a campground should be left cleaner than found; the rule is applied to checking in code cleaner than checked out.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Comments">
  <data key="d0">Comments</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A category of code smells related to source code comments, including inappropriate, obsolete, redundant, poorly written, and commented-out code.&lt;SEP&gt;Textual annotations used in source code to explain code; the text characterizes comments as a necessary evil that can become inaccurate and misleading.&lt;SEP&gt;Comments are textual notes added to code, discussed as potentially useful, cluttering, or misleading.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Source File">
  <data key="d0">Source File</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Source File is a named digital file that contains source code, including comments, commented-out code, and position markers. It acts as a software container for program text and is ideally written in a single programming language, although realistic Source Files may contain several languages. To preserve clarity and design consistency, a Source File should not mix higher-level and lower-level concepts.

Legal comments such as copyright and authorship statements are placed at the start of a Source File. In the FitNesse context, a Source File begins with a standard comment header and contains Java code. This placement of headers and legal information helps identify the file’s contents and provenance.

One specific Source File described is a Java source file created for the Day and Month enumerations after those enumerations were moved outside DayDate. This instance reflects the broader role of a Source File as a named digital file used to store and organize source code.&lt;SEP&gt;A source file is a digital file containing code, and the size, layout, and vertical organization of the file are central concerns of the formatting discussion.&lt;SEP&gt;Source File is a file containing source code in which closely related concepts should be kept vertically close when they belong in the same file.&lt;SEP&gt;Source File is a hierarchical source-code text that contains file-level declarations, classes, methods, and nested blocks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abstract Method">
  <data key="d0">Abstract Method</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Abstract Method is a method in a base class that concrete classes must implement, thereby enforcing structural compliance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601769</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Format String">
  <data key="d0">Format String</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Format String is a schema string, such as "l,p#,d*", supplied to the Args constructor to define the names and types of command-line arguments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601769</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IOException">
  <data key="d0">IOException</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">An exception type declared by the TestNG getOos method.&lt;SEP&gt;IOException is a Java exception class declared in the GetOos method signature.&lt;SEP&gt;IOException is a Java I/O exception imported in the Server listing, declared by the constructor, and caught when closing sockets.&lt;SEP&gt;Java exception type caught when closing sockets and declared for message operations in MessageUtils.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java exception class whose occurrence in the described code indicates that a properties file was not present.&lt;SEP&gt;IOException is an exception type caught in Load Properties when a properties file is absent.&lt;SEP&gt;IOException is the exception type caught when opening, loading, or closing a FileInputStream in the snippet.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Case">
  <data key="d0">Test Case</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Test Case is a test example used to verify code behavior, but the Understand The Algorithm rule says passing Test Cases is not sufficient by itself.&lt;SEP&gt;A Test Case is an executable test item that may be disabled or turned off, as illustrated by the really big file example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Redundant Comment">
  <data key="d0">Redundant Comment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Redundant Comment is a comment that repeats existing information and is considered a place to collect lies and misinformation.&lt;SEP&gt;A code comment that repeats the meaning of the surrounding code without adding intent, rationale, or clarity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Source Code Control System">
  <data key="d0">Source Code Control System</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A record-keeping system that stores code history and can preserve commented-out or deleted code as previous versions.&lt;SEP&gt;A system that remembers deleted source code, reducing the fear of deleting a dead function.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601770</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IDE">
  <data key="d0">IDE</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An integrated development environment where unit tests may be run by clicking one button.&lt;SEP&gt;IDE is an integrated development environment that can mark covered lines in green and uncovered lines in red to indicate code coverage.&lt;SEP&gt;An integrated development environment providing refactoring tools used to move and rename methods.&lt;SEP&gt;A development environment whose autocomplete can be made less helpful by ubiquitous class prefixes.&lt;SEP&gt;Development environment that hides the standard legal comment header from view by automatically collapsing it.&lt;SEP&gt;IDE is a development environment that hides the FitNesse comment header by automatically collapsing it.&lt;SEP&gt;IDE is an integrated development environment that can provide features and gestures to locate TODO comments in code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Refactoring">
  <data key="d0">Refactoring</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Refactoring, as a software engineering practice, is an iterative, incremental process for improving existing code and classes while preserving behavior. It is often supported by tests to prevent design degradation and is characterized as trial-and-error: small changes may later be revised or undone as understanding improves. It involves moving code in small steps, keeping tests running, and separating levels of abstraction; this can expose subtle markup errors and make functions cleaner, more expressive, and easier to understand, with their behavior becoming obvious. The process has been compared to solving a Rubik's Cube, where many small, enabling steps are required to reach a larger goal.

In concrete projects, Refactoring has been described as a restructuring activity undertaken after feature addition has stopped, for example to make an argument-parsing structure more maintainable. It can be applied to specific classes and methods: for SerialDate, the author aimed to understand and refactor the class while working toward passing independent unit tests; for DayDate methods, it involved moving code, renaming members, converting static methods to instance methods, and deleting unused code. Overall, Refactoring is a code-change and software-design improvement technique focused on making existing code cleaner, more organized, and easier to maintain.

A distinct entity with the same name is Refactoring, a book by Martin Fowler that identifies many different code smells. The book treats Refactoring as a recognized software improvement activity and documents patterns for identifying and addressing problems in code.&lt;SEP&gt;A code-improvement technique used to transform the long function in Listing 3-1 into the clearer function in Listing 3-2.&lt;SEP&gt;A referenced work from which the term Feature Envy is cited.&lt;SEP&gt;A code improvement approach that helps Iterative and Incremental Agility work at the code level and produces Clean Code.&lt;SEP&gt;Refactoring is the process of transforming noisy test code into cleaner and more explanatory test code.&lt;SEP&gt;The practice of transforming code into more succinct and expressive forms, exemplified by transforming an original EnvironmentControllerTest.java listing into a refactored listing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-15.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
