<node id="Scope">
  <data key="d0">Scope</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Scope is the region of visibility for variables and functions, and Feature Envy involves envy of another class scope.&lt;SEP&gt;Scope is a program region in which the same name cannot refer to two different things, making meaningful name distinctions important.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Accessors">
  <data key="d0">Accessors</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Accessors are methods used in Feature Envy examples to access or manipulate data within another object.&lt;SEP&gt;Accessors are a category of methods mentioned under Method Names as requiring appropriate naming.&lt;SEP&gt;Accessors are accessor methods that should be named for their value and prefixed with Get according to the Javabean Standard.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Socket">
  <data key="d0">Socket</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Socket is a Java class representing an individual client connection used by both the server and the client.&lt;SEP&gt;Socket is a Java network class imported from java.net and used by Server as the client connection over which messages are received and sent.&lt;SEP&gt;Java socket type imported from java.net and used for network communication in server and message utility code.&lt;SEP&gt;Socket is a Java class used for network communication and appearing as a field and constructor parameter in FitNesseExpediter and as a parameter in FitNesseServer.&lt;SEP&gt;Socket is a software object representing a network endpoint that FitNesseServer and FitNesseExpediter use for communication.&lt;SEP&gt;Socket is a communication endpoint mentioned as a source from which messages are parsed when using Data Transfer Objects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Software System">
  <data key="d0">Software System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Software System is a system whose behavior developers must understand, whose complexity can increase over time, and which should be kept small and understandable.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790602143</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Atomic Operation">
  <data key="d0">Atomic Operation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An operation that the Java Memory Model considers to be atomic.&lt;SEP&gt;An operation defined as uninterruptable.&lt;SEP&gt;An operation guaranteed to have a single effective outcome even if the executing thread is interrupted, as with the three resetId instructions.&lt;SEP&gt;The requirement to set coordinates together as a single indivisible operation rather than independently.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interface">
  <data key="d0">Interface</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A programming interface mentioned in a coding standard that may require an interface for each class.&lt;SEP&gt;Interface is a boundary of a class or module that exposes methods, data, or other capabilities to dependent code.&lt;SEP&gt;A software contract between an interface provider and an interface user that defines available operations and capabilities.&lt;SEP&gt;Interface is a contract used between application code and external code, exercised by Production Code and Outbound Tests, and insulated by application-specific interfaces.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Visitor Pattern">
  <data key="d0">Visitor Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A standard design pattern named as an example of standard nomenclature for expressing design.&lt;SEP&gt;Visitor Pattern is a design pattern that gives the AccountVisitor name meaning.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Law of Demeter">
  <data key="d0">Law of Demeter</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Law of Demeter is a design principle mentioned as another name for avoiding transitive navigation.&lt;SEP&gt;A software design principle that discourages a function from navigating through objects to ask for their internal details; the text refers to Demeter violations.&lt;SEP&gt;Law of Demeter is a heuristic stating that a module should not know the internal details of the objects it manipulates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Feature Envy">
  <data key="d0">Feature Envy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Feature Envy is a code smell in which a method operates on the data of another class rather than the data of the class to which it belongs.&lt;SEP&gt;Feature Envy is a code smell associated with monthCodeToQuarter because the method appears to belong to the Month enum rather than DayDate.&lt;SEP&gt;A design smell term, cited from Refactoring, associated with external functions using another structure's variables in a procedural way.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Martin Fowler">
  <data key="d0">Martin Fowler</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The author of the book Refactoring who identified many different code smells.&lt;SEP&gt;Martin Fowler is the person associated with the code smell concept, specifically cited as the source of the Feature Envy code smell.&lt;SEP&gt;Martin Fowler is a person listed as an author of Refactoring: Improving the Design of Existing Code in the bibliography.&lt;SEP&gt;Martin Fowler is an author of Refactoring: Improving the Design of Existing Code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Refactoring: Improving the Design of Existing Code">
  <data key="d0">Refactoring: Improving the Design of Existing Code</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Refactoring: Improving the Design of Existing Code is a book by Martin Fowler et al. published by Addison-Wesley in 1999, listed in the bibliography.&lt;SEP&gt;Refactoring: Improving the Design of Existing Code is a 1999 book by Martin Fowler et al., published by Addison-Wesley.&lt;SEP&gt;Refactoring: Improving the Design of Existing Code is a book authored by Martin Fowler and co-authors, published by Addison-Wesley in 1999.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Message">
  <data key="d0">Message</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A Message is data sent from a producer to a consumer, and a consumer may block while expecting a Message.&lt;SEP&gt;A most recent message received by the current object in the current thread, which caused the method invocation.&lt;SEP&gt;A message is sent to an object in the current thread, and the receipt of the message causes a method invocation.&lt;SEP&gt;The first argument in a three-argument assertEquals overload, often read as expected and requiring cognitive pausing.&lt;SEP&gt;Message is data sent over a socket and parsed by Data Transfer Objects in the discussed communication scenarios.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Error Message">
  <data key="d0">Error Message</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Error Message is the textual message associated with an ArgsException ErrorCode value, including messages for unexpected, missing, or invalid argument conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601896</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Null">
  <data key="d0">Null</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Null is a possible return value that requires explicit checking when a function may return null.&lt;SEP&gt;Null is an absent object reference value in programming that can cause a NullPointerException when dereferenced or passed into a method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Logging">
  <data key="d0">Logging</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Logging is a boolean value obtained from the l argument and passed to Execute Application in the example.&lt;SEP&gt;Logging is the application activity of recording error information in a catch block so errors can be reported.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Component">
  <data key="d0">Component</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Component is a software container that should contain concepts at a consistent level of abstraction.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java Swing component class used by SuperDashboard methods getLastFocusedComponent and setLastFocused to track the last focused component.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java type referenced by SuperDashboard methods that retrieve or set the last focused component.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Client Code">
  <data key="d0">Client Code</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Client Code is the consuming code that calls IntegerIteratorServerLocked, obtains a value from GetNextOrNull, and checks for null instead of using hasNext.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601896</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Application">
  <data key="d0">Application</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Application is the whole software system that should not be forced to know specific classes due to misplaced general elements.&lt;SEP&gt;Application is the software system that builds and passes Maps, retrieves Sensor objects, and integrates foreign code.&lt;SEP&gt;Application is the software component that uses constructed objects after startup and is designed to have no knowledge of main or the construction process.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-08.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NullPointerException">
  <data key="d0">NullPointerException</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">NullPointerException is an exception that getBoolean can throw when a requested argument is absent before null detection is added.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java runtime exception thrown when code attempts to use a null object reference.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Java 5">
  <data key="d0">Java 5</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Java 5 is a version of Java offering improvements for concurrent development, including thread-safe collections.&lt;SEP&gt;A version of the Java platform that provides improvements for concurrent development and threaded code.&lt;SEP&gt;Java 5 is the Java language version mentioned as the point at which enums were added to the language.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java version that introduced the Executor framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Java 5 is a version of the Java platform that introduces the Executor Framework and nonblocking classes such as AtomicBoolean, AtomicInteger, and AtomicReference.&lt;SEP&gt;A version of Java in which generics support was added, causing changes to Map usage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Production Code">
  <data key="d0">Production Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Production Code is non-testing application code that is intended for use in a production environment. It is contrasted with test code and with testing APIs for engineering-standard purposes, and it must remain simple, succinct, expressive, and efficient. As operational code, Production Code can exercise an interface at a clean boundary and can serve as a model for outbound tests. It may also represent code within a system in which concurrency implementation details are directly embedded.

Production Code is the code under test in test-oriented and quality-assurance workflows. Unit tests cover it as it evolves, and changes to Production Code generally require corresponding changes to its tests. The design and use of tests can influence the flexibility, maintainability, and reusability of Production Code. In some contexts, Production Code is the application code that may contain threading defects; ConTest instruments this code, and tests attempt to prove that the Production Code is broken.

Production Code can also refer to application code already in production, where unfamiliar third-party functionality may be difficult to integrate and debug. This setting is often contrasted with isolated learning tests, which focus on controlled experimentation rather than the practical integration and maintenance challenges of real production code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-08.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606395</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adapter">
  <data key="d0">Adapter</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Adapter is a wrapping technique used in the server-based locking option to present a different API for a HashTable.&lt;SEP&gt;Adapter is a design approach mentioned for wrapping the HashTable and providing a different API for server-based locking.&lt;SEP&gt;Adapter is a design pattern recommended for changing an API and adding locking when the server code cannot be directly modified.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601910</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Map">
  <data key="d0">Map</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Map is a Java collection type used in the Args.java first draft to store boolean, string, and integer argument values keyed by Character.&lt;SEP&gt;Map is a Java mapping interface used by Args for argument storage collections such as stringArgs, booleanArgs, and intArgs.&lt;SEP&gt;Map is a generic collection type used by Args to store ArgumentMarshaler instances keyed by argument characters.&lt;SEP&gt;Map is the Java generic map type used for the argument marshaler fields in Args.&lt;SEP&gt;Map is a wrapper used to wrap third-party code, reducing the number of places in the code that refer to third-party particulars.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Protected">
  <data key="d0">Protected</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Protected is an access modifier discussed as not constraining enough when applied to all variables by default.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790602111</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Separation Of Concerns">
  <data key="d0">Separation Of Concerns</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Separation Of Concerns is the design property that makes code simpler to understand and maintain.&lt;SEP&gt;Separation Of Concerns is a design principle that minimizes coupling between domains, architecture, and implementation concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;Separation Of Concerns is a programming and software-design concern. Maintaining a separation of concerns is important in programming activities and in programs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Separation of Concerns">
  <data key="d0">Separation of Concerns</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Separation of Concerns is a structural benefit offered by concurrency because a concurrent application resembles many little collaborating computers.&lt;SEP&gt;A design outcome of partitioning described as making code simpler to understand and maintain.&lt;SEP&gt;Separation of Concerns is described as an old and important design technique used to keep construction, startup, runtime behavior, and other responsibilities distinct.&lt;SEP&gt;A proper separation that allows software system architectures to grow incrementally.&lt;SEP&gt;Separation of Concerns is a software design principle that the EJB2 architecture approaches in some areas by declaring transactional, security, and persistence behaviors in deployment descriptors independently of source code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Single Responsibility">
  <data key="d0">Single Responsibility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The requirement that a method perform one narrow task, reflected in the instruction that the compacting method should do nothing but compacting.&lt;SEP&gt;Single Responsibility is a design principle in which a class encapsulates one distinct responsibility, as attributed to each small class in the text.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Large Function">
  <data key="d0">Large Function</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Large Function is a sizable function that selector arguments may avoid splitting.&lt;SEP&gt;Large Function is the unnamed function discussed as large, doing more than one thing, and having a structure that violates design principles.&lt;SEP&gt;A function containing many variables and behavior that can be broken into smaller functions, often leading to additional classes and improved organization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stack">
  <data key="d0">Stack</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Stack is a Java interface used as an example that declares push, pop, and percentFull.&lt;SEP&gt;A cohesive Java class that implements a stack using a topOfStack count and an element list, providing size, push, and pop methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tight Coupling">
  <data key="d0">Tight Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tight Coupling is a design weakness that makes it difficult to write tests and is reduced through dependency injection, interfaces, and abstraction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790602143</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Size Method">
  <data key="d0">Size Method</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Size Method is a Java method in a collection class that can be used as the definition for IsEmpty Method to eliminate duplication.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790602111</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Suite">
  <data key="d0">Test Suite</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Test Suite is a collection of tests expected to cover everything that could possibly break in the system under test.&lt;SEP&gt;A collection of tests maintained by a software team to verify the behavior of production code.&lt;SEP&gt;Test Suite is a collection of tests written to verify the precise behavior of the original program before refactoring changes were applied.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Microsoft">
  <data key="d0">Microsoft</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Microsoft is the organization referenced through Microsoft Excel and the Microsoft Website.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsoft is the organization whose website hosts support article Q181370 about Excel's 1900 leap-year behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsoft is a company whose shares, referenced by the symbol MSFT, are added to the portfolio in the test scenario.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsoft is a stock issuer used in the test example, where a test implementation returns a fixed price for Microsoft shares.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-032&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interfaces">
  <data key="d0">Interfaces</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Interfaces are a technique used to minimize coupling when writing tests and improving design.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790602111</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Three Laws of TDD">
  <data key="d0">Three Laws of TDD</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Three Laws of TDD are the test-driven development laws whose adherence naturally implies testability.&lt;SEP&gt;Three Laws of TDD are rules for Test Driven Development that require a failing unit test before production code, limit unit test code to what is sufficient to fail, and limit production code to what is sufficient to pass the failing test.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Timer">
  <data key="d0">Timer</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Timer is an object used in the conditional extraction example for deletion logic.&lt;SEP&gt;Timer is a class in the C++ Program that implements a simple timer and includes the ScheduleCommand method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Operating System">
  <data key="d0">Operating System</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Operating System is the software environment that may or may not preempt threads, affecting whether a particular algorithm always works.&lt;SEP&gt;A software environment that can affect test behavior; a different operating system may require retuning the threading test with different values.&lt;SEP&gt;Operating System is the software system from which well-designed unit tests isolate production code instead of relying on standard timing functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PathParser">
  <data key="d0">PathParser</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A class whose render method is used by doRename to convert pathToRename into a returned path string.&lt;SEP&gt;PathParser is a code component used in the example to render a page path name.&lt;SEP&gt;PathParser is a class used to parse page path strings into WikiPagePath objects in a clarification example.&lt;SEP&gt;PathParser is a utility used to parse resource strings into paths and render full page paths.&lt;SEP&gt;PathParser is a utility class used by makePageResponse to render the full page path into pageTitle.&lt;SEP&gt;PathParser is a class whose parse method transforms strings into PagePath instances used by crawlers.&lt;SEP&gt;A utility used to parse page path strings.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Production Environment">
  <data key="d0">Production Environment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Production Environment is a deployment context where instrumented code should not remain because instrumented code can unnecessarily slow execution.&lt;SEP&gt;The real-world operating conditions that varying-load tests aim to approximate.&lt;SEP&gt;The execution environment for production code, described as having needs that differ from a test environment and may have constrained resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Production Environment is an environment where operations involving inefficiency may be unacceptable, as part of the dual standard.&lt;SEP&gt;Production Environment is an environment in which repeatable tests should be able to run.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="XML">
  <data key="d0">XML</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A markup language that may appear in a Java source file or a JSP file.&lt;SEP&gt;Configuration file format used for declarative configuration in Spring and EJB3, though it can be verbose and hard to read.&lt;SEP&gt;XML is the markup and content format asserted in the responder tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Structured markup content returned by getContent and validated by the page hierarchy test.&lt;SEP&gt;XML is a markup language expected as a response in the SerializedPageResponderTest example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Testability">
  <data key="d0">Testability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Testability is the ease with which thread-related code can be run repeatedly in many configurations, and it implies a level of plug-ability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601928</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Design Patterns">
  <data key="d0">Design Patterns</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Standardized design solutions whose names function as standard nomenclature for communicating design to other developers.&lt;SEP&gt;Well-known patterns that often serve as ways to eliminate duplication in software design.&lt;SEP&gt;Reusable software design constructs mentioned as an abstraction level that Domain-Specific Languages raise above.&lt;SEP&gt;A concept or body of work behind which Ward Cunningham is described as a motive force.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Main Function">
  <data key="d0">Main Function</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Main Function is the Java main method in the usage example that receives the command-line argument array and creates an Args instance.&lt;SEP&gt;Main Function is the entry point or main function that can contain all construction logic so that the application receives objects that have already been built and wired.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LineItem">
  <data key="d0">LineItem</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">LineItem is a Java class that stores a name, whole hours, and tenths for one line in an hourly report.&lt;SEP&gt;LineItem is a class within HourlyReporter that stores the fields name, hours, and tenths.&lt;SEP&gt;LineItem is a software object in an order processing example that is created by the application and added to an Order.&lt;SEP&gt;A software object instance created in an order processing application and added to an Order.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chicago">
  <data key="d0">Chicago</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Chicago is the geographic location of the cold winter of 1971 during which the locking lesson was learned.&lt;SEP&gt;Chicago is the city where a new hotel under construction is visible from a window.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unit Testing">
  <data key="d0">Unit Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Unit Testing is the practice represented by the unit tests in SerialDateTests and by the independent unit tests written for SerialDate; it is used to check behavior and to measure coverage.&lt;SEP&gt;Unit Testing is a testing activity in which service dependencies may require Test Double or Mock Object assignment and all execution paths of mixed construction and runtime methods should be tested.&lt;SEP&gt;Unit Testing is a software testing practice that is difficult in EJB2 because tight coupling to the heavyweight container requires mocking the container or deploying EJBs and tests to a real server.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dependency Injection">
  <data key="d0">Dependency Injection</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Dependency Injection is a tool or technique used with Dependency Inversion Principle to minimize coupling.&lt;SEP&gt;A mechanism for separating construction from use, described as the application of Inversion of Control to dependency management.&lt;SEP&gt;Container-driven mechanism that instantiates major objects and wires them together on demand.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test-Driven Development">
  <data key="d0">Test-Driven Development</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Test-Driven Development is a development discipline used to keep the system working during refactoring by making changes that do not break behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Test-Driven Development is a development approach whose doctrine requires the system to keep running at all times during changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Test-Driven Development is a software development method that the Narrator learned from Kent Beck in 1999 and describes as a source of professional obsession.&lt;SEP&gt;A development approach that helps Iterative and Incremental Agility work at the code level and produces Clean Code.&lt;SEP&gt;A development practice whose benefits depend on preserved agility and productivity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-046&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JVM">
  <data key="d0">JVM</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Runtime environment referenced in the input as the JVM, which does not guarantee preemptive threading.&lt;SEP&gt;JVM is the Java virtual machine mentioned as not guaranteeing preemptive threading, which affects whether particular algorithms always work on an operating system.&lt;SEP&gt;The Java Virtual Machine, referred to as the JVM, is a runtime environment whose version, along with machine and operating system, can require different tuning values for a threading test to expose failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790602143</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JDK">
  <data key="d0">JDK</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Java Development Kit that includes the java.util.concurrent package and related concurrency classes.&lt;SEP&gt;JDK is the Java Development Kit that provides dynamic proxies, which work only with interfaces.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CGLIB">
  <data key="d0">CGLIB</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">CGLIB is a tool mentioned as a possible technology for programmatically instrumenting code.&lt;SEP&gt;CGLIB is a byte-code manipulation library mentioned as an option for proxying classes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ASM">
  <data key="d0">ASM</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ASM is a tool mentioned as a possible technology for programmatically instrumenting code.&lt;SEP&gt;ASM is a byte-code manipulation library mentioned as an option for proxying classes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="POJO">
  <data key="d0">POJO</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">POJO is a plain old Java object that knows nothing about threading and is used to separate thread-ignorant behavior from thread-aware control code.&lt;SEP&gt;POJO is a Plain-Old Java Object style in which objects focus on domain logic and avoid dependencies on enterprise frameworks, making them simpler and easier to test.&lt;SEP&gt;POJO is a Plain Old Java Object used to implement domain logic while remaining decoupled from architecture concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;POJO is the object style applied to the Bank object, whose basic behavior is extended by nested decorators.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Decorator">
  <data key="d0">Decorator</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A standard design pattern whose convention is reflected in the names of decorating classes, such as AutoHangupModemDecorator.&lt;SEP&gt;Pattern noted as part of the combination used by DayDateFactory static methods that delegate to abstract methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Design pattern in which nested objects extend the basic behavior of an object, as illustrated by the Russian doll analogy around Bank.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Client">
  <data key="d0">Client</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">A Client is the calling side in client-based locking that locks the server before calling the first method and maintains the lock through the last method.&lt;SEP&gt;Client is the software component that uses a server object and, under client-based locking, must perform locking.&lt;SEP&gt;A consumer that uses a local or remote interface defined for an EJB.&lt;SEP&gt;Client is the calling component that believes the interaction targets Bank directly while nested decorators handle the actual interaction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Software Project">
  <data key="d0">Software Project</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Software Project is a software development effort whose long-term maintenance is described as the majority of its cost.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790602143</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="POJOs">
  <data key="d0">POJOs</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Plain old Java objects that are not thread aware and can be tested outside a threaded environment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790602143</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Magic Numbers">
  <data key="d0">Magic Numbers</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Magic Numbers are unexplained numeric literals that obscure intent, such as the value 400 in m_otCalc.&lt;SEP&gt;Magic Numbers are numeric literals in code whose meaning is not clear from the name or surrounding code, as illustrated by the poorly named scoring method.&lt;SEP&gt;Magic numbers are unexplained numeric constants in code, such as the value 4, that obscure intent until the values are given names or methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="d">
  <data key="d0">d</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A private static helper method that creates and returns a SpreadsheetDate object from day, month, and year arguments.&lt;SEP&gt;A date construction or factory method used to create date values for assertions and method calls in BobsSerialDateTest.java.&lt;SEP&gt;A date-creation helper method used in BobsSerialDateTest.java to construct date values such as d(18, APRIL, 2006).&lt;SEP&gt;d is a helper method invoked with day, month, and year arguments to produce a date value used in equality assertions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-030&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-031</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601627</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data">
  <data key="d0">Data</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Information that can be partitioned into independent subsets for concurrent processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Data is the information within an object that a method may access or manipulate.&lt;SEP&gt;Data is a generic naming term used as a noise word in identifiers such as ProductData.&lt;SEP&gt;Data is an example word to avoid in the name of a class.&lt;SEP&gt;Data is a generic word that the naming guidance says to avoid in class names.&lt;SEP&gt;Data is the domain in which Codd's database normal forms eliminate duplication.&lt;SEP&gt;Data is internal information hidden by objects and exposed by data structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Source">
  <data key="d0">Source</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Source is the string parameter passed to compactString and provides the substring inserted between DELTA_START and DELTA_END.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601627</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compiler">
  <data key="d0">Compiler</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Compiler is the Java tool that automatically generates serialVersionUID when the variable is not declared.&lt;SEP&gt;Compiler is a software tool that can be satisfied by syntactically valid but arbitrary name differences.&lt;SEP&gt;A software tool that recognizes the address context of variables when the variables are enclosed in the Address class.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hungarian Notation">
  <data key="d0">Hungarian Notation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Hungarian Notation is a naming convention that obscures the author intent in the m_otCalc example.&lt;SEP&gt;A naming style that encodes type or scope information in prefixes, such as m_ or f, and is described as pollution to avoid.&lt;SEP&gt;Hungarian Notation is a historical naming convention that encodes variable type information into names and was considered important in the Windows C API when compilers did not check types.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Member Variables">
  <data key="d0">Member Variables</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The class-level storage introduced when compactExpected and compactActual are promoted from local values to fields shared by the class.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601627</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bug">
  <data key="d0">Bug</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Bug is the suspected nonfunctional guard in compactString that becomes apparent after the suffix length correction.&lt;SEP&gt;Bug is a defect in a function that may indicate nearby defects and can motivate exhaustive testing of the affected function.&lt;SEP&gt;Bug is a defect that is hard to hide when code logic is straightforward.&lt;SEP&gt;A software defect in the killer app that was not repaired from one release to the next and contributed to product decline.&lt;SEP&gt;An unintended defect or possible fault introduced by a code change.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concrete Class">
  <data key="d0">Concrete Class</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concrete Class is a class that must implement all abstract methods required by a base class.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601627</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Users">
  <data key="d0">Users</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Users are the people who receive error messages from ArgsException and may write custom messages if supplied messages are disliked.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601627</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Arguments">
  <data key="d0">Arguments</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Arguments is a class in the FitNesse example that stores command-line argument data and default configuration constants.&lt;SEP&gt;Arguments are values passed to a function, and the text describes them as requiring conceptual power and increasing testing difficulty.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Processor">
  <data key="d0">Processor</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">A computing processor in which independent threads may execute.&lt;SEP&gt;Processor is a hardware component used as a comparison when running threaded code with more threads than processors.&lt;SEP&gt;Processor is a class of computational activity involving numerical calculations, regular expression processing, and garbage collection that can make a process processor bound.&lt;SEP&gt;Processor is an example word to avoid in the name of a class.&lt;SEP&gt;Processor is a generic word that the naming guidance says to avoid in class names.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="get">
  <data key="d0">get</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Method called on a GregorianCalendar instance to retrieve a calendar field such as Calendar.DATE.&lt;SEP&gt;get is a method in ArgumentMarshaler and its subclasses that returns the stored argument value.&lt;SEP&gt;get is a method declared in ArgumentMarshaler that returns the parsed argument value as Object.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-016</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790601627</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter 12: Emergence">
  <data key="d0">Chapter 12: Emergence</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Chapter 12: Emergence is a chapter that presents the topic of getting clean via emergent design.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Emergent Design">
  <data key="d0">Emergent Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Emergent Design is an approach in which following simple design rules helps create good designs and facilitates the emergence of good designs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Four Rules Of Simple Design">
  <data key="d0">Four Rules Of Simple Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Four Rules Of Simple Design are a prioritized set of principles associated with simple design, with keeping function and class counts low described as the lowest-priority rule.&lt;SEP&gt;Four Rules Of Simple Design is a set of ordered simple design rules: runs all tests, contains no duplication, expresses programmer intent, and minimizes number of classes and methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Simple Design">
  <data key="d0">Simple Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Simple Design is a software design practice that emphasizes keeping systems, functions, and classes small while prioritizing tests, eliminating duplication, and clear expression.&lt;SEP&gt;Simple Design is a software design that follows the four ordered rules of running tests, avoiding duplication, expressing programmer intent, and minimizing classes and methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Runs All The Tests">
  <data key="d0">Runs All The Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Runs All The Tests is the first and most important simple design rule requiring a design to produce a system that acts as intended and passes tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contains No Duplication">
  <data key="d0">Contains No Duplication</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Contains No Duplication is a simple design rule requiring elimination of duplicate code or implementation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Expresses The Intent Of The Programmer">
  <data key="d0">Expresses The Intent Of The Programmer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Expresses The Intent Of The Programmer is a simple design rule requiring code to express the programmer's intent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Minimizes The Number Of Classes And Methods">
  <data key="d0">Minimizes The Number Of Classes And Methods</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Minimizes The Number Of Classes And Methods is a simple design rule requiring reduction of the number of classes and methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SRP">
  <data key="d0">SRP</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">SRP is a design principle referenced in the text; classes conforming to SRP are easier to test.&lt;SEP&gt;SRP is the abbreviation for the Single Responsibility Principle. SRP is described as an important and simple concept in OO design, and as one of the most abused class design principles.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DIP">
  <data key="d0">DIP</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">DIP is a design principle referenced in the text; DIP is used with dependency injection, interfaces, and abstraction to minimize coupling.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Testable System">
  <data key="d0">Testable System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Testable System is a system that is comprehensively tested and passes all tests all of the time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Verification">
  <data key="d0">Verification</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Verification is the ability to confirm that a system acts as intended.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deployment">
  <data key="d0">Deployment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Deployment is the act of placing a system into use; the text states an unverifiable system should never be deployed.&lt;SEP&gt;Deployment is the process of releasing and redeploying software components, discussed in relation to base classes, derivative classes, jar files, and independent components.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abstraction">
  <data key="d0">Abstraction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Abstraction is a technique used to minimize coupling when writing tests and improving design.&lt;SEP&gt;Abstraction is a level of detail in software design where higher level concepts are separated from lower level implementation details.&lt;SEP&gt;Abstraction is a generalization that can replace duplicated code and separate higher-level general concepts from lower-level detailed concepts.&lt;SEP&gt;Abstraction is described as an appropriate level that lets individuals and components work effectively without understanding the entire big picture.&lt;SEP&gt;A simple abstraction built early in a program to support change and reduce arbitrary collection behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Low Coupling">
  <data key="d0">Low Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Low Coupling is a primary object-oriented design goal that improves when tests, dependency injection, interfaces, and abstraction are used.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="High Cohesion">
  <data key="d0">High Cohesion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">High Cohesion is a primary object-oriented design goal that improves through testing and refactoring.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
