  <data key="d0">Vacation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Vacation is the employee benefit calculated from hours worked, adjusted for legal minimums, and applied to a payroll record.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Good Names">
  <data key="d0">Good Names</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Good Names are descriptive class and function names that allow readers to understand responsibilities without surprise.&lt;SEP&gt;Names chosen for code elements that are intended to be descriptive and precise.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Small Functions And Classes">
  <data key="d0">Small Functions And Classes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Small Functions And Classes are code units kept deliberately small to make naming, writing, and understanding easier.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Standard Nomenclature">
  <data key="d0">Standard Nomenclature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Standard Nomenclature is the use of established terminology, including design pattern names, to communicate design to other developers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Documentation By Example">
  <data key="d0">Documentation By Example</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Documentation By Example is the role of unit tests in helping readers quickly understand what a class does through executable examples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Workmanship">
  <data key="d0">Workmanship</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Workmanship is the care taken in naming, splitting, and polishing functions and classes so that code remains readable for future maintainers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coding Standard">
  <data key="d0">Coding Standard</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Coding Standard is a prescribed rule, such as requiring an interface for every class, that may produce dogmatic class proliferation.&lt;SEP&gt;Coding Standard is a team-level set of conventions for variable declaration, naming, braces, and other code style choices.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dogmatism">
  <data key="d0">Dogmatism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dogmatism is described as pointless adherence to design rules such as creating an interface for every class or separating fields and behavior into data classes and behavior classes.&lt;SEP&gt;Dogmatism is rigid adherence to coding rules, such as separating fields and behavior into separate classes, that the text advises resisting.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pragmatic Approach">
  <data key="d0">Pragmatic Approach</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A Pragmatic Approach is a practical stance that resists dogmatic rules while keeping overall systems, functions, and classes small.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="US">
  <data key="d0">US</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">US is the region associated with US-specific legal minimum vacation rules and the USVacationPolicy implementation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EU">
  <data key="d0">EU</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">EU is the region associated with EU-specific legal minimum vacation rules and the EUVacationPolicy implementation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="accrueEuropeanDivisionVacation">
  <data key="d0">accrueEuropeanDivisionVacation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">accrueEuropeanDivisionVacation is a vacation accrual method named in the text as largely duplicating accrueUSDivisionVacation except for legal minimums.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="High Class And Method Counts">
  <data key="d0">High Class And Method Counts</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">High Class And Method Counts are elevated counts of classes and methods that are sometimes described as the result of pointless dogmatism.&lt;SEP&gt;High Class And Method Counts are excessive counts of classes and methods in a software system, often resulting from dogmatic coding practices.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Low Function And Class Counts">
  <data key="d0">Low Function And Class Counts</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Low function and class counts is a simple design rule that advocates minimizing the number of functions and classes in a system while resisting dogmatic practices that inflate those counts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Experience">
  <data key="d0">Experience</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Experience refers to many decades of professional expertise that are crystallized into the simple design practices described in the text.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Good Principles And Patterns">
  <data key="d0">Good Principles And Patterns</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Good principles and patterns are software design practices and patterns that developers can learn over many years but may adopt more readily by following simple design.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change">
  <data key="d0">Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Extreme Programming Explained: Embrace Change is a 1999 book by Kent Beck published by Addison-Wesley.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Eliminate Duplication">
  <data key="d0">Eliminate Duplication</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Eliminate Duplication is the simple design practice of removing duplicated code, considered more important than keeping function and class counts low.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Express Yourself">
  <data key="d0">Express Yourself</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Express Yourself is the simple design practice of making code clear, considered more important than keeping function and class counts low.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interface For Each Class">
  <data key="d0">Interface For Each Class</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Interface For Each Class is a dogmatic coding standard that insists on creating an interface for each and every class.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Small Overall System">
  <data key="d0">Small Overall System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Small Overall System is the design goal of keeping the entire system small while keeping functions and classes small.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790593524</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gamma Et Al.">
  <data key="d0">Gamma Et Al.</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Gamma Et Al. are the cited authors of Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object Oriented Software.&lt;SEP&gt;Gamma Et Al. is the cited author group for Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object Oriented Software.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-d813b42e06df0d7b6f90cb9e254aaf18-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-12.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrency">
  <data key="d0">Concurrency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrency is a decoupling strategy and software topic that separates what gets done from when it gets done, while presenting significant difficulty.&lt;SEP&gt;The simultaneous execution of multiple threads, whose complexity motivates separating thread management.&lt;SEP&gt;Concurrency is the principle or technique of performing multiple operations simultaneously; the text states that concurrency can increase efficiency when a process is I/O bound.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread">
  <data key="d0">Thread</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Thread is an abstraction of schedule and a unit of concurrent execution in multithreaded systems. It represents an execution flow that can execute bytecode instructions, be interrupted, and interleave with other threads, while its steps can combine with the steps of other threads to form possible execution pathways. As a concurrent execution unit, a Thread can access shared resources, wait for events, and participate in concurrency problems. In concurrent code, multiple threads may operate on shared data, and a system may involve more threads than available processors.

In the Java programming context, Thread is a class that represents an execution thread capable of starting and running a Runnable. It can be instantiated with a Runnable, such as a client handler used to execute client connection processing or client handling, thereby starting concurrent execution. Thread is also a Java construct used in tests to execute a Runnable and to represent concurrent tasks in performance testing.

Thread objects can be created by code and may be pooled by an Executor Framework or Thread Pool. In specific testing scenarios, Thread is represented by instances such as t1 and t2, which run an anonymous inner class that uses ClassWithThreadingProblem. A Thread can share a PageIterator, use a separate PageReader, participate in multithreaded throughput calculations, or take part in deadlock scenarios. Additionally, the Server process method may use Thread.sleep to pause execution for 1000 milliseconds.&lt;SEP&gt;Thread is a class used in the concurrent test to start many concurrent widget-building operations.&lt;SEP&gt;Thread is a new execution thread in which the execute method of a Command is scheduled to run after a specified delay.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="James O. Coplien">
  <data key="d0">James O. Coplien</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">James O. Coplien is cited for the statement that objects are abstractions of processing and threads are abstractions of schedule.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clean Concurrency">
  <data key="d0">Clean Concurrency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Clean Concurrency is a complex topic about writing correct concurrent programs and is described as worthy of a standalone book.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrency II">
  <data key="d0">Concurrency II</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrency II is a tutorial on page 317 that provides a more detailed explanation of concurrency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Servlet">
  <data key="d0">Servlet</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A servlet is a component in the standard servlet model of Web applications and executes asynchronously when Web requests arrive.&lt;SEP&gt;A Servlet is a subclass of HttpServlet that can act as if it has its own machine when using only local variables, though it may later use shared resources.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java web component that processes requests and may be involved in concurrent execution when local variables or shared resources are used.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Web Application">
  <data key="d0">Web Application</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Web application is a software system that may use the servlet model and may run under a Web or EJB container.&lt;SEP&gt;Web Application is a software application that has two shared resource pools and the Create Operation and Update Operation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Web Or EJB Container">
  <data key="d0">Web Or EJB Container</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Web or EJB container is a runtime environment that partially manages concurrency for servlets while still requiring careful programmer attention.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Web Request">
  <data key="d0">Web Request</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A Web request is an incoming request that triggers asynchronous execution of servlets in a Web application.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Information Aggregator">
  <data key="d0">Information Aggregator</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An information aggregator is a single-threaded system that acquires information from many Web sites and merges it into a daily summary.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Web Site">
  <data key="d0">Web Site</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">A Web site is a source of information that an information aggregator may visit in sequence or concurrently.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Web Socket">
  <data key="d0">Web Socket</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">A Web socket is a communication endpoint at which a single-threaded information aggregator spends time waiting for I/O to complete.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multithreaded Algorithm">
  <data key="d0">Multithreaded Algorithm</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A multithreaded algorithm is a concurrent approach that can improve performance by processing multiple Web sites at once.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="User">
  <data key="d0">User</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A user is a person handled by a system whose response time may degrade as more users require service.&lt;SEP&gt;A code class or object referenced in checkPassword to validate authentication data.&lt;SEP&gt;User is a class referenced by UserValidator and UserGateway, representing a user with a password-encoded phrase.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Computer">
  <data key="d0">Computer</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A computer is a processing machine that can process a data set, and multiple computers can process data sets in parallel.&lt;SEP&gt;Computer is the host system that ran the trap function and scheduler and contained the front panel switches used to work around terminal lock-ups.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Set">
  <data key="d0">Data Set</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A data set is a collection of data that can be interpreted, and in some systems can be processed on different computers in parallel.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Class X">
  <data key="d0">Class X</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Class X is a trivial Java class with a lastIdUsed field and a getNextId method whose shared instance can expose concurrency problems.&lt;SEP&gt;Class X is a trivial Java class containing the field lastIdUsed and the method getNextId.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Get Next Id">
  <data key="d0">Get Next Id</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Get Next Id is a method in class X that increments and returns the lastIdUsed field.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Last Id Used">
  <data key="d0">Last Id Used</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Last Id Used is a private integer field in class X that stores the most recently used identifier and can be shared between threads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stack Backtrace">
  <data key="d0">Stack Backtrace</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A stack backtrace is runtime call information that, in single-threaded applications, can often determine the state of the entire application.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Breakpoint">
  <data key="d0">Breakpoint</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A breakpoint is a debugging tool that a programmer can use, alone or as a sequence, to infer the state of a single-threaded system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deadlock">
  <data key="d0">Deadlock</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Deadlock is a concurrency issue mentioned as a risk that programmers must guard against when using containers.&lt;SEP&gt;A concurrency condition in which two or more threads wait for one another to finish while holding required resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Deadlock is a concurrency failure condition in which competing processes or waiting threads may stop making progress, including cases where threads wait for a signal that never arrives.&lt;SEP&gt;Concurrency failure in which tasks block and cannot proceed, potentially exposed by frequent task swapping.&lt;SEP&gt;Deadlock is a state in which threads wait indefinitely for resources, and the described scenario arises from Create Operation and Update Operation acquiring database and master repository connections in opposite order.&lt;SEP&gt;Deadlock is a possible system state when all four required conditions hold, and deadlock can be prevented by breaking any one of those conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Deadlock is a concurrent programming problem discussed in the chapter, with clean disciplines presented for preventing it.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrent Update">
  <data key="d0">Concurrent Update</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrent Update is a concurrency issue mentioned as a risk that programmers must guard against when using containers.&lt;SEP&gt;Concurrent Update is a potential simultaneous modification of data that requires attention to locking or transaction management.&lt;SEP&gt;Concurrent Update is a concurrency issue discussed in the chapter, and it can be prevented by the disciplines of clean synchronization and locking.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Response Time">
  <data key="d0">Response Time</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Response Time is a performance measure that can degrade as the number of users increases, motivating concurrent handling of users.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Throughput">
  <data key="d0">Throughput</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Throughput is a quantitative system performance measure that expresses the rate or amount of work processed by a system. It is commonly evaluated in terms of the completion of a series of client requests within a target time, the rate of page processing, or the overall workload completed by the system. Throughput is used to compare system behavior, including single-threaded and multithreaded solutions, and can also serve as a quantitative basis for self-tuning threading.

As a system performance property, throughput can be improved by concurrency, especially when I/O waiting can be overlapped with other work, making it a motivation for concurrent programming. However, throughput can be adversely affected by readers-writers coordination, competing threads, or processes that compete for resources. It may also become poor due to concurrency-related conditions such as Starvation or Livelock.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598956</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Single-Threaded Application">
  <data key="d0">Single-Threaded Application</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Single-Threaded Application is a program in which what gets done and when it gets done are strongly coupled, often making state inferable from a stack backtrace.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrency Bugs">
  <data key="d0">Concurrency Bugs</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrency Bugs are defects in concurrent programs that are often not repeatable and may be ignored as one-offs instead of treated as true defects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Web or EJB Container">
  <data key="d0">Web or EJB Container</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Web or EJB Container is a runtime environment that partially manages concurrency for Servlet while still requiring careful programmer attention.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getNextId">
  <data key="d0">getNextId</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">getNextId is a method in Class X that increments lastIdUsed and returns the incremented value.&lt;SEP&gt;getNextId is a method in Class X that increments and returns the lastIdUsed field.&lt;SEP&gt;A method that increments lastId using the increment operation and returns the resulting value.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="lastIdUsed">
  <data key="d0">lastIdUsed</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">lastIdUsed is a private integer field in Class X that records the last identifier used and can be changed to long.&lt;SEP&gt;lastIdUsed is a private integer field in Class X that stores the most recently used identifier and can be shared between threads.&lt;SEP&gt;An integer field in IdGenerator that stores the last generated identifier and is updated by incrementValue.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Objects">
  <data key="d0">Objects</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Objects are abstractions of processing according to James O. Coplien.&lt;SEP&gt;Objects are software entities that contain business rules, expose behavior, and hide internal data.&lt;SEP&gt;Objects are software entities that hide internal data and expose operations, and are appropriate when adding new data types is common.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Processing">
  <data key="d0">Processing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Processing is the activity that Objects abstract in the cited quote by James O. Coplien.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multithreaded Code">
  <data key="d0">Multithreaded Code</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Multithreaded Code is code that executes in multiple threads and may look fine on the surface but be broken at a deeper level.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrent Algorithm">
  <data key="d0">Concurrent Algorithm</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A Concurrent Algorithm is an algorithm designed for concurrency whose design can differ remarkably from a Single-Threaded System.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Design">
  <data key="d0">Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Design refers to the structural strategy of a system, and concurrency often requires a fundamental change in Design strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;The partitioning and organization of system components.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Overhead">
  <data key="d0">Overhead</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Overhead is the cost incurred by concurrency in performance and in writing additional code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Daily Summary">
  <data key="d0">Daily Summary</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A Daily Summary is the merged output produced by Information Aggregator from information acquired from many Web sites.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Input/Output">
  <data key="d0">Input/Output</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Input/Output is the I/O activity at Web sockets that can cause waiting in a single-threaded information aggregator.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="24 Hours">
  <data key="d0">24 Hours</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">24 Hours is the time limit for the daily run of Information Aggregator.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Testing Concurrent Code">
  <data key="d0">Testing Concurrent Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Testing Concurrent Code is the activity of testing concurrent code, mentioned as an issue concluding the chapter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Single-Threaded System">
  <data key="d0">Single-Threaded System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Single-Threaded System is a system that executes in one thread, and the design of Concurrent Algorithm can differ from Single-Threaded System.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrent Programming">
  <data key="d0">Concurrent Programming</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrent Programming involves coordinating multiple threads and is described as difficult because threads can interfere through shared state.&lt;SEP&gt;Concurrent Programming is described as a large and treacherous territory, with the chapter covering clean concurrent code, concurrent update, synchronization, locking, threads, deadlock, and instrumentation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread One">
  <data key="d0">Thread One</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Thread One is one of the two threads that share an instance of Class X and call getNextId.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread Two">
  <data key="d0">Thread Two</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Thread Two is one of the two threads that share an instance of Class X and call getNextId.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Just-In-Time Compiler">
  <data key="d0">Just-In-Time Compiler</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Just-In-Time Compiler compiles generated byte-code and influences the number of possible execution paths.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Java Memory Model">
  <data key="d0">Java Memory Model</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Java Memory Model defines what operations are considered atomic and affects possible execution paths.&lt;SEP&gt;A model stating that assignment to a 32-bit value is uninterruptable.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Execution Path">
  <data key="d0">Execution Path</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An execution path is one of the possible interleavings or paths two threads can take while executing getNextId.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Long Type">
  <data key="d0">Long Type</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The long data type is an alternative type for lastIdUsed that increases the number of possible execution paths.&lt;SEP&gt;A 64-bit data type discussed as an alternative type for LastId Variable and LastIdUsed Variable, with assignments treated as two 32-bit operations under the JVM Specification.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrency Defense Principles">
  <data key="d0">Concurrency Defense Principles</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrency Defense Principles is a series of principles and techniques for defending systems from concurrent code problems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrency Design">
  <data key="d0">Concurrency Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrency Design is described as complex enough to warrant separation from other production code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Limit The Scope Of Data">
  <data key="d0">Limit The Scope Of Data</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Limit The Scope Of Data is a corollary recommending restriction of shared data access and critical sections.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Synchronized Keyword">
  <data key="d0">Synchronized Keyword</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The synchronized keyword is a Java construct used to protect a critical section that uses a shared object.&lt;SEP&gt;Synchronized Keyword is the Java keyword that introduces a lock and guards sections of code so only one thread executes them at a time.&lt;SEP&gt;Synchronized Keyword is a Java keyword that always acquires a lock and is described as costlier than nonblocking conflict detection in many cases.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Critical Section">
  <data key="d0">Critical Section</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A critical section is code that uses a shared object and may be protected by the synchronized keyword.&lt;SEP&gt;Critical Section is a section of code that must be protected from simultaneous use, and extending synchronization beyond the minimal critical section can increase contention.&lt;SEP&gt;Code section requiring proper synchronization or protection, whose omission may be exposed by task swapping.&lt;SEP&gt;Critical Section is the small section inside PageIterator that is protected by the synchronized block.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shared Object">
  <data key="d0">Shared Object</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A shared object is an object shared between threads whose same field can be modified by multiple threads.&lt;SEP&gt;A Shared Object is an object on which multiple methods may operate, and using more than one method on the same Shared Object can create locking dependencies.&lt;SEP&gt;An object or value accessible by multiple threads, whose safe concurrent use requires understanding the memory model.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DRY Principle">
  <data key="d0">DRY Principle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The DRY Principle is referenced regarding duplication of effort needed to guard shared data.&lt;SEP&gt;A principle called DRY by Dave Thomas and Andy Hunt, meaning Don't Repeat Yourself.&lt;SEP&gt;DRY Principle is a principle that is violated when client-based locking requires duplicated locking code across multiple clients.&lt;SEP&gt;DRY Principle is a software principle aimed at eliminating duplication in code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Encapsulation">
  <data key="d0">Data Encapsulation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Data Encapsulation is recommended to limit access to data that may be shared.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shared Data">
  <data key="d0">Shared Data</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shared Data is data that can be updated in multiple places or by multiple threads, increasing failure risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Shared Data is data used by multiple threads in concurrent code, increasing complexity and the need for careful synchronization.&lt;SEP&gt;Shared Data is data accessed or modified by multiple threads, and its presence increases the risk of concurrency issues when synchronization is inadequate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Use Copies Of Data">
  <data key="d0">Use Copies Of Data</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Use Copies Of Data is a corollary suggesting copying objects and treating them as read-only or merging thread results in one thread.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intrinsic Lock">
  <data key="d0">Intrinsic Lock</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An intrinsic lock is the synchronization mechanism whose overhead may be avoided when copies of objects replace shared objects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Garbage Collection">
  <data key="d0">Garbage Collection</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Garbage Collection is the overhead that may increase when extra objects are created for copies of data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Threads Should Be As Independent As Possible">
  <data key="d0">Threads Should Be As Independent As Possible</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Threads Should Be As Independent As Possible is a corollary stating threads should share no data, process independent client requests, and use local variables.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Client Request">
  <data key="d0">Client Request</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A client request is the unit of work processed by an independent thread, with data coming from an unshared source.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HttpServlet">
  <data key="d0">HttpServlet</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">HttpServlet is a class whose subclasses receive request information through doGet and doPost methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="doGet">
  <data key="d0">doGet</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">doGet is a method in HttpServlet through which request information is passed to the servlet.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="doPost">
  <data key="d0">doPost</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">doPost is a method in HttpServlet through which request information is passed to the servlet.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Database Connections">
  <data key="d0">Database Connections</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Database Connections are shared resources that servlet applications eventually may use, creating concurrency concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;Connections to a database that can serve as shared resources in applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Database Connections is a pool of local database connections with a size of ten, used by Create Operation and Update Operation.&lt;SEP&gt;Database Connections are resources identified as inherently not thread safe.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread-Safe Collections">
  <data key="d0">Thread-Safe Collections</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Thread-Safe Collections are provided by Java 5 for use when writing threaded code.&lt;SEP&gt;Collections designed to be used safely in multithreaded situations while maintaining good performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Thread-Safe Collections are collections designed to support concurrent operations, such as the collections available in Java.util.concurrent.&lt;SEP&gt;Thread-Safe Collections are collections with extended interfaces recommended as an alternative when the server code is not owned.&lt;SEP&gt;Thread-Safe Collections are collections presented as an option for fixing nonthread-safe operations, with ConcurrentHashMap given as an example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Byte-Code">
  <data key="d0">Byte-Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Generated byte-code produced from Java code and used by the Just-In-Time Compiler when determining possible execution paths.&lt;SEP&gt;Generated instruction sequences for methods, including resetId and getNextId, used to illustrate thread behavior, atomicity, and concurrency issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Byte-code libraries handled transparently by the framework as an alternative or complement to Java proxies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Synchronization">
  <data key="d0">Synchronization</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mechanism or requirement for coordinating threads, including synchronized sections and intrinsic locks, and a source of problems in concurrent code.&lt;SEP&gt;Synchronization is the coordination of concurrent execution, and extending synchronization beyond the minimal critical section can increase contention and degrade performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Synchronization is a blocking concurrency mechanism that acquires a lock and is described as analogous to pessimistic locking in databases.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Incorrect Result">
  <data key="d0">Incorrect Result</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An unexpected outcome from interfering thread executions, such as both Thread One and Thread Two receiving 43 while lastIdUsed remains 43.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="12,870 Execution Paths">
  <data key="d0">12,870 Execution Paths</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The number of different possible execution paths for two threads executing getNextId when considering generated byte-code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="2,704,156 Execution Paths">
  <data key="d0">2,704,156 Execution Paths</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The increased number of possible execution paths for two threads executing getNextId when lastIdUsed uses the long type.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrency-Related Code">
  <data key="d0">Concurrency-Related Code</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Code that implements concurrency and has its own life cycle, challenges, and failure modes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shared Resources">
  <data key="d0">Shared Resources</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Resources that multiple applications or threads may use, such as database connections, and that require coordination.&lt;SEP&gt;Resources shared by applications, such as database connections, that servlet applications may eventually use.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Partitioning">
  <data key="d0">Data Partitioning</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A technique for partitioning data into independent subsets that independent threads can operate on, possibly on different processors.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Integer Type">
  <data key="d0">Integer Type</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The int data type used for lastIdUsed in the initial example before being changed to long.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Object Creation">
  <data key="d0">Object Creation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The creation of additional objects when copies of data are used, which can affect performance and garbage collection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Result Merging">
  <data key="d0">Result Merging</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A technique in which results from multiple threads are collected in copies and then merged in a single thread.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Synchronization Problems">
  <data key="d0">Synchronization Problems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Issues that can occur when concurrent threads access shared state without proper coordination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Threaded Code">
  <data key="d0">Threaded Code</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Code that uses multiple threads and requires consideration of thread safety, shared resources, and coordination.&lt;SEP&gt;Threaded Code is code that uses multiple threads and shared data, making testing, correctness, and failure diagnosis more complex than nonthreaded code.&lt;SEP&gt;Software code that uses threads and requires special configurations, instrumentation, and platform testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Threaded Code is the threaded version of the client/server code referenced for review starting on page 346.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Doug Lea">
  <data key="d0">Doug Lea</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A programmer and author who wrote a seminal book on concurrent programming in Java and developed thread-safe collections.&lt;SEP&gt;Doug Lea is the author of Concurrent Programming In Java: Design Principles And Patterns.&lt;SEP&gt;Doug Lea is identified as the author of the recommended book Concurrent Programming in Java: Design Principles and Patterns.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrent Programming in Java">
  <data key="d0">Concurrent Programming in Java</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A seminal book by Doug Lea that addresses concurrent programming in Java.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="java.util.concurrent">
  <data key="d0">java.util.concurrent</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Java package that contains thread-safe collections and classes supporting advanced concurrency design.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java package containing the Executor class or framework.&lt;SEP&gt;java.util.concurrent is a Java package that contains the Executor Framework.&lt;SEP&gt;java.util.concurrent is the Java package referenced in the code example as providing thread-safe collections with operations such as PutIfAbsent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ConcurrentHashMap">
  <data key="d0">ConcurrentHashMap</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A concurrent map implementation that performs better than HashMap in nearly all situations and supports simultaneous concurrent reads and writes.&lt;SEP&gt;ConcurrentHashMap is a Java map used in an example of thread-safe collection operations, where PutIfAbsent is called with an Integer key and String value.&lt;SEP&gt;ConcurrentHashMap is a thread-safe collection class presented as an alternative to HashTable, with an example using putIfAbsent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HashMap">
  <data key="d0">HashMap</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">HashMap is a general-purpose Java Map implementation that stores key-value pairs using hash-based access. It is commonly used as a concrete collection type in Java applications, including in a sensor example where it serves as the selected map type. In concurrent contexts, HashMap may be outperformed by ConcurrentHashMap, which is specifically designed for thread-safe concurrent access.

In the context of argument parsing, HashMap is used within the Args class or draft implementations to manage argument-related data. It is used to initialize or instantiate storage for booleanArgs, stringArgs, and intArgs, and it is also used in a Boolean-only Args draft to store boolean argument data. HashMap serves as the map storage for boolean and string argument marshalers, and more generally as the concrete map implementation for argument marshaler fields in Args.

Additionally, HashMap is used by Args to hold ArgumentMarshaler instances keyed by character, allowing the parser to associate each argument character with its corresponding marshaler. It is also selected as the map implementation for an argument type when parsing schema elements, making it a recurring concrete choice for storing and organizing parsed argument configuration in Java-based parsing logic.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604946</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ReentrantLock">
  <data key="d0">ReentrantLock</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A lock that can be acquired in one method and released in another.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Semaphore">
  <data key="d0">Semaphore</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A concurrency control class implementing a lock with a count.&lt;SEP&gt;Semaphore is a common example of a resource subject to mutual exclusion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CountDownLatch">
  <data key="d0">CountDownLatch</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A synchronization class that waits for a number of events before releasing all threads waiting on it.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="java.util.concurrent.atomic">
  <data key="d0">java.util.concurrent.atomic</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Java package recommended for familiarity when using concurrency classes in Java.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="java.util.concurrent.locks">
  <data key="d0">java.util.concurrent.locks</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Java package recommended for familiarity when using concurrency classes in Java.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrent Application">
  <data key="d0">Concurrent Application</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A software application in which behavior is partitioned among multiple executing threads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bound Resources">
  <data key="d0">Bound Resources</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Resources of a fixed size or number, such as database connections or fixed-size read/write buffers, used in a concurrent environment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mutual Exclusion">
  <data key="d0">Mutual Exclusion</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The principle that only one thread can access shared data or a shared resource at a time.&lt;SEP&gt;Mutual Exclusion is a deadlock condition in which resources are limited in number and cannot be used by multiple threads at the same time.&lt;SEP&gt;Mutual Exclusion is one of the four conditions that must hold for Deadlock to be possible.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Starvation">
  <data key="d0">Starvation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A concurrency condition in which one thread or a group of threads is prohibited from proceeding for an excessively long time or forever.&lt;SEP&gt;Starvation is a problem in which one thread repeatedly cannot acquire the resources needed by that thread.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Livelock">
  <data key="d0">Livelock</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A concurrency condition in which threads repeatedly attempt to make progress but are unable to do so for an excessively long time or forever.&lt;SEP&gt;Livelock is a concurrency failure condition that can occur in systems in which processes compete for resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Livelock is a problem in which several threads repeatedly acquire and release resources in lockstep.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Producer-Consumer">
  <data key="d0">Producer-Consumer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A concurrency model in which one or more producer threads create work and place it in a queue, while one or more consumer threads acquire and complete it.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Readers-Writers">
  <data key="d0">Readers-Writers</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A concurrency model involving a shared resource that is primarily read by many readers and occasionally updated by writers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dining Philosophers">
  <data key="d0">Dining Philosophers</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A concurrency problem in which philosophers, analogous to threads, compete for forks, analogous to resources, around a table.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Queue">
  <data key="d0">Queue</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A buffer or waiting structure used in producer-consumer to hold work and require producers and consumers to wait and signal each other.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fork">
  <data key="d0">Fork</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A resource in the dining philosophers problem that a philosopher or analogous thread must acquire in order to eat.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Executor Framework">
  <data key="d0">Executor Framework</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Java framework used for executing unrelated tasks in concurrent applications.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java 5 framework that allows sophisticated execution using thread pools.&lt;SEP&gt;Executor Framework is a Java 5 software framework that enables sophisticated execution using thread pools, pools threads, resizes automatically, recreates threads when necessary, supports futures, and works with Runnable and Callable classes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nonblocking Solutions">
  <data key="d0">Nonblocking Solutions</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrency approaches that avoid blocking threads when possible.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Independent Subsets">
  <data key="d0">Independent Subsets</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Independent subsets of data that can be operated on by independent threads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Independent Threads">
  <data key="d0">Independent Threads</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Threads that operate independently and may execute on different processors.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Buffer">
  <data key="d0">Buffer</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A data structure or fixed-size read/write buffer used as a bound resource in concurrent systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Buffer is an object used in the negative conditional example for compaction logic.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Producer">
  <data key="d0">Producer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Producer is a role in a producer/consumer pair that produces messages for a consumer and may receive a shutdown signal from a parent thread.&lt;SEP&gt;A role in the producer-consumer model where one or more producer threads create work and place work in a queue or buffer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Consumer">
  <data key="d0">Consumer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Consumer is a role in a producer/consumer pair that expects messages from a producer and may block waiting for a message during shutdown.&lt;SEP&gt;A role in the producer-consumer model where one or more consumer threads acquire work from a queue or buffer and complete work.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Writer">
  <data key="d0">Writer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A role in the readers-writers model where threads update a shared resource.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Work">
  <data key="d0">Work</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A unit of work created by producer threads and completed by consumer threads in a producer-consumer system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Composite Operations">
  <data key="d0">Composite Operations</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Common composite operations supported by methods in concurrent collections such as ConcurrentHashMap.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lock">
  <data key="d0">Lock</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lock is a mechanism introduced by the synchronized keyword that guards code sections and can create delays and overhead.&lt;SEP&gt;A synchronization primitive used to control access to shared data or resources, including ReentrantLock, Semaphore, and CountDownLatch.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Advanced Concurrency Design">
  <data key="d0">Advanced Concurrency Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Advanced concurrency design patterns and support classes provided by Java concurrency packages.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Producer-Consumer Problem">
  <data key="d0">Producer-Consumer Problem</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Producer-Consumer Problem is a classic concurrent problem listed as one of three foundational problems for concurrent programming.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Readers-Writers Problem">
  <data key="d0">Readers-Writers Problem</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Readers-Writers Problem is a classic concurrent problem listed as one of three foundational problems for concurrent programming.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dining Philosophers Problem">
  <data key="d0">Dining Philosophers Problem</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dining Philosophers Problem is a classic concurrent problem listed as one of three foundational problems for concurrent programming.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrent Problem">
  <data key="d0">Concurrent Problem</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrent Problem is a programming problem involving concurrency, often described as a variation of classic concurrent problems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Competing System">
  <data key="d0">Competing System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Competing System is a system in which processes compete for resources and can experience concurrency-related failures if not carefully designed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Synchronized Method">
  <data key="d0">Synchronized Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Synchronized Method is a method in Java protected by the synchronized mechanism, and multiple synchronized methods on the same shared class can create dependencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dependency Between Synchronized Methods">
  <data key="d0">Dependency Between Synchronized Methods</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dependency Between Synchronized Methods is a condition in concurrent code where synchronized methods depend on one another, causing subtle bugs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrent Code">
  <data key="d0">Concurrent Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrent Code is code that uses multiple threads and shared data, making correctness, testing, and shutdown behavior more complex.&lt;SEP&gt;Concurrent Code is software in which multiple threads and shared data interact, making correctness difficult and increasing the chance of subtle failures.&lt;SEP&gt;Concurrent Code is code executed by multiple threads that can be broken by unsafe dependencies or synchronization assumptions.&lt;SEP&gt;Concurrent Code is code that performs concurrent operations, and disciplines such as clean synchronization and locking help keep it clean.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shared Class">
  <data key="d0">Shared Class</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shared Class is a class on which multiple synchronized methods may reside, creating dependencies between those methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Client-Based Locking">
  <data key="d0">Client-Based Locking</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Client-Based Locking is a corrective approach in which the client locks the server before calling the first method and extends the lock to include code calling the last method.&lt;SEP&gt;Client-Based Locking is a synchronization strategy in which every client of a shared resource must acquire and release a lock before using it.&lt;SEP&gt;Client-Based Locking is a locking strategy in which each client must lock the server properly and is described as problematic for repeated code and error risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Client-Based Locking is a coordination option in which a HashTable is locked before use and all other users are expected to follow the same locking convention.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Server-Based Locking">
  <data key="d0">Server-Based Locking</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Server-Based Locking is a corrective approach in which a server method locks the server, calls the needed methods, and then unlocks.&lt;SEP&gt;Server-Based Locking is a synchronization strategy in which the resource or API changes so that locking is handled differently, also changing client usage.&lt;SEP&gt;Server-Based Locking is a preferred locking strategy that places locking logic in the server to reduce code, improve performance, reduce error, enforce policy, and reduce shared-variable scope.&lt;SEP&gt;Server-Based Locking is a coordination option in which a HashTable is wrapped in its own object and exposed through a different API.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adapted Server">
  <data key="d0">Adapted Server</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Adapted Server is an intermediary that performs locking when the original server cannot be changed, described as an example of server-based locking.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Contention">
  <data key="d0">Contention</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Contention is increased when synchronization extends beyond the minimal critical section, contributing to performance degradation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Performance Degradation">
  <data key="d0">Performance Degradation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Performance Degradation is a reduction in performance caused by increased contention from excessive synchronization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Graceful Shutdown">
  <data key="d0">Graceful Shutdown</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Graceful Shutdown is the process of stopping a running system correctly, and Graceful Shutdown can be hard to implement due to Deadlock and blocked waiting threads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Parent Thread">
  <data key="d0">Parent Thread</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Parent Thread is a thread that spawns child threads, waits for child threads to finish, releases resources, and sends shutdown instructions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Child Thread">
  <data key="d0">Child Thread</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Child Thread is a thread spawned by a parent thread, and a child thread may be deadlocked, operate as a producer or consumer, or fail to finish.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shutdown Signal">
  <data key="d0">Shutdown Signal</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shutdown Signal is a signal sent to spawned threads to abandon tasks and finish, and a blocked consumer may not be able to receive the Shutdown Signal.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spurious Failure">
  <data key="d0">Spurious Failure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Spurious Failure is a failure in threaded code that may appear one-off, such as occurring rarely, and should be treated as a candidate threading issue.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Threading Issue">
  <data key="d0">Threading Issue</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Threading Issue is a potential defect or concurrency problem that may be indicated by spurious failures in threaded code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nonthreaded Code">
  <data key="d0">Nonthreaded Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Nonthreaded Code is code that does not use multiple threads and should be made to work before threaded code is developed.&lt;SEP&gt;Code that executes outside threaded environments and should be verified before debugging threaded behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Enterprise Applications">
  <data key="d0">Enterprise Applications</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Enterprise Applications are software systems in which processes compete for resources and can exhibit concurrency-related problems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Processes">
  <data key="d0">Processes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Processes are units of execution or work that compete for resources in enterprise applications and concurrent systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resources">
  <data key="d0">Resources</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Resources are items for which processes compete in enterprise applications and concurrent systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Efficiency Degradation">
  <data key="d0">Efficiency Degradation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Efficiency Degradation is a reduction in efficiency that can occur in systems whose processes compete for resources.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Server">
  <data key="d0">Server</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Server is the called side in client-based and server-based locking, and it may be locked by a client or lock itself through a dedicated method.&lt;SEP&gt;An example server component with multiple reasons to change, causing a violation of the Single Responsibility Principle.&lt;SEP&gt;Server is the software object or service that can contain locking code and shared variables under server-based locking.&lt;SEP&gt;Server is a Java class in the listing that implements Runnable, uses a ServerSocket to accept client sockets, processes messages with MessageUtils, sends a reply after a sleep, and closes resources.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Producer-Consumer Wikipedia Page">
  <data key="d0">Producer-Consumer Wikipedia Page</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Producer-Consumer Wikipedia Page is the linked external page describing the producer-consumer problem.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Readers-Writers Wikipedia Page">
  <data key="d0">Readers-Writers Wikipedia Page</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Readers-Writers Wikipedia Page is the linked external page describing the readers-writers problem.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dining Philosophers Wikipedia Page">
  <data key="d0">Dining Philosophers Wikipedia Page</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Dining Philosophers Wikipedia Page is the linked external page describing the dining philosophers problem.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Failure">
  <data key="d0">Test Failure</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A Test Failure is an unsuccessful test result in threaded code that should be tracked down rather than dismissed if a later run passes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="System Failures">
  <data key="d0">System Failures</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Rare or intermittent failures in concurrent systems that may appear only after many executions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="One-Offs">
  <data key="d0">One-Offs</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Assumed non-repeatable failure explanations, such as cosmic rays or hardware glitches, that should not be treated as one-off events.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Threads">
  <data key="d0">Threads</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrent execution units that may call non-thread-aware components and interact with hardware resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Threads are concurrent execution units that can operate on shared data, use a common resource pool, and run in different orderings when jiggled.&lt;SEP&gt;Threads are mechanisms that can enhance the throughput of an I/O-bound system and are discussed as part of clean concurrent programming techniques.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrency-Supporting Code">
  <data key="d0">Concurrency-Supporting Code</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Code that supports concurrent execution and can be configured to run with various thread counts and test doubles.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Doubles">
  <data key="d0">Test Doubles</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Substitute components that threaded code can interact with instead of real components during testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trial And Error">
  <data key="d0">Trial And Error</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Experimental adjustment process used to find the right balance of threads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Performance Timing">
  <data key="d0">Performance Timing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Measurement of system performance under different configurations to inform thread tuning.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread Count Tuning">
  <data key="d0">Thread Count Tuning</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Adjustment of the number of threads, including changing the number during execution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Self-Tuning">
  <data key="d0">Self-Tuning</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Automatic adjustment of threading based on throughput and system utilization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Processors">
  <data key="d0">Processors</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Hardware processing units or cores that determine resource contention and task swapping behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Task Swapping">
  <data key="d0">Task Swapping</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Switching between tasks or threads that can expose concurrency defects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrent Programming Course">
  <data key="d0">Concurrent Programming Course</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A course on concurrent programming developed in 2007 under OS X and presented using Windows XP.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
