<node id="Preemptive Threading">
  <data key="d0">Preemptive Threading</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Threading model in which the operating system can preempt tasks, supported by modern operating systems but not guaranteed by Java or the JVM.&lt;SEP&gt;Preemptive Threading is a threading model in which an operating system can interrupt threads, and the JVM does not guarantee this behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hand-Coded Instrumentation">
  <data key="d0">Hand-Coded Instrumentation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Manual insertion of instrumentation calls into code for testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Automated Instrumentation">
  <data key="d0">Automated Instrumentation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Instrumentation approach alternative to hand-coded insertion, intended to mix configurations across runs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NextUrlOrNull">
  <data key="d0">NextUrlOrNull</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Example synchronized Java method that demonstrates hand-coded insertion of a yield call for testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Jigs">
  <data key="d0">Test Jigs</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Test harnesses that can invoke POJOs under different threading regimes to support instrumentation and testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Test jigs are test harnesses that invoke POJOs under different regimes of calls to sleep and yield.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cosmic Ray">
  <data key="d0">Cosmic Ray</data>
  <data key="d1">naturalobject</data>
  <data key="d2">Cosmic Ray is an external natural event mentioned as a possible one-off explanation for rare system failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hardware Glitch">
  <data key="d0">Hardware Glitch</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Hardware Glitch is a hardware malfunction mentioned as a possible one-off explanation for rare system failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="System Utilization">
  <data key="d0">System Utilization</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">System Utilization is a quantitative measure referenced as a basis for self-tuning threading.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Execution Pathways">
  <data key="d0">Execution Pathways</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Execution Pathways are abstract paths through code execution, where only a few possible pathways may cause failure in concurrent code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Failure Conditions">
  <data key="d0">Failure Conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Failure Conditions are incorrect-code conditions that tests are written to demonstrate, especially across different platforms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Threading Classes">
  <data key="d0">Threading Classes</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Threading Classes are classes that control threading and are separated from non-thread-aware POJOs to ease instrumentation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Modern Operating Systems">
  <data key="d0">Modern Operating Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Modern Operating Systems support preemptive threading, while Java and the JVM do not guarantee preemptive threading.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Processor Cores">
  <data key="d0">Processor Cores</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Processor Cores are processing units referenced alongside Processors when deciding to run more threads than processing capacity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vulnerable Section">
  <data key="d0">Vulnerable Section</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Vulnerable Section is a code section through which many possible Execution Pathways pass, where only a few pathways fail.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ThreadJigglePoint">
  <data key="d0">ThreadJigglePoint</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ThreadJigglePoint is a Java class with a static jiggle method used as an instrumentation point for inserting calls that can do nothing, sleep, or yield during tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jiggling">
  <data key="d0">Jiggling</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Jiggling is a testing strategy that perturbs thread execution by randomly selecting among doing nothing, sleeping, and yielding so threads run in different orderings at different times.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrency Issues">
  <data key="d0">Concurrency Issues</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrency Issues are subtle and infrequent failures that arise from multiple threads operating on shared data or using a common resource pool, often appearing under load or at random times.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Common Resource Pool">
  <data key="d0">Common Resource Pool</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Common Resource Pool is a pool of resources used by multiple threads, and its use can create concurrency issues.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Aspect-Oriented Framework">
  <data key="d0">Aspect-Oriented Framework</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Aspect-Oriented Framework is a tool category mentioned as a possible technology for programmatically instrumenting code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ConTest">
  <data key="d0">ConTest</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ConTest is a concurrency testing tool that performs a jiggling-like approach with considerable sophistication.&lt;SEP&gt;A software tool created by IBM that instruments classes and production code to increase the likelihood that non-thread-safe code fails, improving detection of threading issues.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IBM">
  <data key="d0">IBM</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">IBM is the organization that developed the ConTest tool.&lt;SEP&gt;An organization that created the ConTest tool for testing thread-based code.&lt;SEP&gt;IBM is the organization associated with the haifa.ibm.com domain in the footnote URL.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread-Aware Code">
  <data key="d0">Thread-Aware Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Thread-Aware Code is code that controls or depends on threading behavior and should be small, focused, and tested in isolation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread-Ignorant Code">
  <data key="d0">Thread-Ignorant Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Thread-Ignorant Code is code that knows nothing about threading and is kept separate from thread-aware code to simplify testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Plugability">
  <data key="d0">Plugability</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Plugability is the property implied by testability that allows thread-related code to run in a wider range of configurations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrent Programming In Java: Design Principles And Patterns">
  <data key="d0">Concurrent Programming In Java: Design Principles And Patterns</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrent Programming In Java: Design Principles And Patterns is a 1999 second edition book by Doug Lea published by Prentice Hall.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Agile Software Development: Principles, Patterns, And Practices">
  <data key="d0">Agile Software Development: Principles, Patterns, And Practices</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Agile Software Development: Principles, Patterns, And Practices is a 2002 book by Robert C. Martin published by Prentice Hall.&lt;SEP&gt;Agile Software Development: Principles, Patterns, And Practices is a book by Robert C. Martin published by Prentice Hall in 2002.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sleep">
  <data key="d0">Sleep</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Sleep is a call that can be invoked in test regimes and randomly selected by jiggling or a simple aspect.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Yield">
  <data key="d0">Yield</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Yield is a call that can be invoked in test regimes and randomly selected by jiggling or a simple aspect.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Locking">
  <data key="d0">Locking</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Locking is the practice of locking regions of code that require protection while avoiding locking regions that do not need it.&lt;SEP&gt;Locking is a discipline for keeping concurrent code clean and preventing concurrent update.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Locked Section">
  <data key="d0">Locked Section</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A locked section is a region of code that must be locked, and calling one locked section from another should be avoided.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boundary Cases">
  <data key="d0">Boundary Cases</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Boundary cases are edge conditions such as shutting down cleanly or finishing the iteration of a loop that can be thorny in concurrent code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Production">
  <data key="d0">Production</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Production is the runtime environment where jiggling is not desired and where a no-op jiggle implementation is used.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="No-Op Jiggle Implementation">
  <data key="d0">No-Op Jiggle Implementation</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">No-Op Jiggle Implementation is an implementation of ThreadJigglePoint whose jiggle method does nothing and is used in production.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Random Jiggle Implementation">
  <data key="d0">Random Jiggle Implementation</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Random Jiggle Implementation is an implementation of ThreadJigglePoint that uses a random number to choose between sleeping, yielding, or falling through.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Well-Written Tests">
  <data key="d0">Well-Written Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Well-written tests are test works that, combined with jiggling, increase the chance of finding errors.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="One-Off Failures">
  <data key="d0">One-Off Failures</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">One-Off Failures are failures initially written off as one-time occurrences, but they can recur under load or at random times.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Load">
  <data key="d0">Load</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Load is an operating condition under which one-off failures typically occur.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Simple Aspect">
  <data key="d0">Simple Aspect</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Simple Aspect is an aspect that randomly selects among doing nothing, sleeping, or yielding.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-178d6fd5b64909f0f5e42a12e3534440-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-13.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594063</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Eric Gamma">
  <data key="d0">Eric Gamma</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Eric Gamma is a co-creator of JUnit who collaborated with Kent Beck and wanted to learn about Kent Beck's Smalltalk testing framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594567</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Atlanta">
  <data key="d0">Atlanta</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Atlanta is the destination of the flight on which Kent Beck and Eric Gamma wrote the basics of JUnit.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594567</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Smalltalk">
  <data key="d0">Smalltalk</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Smalltalk is a programming language associated with Kent Beck's testing framework referenced in the JUnit origin story.&lt;SEP&gt;Smalltalk is a programming language mentioned as producing a much smaller version of the argument parsing program than Java.&lt;SEP&gt;A programming language associated with Ward Cunningham's thought leadership.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Smalltalk Testing Framework">
  <data key="d0">Smalltalk Testing Framework</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Smalltalk Testing Framework is a testing framework associated with Kent Beck and the Smalltalk language, which Eric Gamma wanted to learn about.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594567</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ComparisonCompactor">
  <data key="d0">ComparisonCompactor</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ComparisonCompactor is a JUnit module that identifies string comparison errors by generating compact difference strings such as `&lt;...B[X]D...&gt;`.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java class in the junit.framework package that compacts comparison failure messages by computing common prefixes and suffixes and formatting expected and actual values through Assert.&lt;SEP&gt;ComparisonCompactor is a Java class shown in the input that compacts expected and actual strings for assertion messages by trimming common prefixes and suffixes around differences.&lt;SEP&gt;ComparisonCompactor is a Java class in the junit.framework package that compacts expected and actual strings for comparison output by identifying common prefixes and suffixes and marking differing middle fragments.&lt;SEP&gt;ComparisonCompactor is a Java class shown in Listing 15-4 that compacts string comparison output by computing common prefixes, suffixes, and context.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594567</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ComparisonCompactorTest.java">
  <data key="d0">ComparisonCompactorTest.java</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ComparisonCompactorTest.java is a Java test source file containing multiple test methods that verify the behavior of ComparisonCompactor.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java source file identified as containing test methods for ComparisonCompactor.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594567</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TestCase">
  <data key="d0">TestCase</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">TestCase is a JUnit framework class extended by ComparisonCompactorTest.java and used for assertion methods such as assertTrue and assertEquals.&lt;SEP&gt;TestCase is the superclass of ArgsExceptionTest in the shown Java test code.&lt;SEP&gt;TestCase is a JUnit framework class imported from junit.framework and used as the parent class for the SerialDateTests class.&lt;SEP&gt;A class from the junit.framework package that BobsSerialDateTest extends.&lt;SEP&gt;TestCase is the superclass extended by BobsSerialDateTest in the Java test class declaration.&lt;SEP&gt;TestCase is a JUnit type used in TestSuite method signatures for creating tests and constructors.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-025&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JUnit Pocket Guide">
  <data key="d0">JUnit Pocket Guide</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">JUnit Pocket Guide is a 2004 book by Kent Beck published by O'Reilly, cited as the source for the JUnit origin anecdote.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594567</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="O'Reilly">
  <data key="d0">O'Reilly</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">O'Reilly is the publisher of the JUnit Pocket Guide.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-17d57202bcc8986bfbb2e844fedcd248-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-15.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790594567</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter 15: JUnit Internals">
  <data key="d0">Chapter 15: JUnit Internals</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Chapter 15: JUnit Internals is a chapter in the source text that critiques an example drawn from the JUnit framework and examines JUnit's internal code.</data>
