</node>
<node id="Heuristics">
  <data key="d0">Heuristics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Practical rules or pearls used to evaluate code, guide refactoring, and identify code smells.&lt;SEP&gt;Heuristics is a subject listed in the Cross References section.&lt;SEP&gt;Rules of thumb or heuristic lists presented in the book.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-044&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shell">
  <data key="d0">Shell</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A command-line environment where a single simple command can be issued to run all unit tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dead Function">
  <data key="d0">Dead Function</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A method that is never called; keeping dead code around is wasteful, and the function should be discarded.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multiple Languages in One Source File">
  <data key="d0">Multiple Languages in One Source File</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A condition in which a single source file contains snippets of many different languages, which is confusing and can be careless.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JSP">
  <data key="d0">JSP</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Java Server Pages, a file technology that may contain HTML, Java, tag library syntax, English comments, Javadocs, XML, and JavaScript.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JavaScript">
  <data key="d0">JavaScript</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A scripting language that may appear in a Java source file or a JSP file.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principle of Least Surprise">
  <data key="d0">Principle of Least Surprise</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A principle that any function or class should implement the behaviors another programmer could reasonably expect.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principle of Least Astonishment">
  <data key="d0">Principle of Least Astonishment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An alternative name for the Principle of Least Surprise, cited through a Wikipedia reference.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Obvious Behavior Is Unimplemented">
  <data key="d0">Obvious Behavior Is Unimplemented</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A code smell in which code fails to implement obvious behaviors that readers and users would reasonably expect.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="StringToDay">
  <data key="d0">StringToDay</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A function that translates the name of a day to a Day enum value, such as translating Monday to Day.Monday.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Day">
  <data key="d0">Day</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Day is a Java enumeration representing a day of the week. It serves as the type for day-of-week values such as Monday, Saturday, and Sunday, and it includes constants for the weekdays. The enumeration provides behavior for construction, lookup, parsing, and string representation, including make, parse, toString, fromInt, and toInt operations, and it maintains an index field for ordinal values. It initializes its weekday values using Calendar constants and uses DateFormatSymbols to resolve full and short weekday names for parsing and string output. Day is used as the target type for StringToDay and by day-of-week methods, including DayDate methods that target a previous, following, or nearest weekday. In DayDate.getDayOfWeek, Day is referenced through Day.SUNDAY, Day.fromInt, and toInt. Its introduction allowed the removal of the isValidWeekdayCode method. Because Day does not depend on DayDate, it was moved outside DayDate into its own source file. SpreadsheetDate uses it to return the Saturday constant for the day-of-week value associated with ordinal zero.

In addition to its role as a weekday enumeration, the name Day was considered as a candidate class name for a date class but was rejected because the name was already heavily used elsewhere. This rejected design does not alter the implemented meaning of Day as a standalone day-of-week type, but it explains why the name required careful handling in the surrounding date-related code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-044</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599273</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Incorrect Behavior at the Boundaries">
  <data key="d0">Incorrect Behavior at the Boundaries</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A code smell in which code does not behave correctly in boundary conditions, corner cases, quirks, or exceptions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boundary Condition">
  <data key="d0">Boundary Condition</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An edge or boundary case in code behavior that requires due diligence and testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Corner Case">
  <data key="d0">Corner Case</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An unusual or extreme case that can confound an elegant and intuitive algorithm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test">
  <data key="d0">Test</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A verification artifact written for a boundary condition to prove that code works in all corner and boundary cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Test is a JUnit framework class imported from junit.framework and used as the return type of the suite method in SerialDateTests.&lt;SEP&gt;Test is a Java type represented by elements in tests and implemented by TestSuite in the shown excerpts.&lt;SEP&gt;A test that may be written for third-party code used by an application, even when testing that code is not the application team's responsibility.&lt;SEP&gt;Verification code that may require protected or package-scope access and is treated as a decisive factor in access decisions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-08.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Overridden Safeties">
  <data key="d0">Overridden Safeties</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A risky practice in which manual control disables or bypasses safety mechanisms, compiler warnings, or failing tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chernobyl Meltdown">
  <data key="d0">Chernobyl Meltdown</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A nuclear accident in which a plant manager overrode safety mechanisms one by one, causing the world's first major civilian nuclear catastrophe.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
