</node>
<node id="Tag Library Syntax">
  <data key="d0">Tag Library Syntax</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tag Library Syntax is a syntax that may appear in a JSP file in addition to HTML.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DayDate">
  <data key="d0">DayDate</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">DayDate is an abstract Java class in the JCommon framework that represents dates. It was chosen as the abstract date type to replace SerialDate in the discussed design and implements Comparable and Serializable. As the common base type for date-related methods, DayDate provides date constants, helper methods, factory methods, tables, and enums for date handling, and it serves as the return type for date operations. In the referenced Java code example, it provides the StringToDay method, and SpreadsheetDate is described as its only current implementation.

DayDate was also the original location for several weekday and month methods that were refactored during the described changes. Its refactoring moved common date behavior, abstract methods, specific moved methods, and date interval logic into the class from SpreadsheetDate, while converting some static date calculations into instance methods. The resulting design emphasizes immutability: operations such as PlusDays adjust the date by returning new DayDate instances rather than mutating the receiver, and the class underwent multiple method changes.

Following the refactoring, DayDate became smaller and its measured code coverage decreased to 84.9 percent. In the associated report, 45 of its 53 executable statements are covered by tests, with the uncovered lines characterized as trivial.&lt;SEP&gt;DayDate is an abstract Java class in package org.jfree.date that represents immutable dates with one-day precision, maps dates to ordinal day numbers from a fixed origin, and provides day, month, and year arithmetic operations.&lt;SEP&gt;DayDate is a Java date class whose provided methods support day-of-week offset calculation, end-of-month calculation, Date conversion, string formatting, ordinal-day comparison, and range testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A superclass referenced by SpreadsheetDate in the JCommon date API.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java type named DayDate that is the declared return type of DayDateFactory.makeDate and SpreadsheetDateFactory._makeDate methods.&lt;SEP&gt;DayDate is a Java date type used for type checking, casting, and return typing in SpreadsheetDate methods and exposed with an ordinal day value.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-044</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Switch/Case or If/Else Chain">
  <data key="d0">Switch/Case or If/Else Chain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Switch/Case or If/Else Chain is a repeated code pattern that tests the same set of conditions and represents subtle duplication.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abstract Class">
  <data key="d0">Abstract Class</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An Abstract Class is a class that can hold higher-level general concepts in an abstraction hierarchy.&lt;SEP&gt;Abstract Class is the class category used to describe DayDate as a class that hides implementation and should not be instantiated.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Base Class">
  <data key="d0">Base Class</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Base Class is a class that holds higher level concepts and should remain independent of lower level derivative details.&lt;SEP&gt;A Base Class is a class that should contain higher-level concepts when abstractions are properly separated.&lt;SEP&gt;Base Class is a code structure that can enforce design decisions by requiring concrete classes to implement abstract methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Base Class is an object-oriented structure into which code is concentrated to avoid redundant implementations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Derivatives">
  <data key="d0">Derivatives</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Derivatives are lower-level counterparts to abstract classes that should contain lower-level detailed concepts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Higher Level Concept">
  <data key="d0">Higher Level Concept</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Higher Level Concept is a general concept that should be placed in an abstract class or base class rather than in derivatives.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lower Level Concept">
  <data key="d0">Lower Level Concept</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lower Level Concept is a detailed concept that should be placed in derivatives rather than in a base class.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Database Schema">
  <data key="d0">Database Schema</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Database Schema is a database design in which Codd Normal Forms can eliminate duplication.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Experiment">
  <data key="d0">Experiment</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">An Experiment is the activity referenced in the Chernobyl context that was inconvenient to run because safety mechanisms were active.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Getting All Tests to Pass">
  <data key="d0">Getting All Tests to Pass</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Getting All Tests to Pass is a software quality goal that Ron Jeffries ranks above Duplication.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Derivative Class">
  <data key="d0">Derivative Class</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Derivative Class is a class that holds lower level implementation details and extends a base class.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="percentFull">
  <data key="d0">percentFull</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">percentFull is a method in Stack that reports how full the stack is and is identified as being at the wrong level of abstraction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="push">
  <data key="d0">push</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">push is a method in Stack that adds an object to the stack and can throw FullException.&lt;SEP&gt;A Stack method that increments topOfStack and adds an element to the element list.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="pop">
  <data key="d0">pop</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">pop is a method in Stack that removes an object from the stack and can throw EmptyException.&lt;SEP&gt;A Stack method that removes and returns an element, throwing PoppedWhenEmpty when the stack is empty.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EmptyException">
  <data key="d0">EmptyException</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">EmptyException is an exception class in the Stack example thrown by pop.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FullException">
  <data key="d0">FullException</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">FullException is an exception class in the Stack example thrown by push.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="OutOfMemoryException">
  <data key="d0">OutOfMemoryException</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">OutOfMemoryException is an exception that cannot be prevented merely by checking percentFull before using a stack.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BoundedStack">
  <data key="d0">BoundedStack</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">BoundedStack is a derivative interface in which percentFull is considered more appropriately placed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Base Classes Depending on Their Derivatives">
  <data key="d0">Base Classes Depending on Their Derivatives</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Base Classes Depending on Their Derivatives is a software design rule that treats base class references to derivative classes as a suspicious dependency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jar File">
  <data key="d0">Jar File</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Jar File is a deployment container that can hold base classes or derivative classes to enable independent redeployment.&lt;SEP&gt;A jar file is a named packaged software file mentioned as one of the software artifacts that should be named carefully.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Finite State Machine">
  <data key="d0">Finite State Machine</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Finite State Machine is an implementation pattern in which a base class may select among a fixed set of derivative classes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Too Much Information">
  <data key="d0">Too Much Information</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Too Much Information is a software design rule that advocates small interfaces and limited exposed data to keep coupling low.&lt;SEP&gt;Historical or arcane detail placed in a comment, such as an RFC discussion, that is not needed to understand the nearby code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coupling">
  <data key="d0">Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Coupling is the degree of dependency between components, interfaces, or classes, and is reduced by smaller interfaces.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dead Code">
  <data key="d0">Dead Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dead Code is code that is not executed, such as unreachable branches, unused methods, or unoccurring switch cases.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Separation">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Separation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Vertical Separation is a software design rule requiring variables and functions to be defined close to usage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Local Variable">
  <data key="d0">Local Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Local Variable is a variable declared near first usage with a small vertical scope.&lt;SEP&gt;A variable defined in method scope, including this representing the current object receiving the most recent message in the current thread.&lt;SEP&gt;A variable defined within a method scope, with nonstatic methods having at least one such variable representing the current object that received the most recent message in the current thread.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Private Function">
  <data key="d0">Private Function</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Private Function is a function defined just below first usage in a class scope.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Utility Function">
  <data key="d0">Utility Function</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Utility Function is a helper function that pertains to detailed implementation, should be hidden from interfaces, and should be deleted when it is never called.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inconsistency">
  <data key="d0">Inconsistency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Inconsistency is a coding rule requiring similar operations to be handled in the same way, with careful attention to chosen conventions.&lt;SEP&gt;Inconsistency is a software design rule introduced as G11; the input only provides the beginning of its explanation about doing something in a certain way.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Principle Of Least Surprise">
  <data key="d0">Principle Of Least Surprise</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Principle Of Least Surprise is a design principle that expects similar behavior in similar circumstances and is referenced by the Inconsistency rule.&lt;SEP&gt;Principle Of Least Surprise is a principle used to decide where code such as constants and functions should be placed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HttpServletResponse">
  <data key="d0">HttpServletResponse</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">HttpServletResponse is a Java response object used in the text as an example of maintaining consistent variable names across functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="processVerificationRequest">
  <data key="d0">processVerificationRequest</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">processVerificationRequest is a method name used as an example of consistent naming for request-processing methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="processDeletionRequest">
  <data key="d0">processDeletionRequest</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">processDeletionRequest is a method name proposed as a similar naming pattern to processVerificationRequest for another kind of request.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clutter">
  <data key="d0">Clutter</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Clutter is a coding rule identifying meaningless artifacts, unused variables, uncalled functions, and uninformative comments that should be removed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Artificial Coupling">
  <data key="d0">Artificial Coupling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Artificial Coupling is a coding rule warning against creating dependencies between unrelated modules by placing variables, constants, or functions in inappropriate locations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Code Smell">
  <data key="d0">Code Smell</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Code Smell is a category of undesirable design characteristic in source code, with Feature Envy given as one example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HourlyPayCalculator">
  <data key="d0">HourlyPayCalculator</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">HourlyPayCalculator is a class containing the calculateWeeklyPay method and used to illustrate Feature Envy.&lt;SEP&gt;HourlyPayCalculator is a class associated with OVERTIME_RATE and the static method calculatePay.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="calculateWeeklyPay">
  <data key="d0">calculateWeeklyPay</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">calculateWeeklyPay is a method that computes weekly pay from hourly data and is used as an example of Feature Envy.&lt;SEP&gt;calculateWeeklyPay is a Java function that calculates weekly pay using worked tenths, straight time, an overtime flag, and rates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HourlyEmployee">
  <data key="d0">HourlyEmployee</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">HourlyEmployee is a class whose employee data is accessed by calculateWeeklyPay and reportHours in the Feature Envy examples.&lt;SEP&gt;HourlyEmployee is a Java class representing an employee whose name and worked tenths are read while HourlyReporter builds a report.&lt;SEP&gt;HourlyEmployee is a Java class that extends Employee and implements payroll calculation for hourly employees using work tenths, an hourly rate or pay grade, and payroll constants.&lt;SEP&gt;HourlyEmployee is a class that extends Employee and calculates pay using inherited constants such as TENTHS_PER_WEEK and OVERTIME_RATE.&lt;SEP&gt;HourlyEmployee is an Employee derivative created by EmployeeFactoryImpl when the employee type is HOURLY.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HourlyEmployeeReport">
  <data key="d0">HourlyEmployeeReport</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">HourlyEmployeeReport is a class containing an HourlyEmployee field and the reportHours method used to illustrate a possible necessary Feature Envy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="reportHours">
  <data key="d0">reportHours</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">reportHours is a method that formats employee hours and is used as an example where Feature Envy may be a necessary evil.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Selector Arguments">
  <data key="d0">Selector Arguments</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Selector Arguments is a coding rule opposing boolean function parameters that combine multiple behaviors into one function.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="overtime">
  <data key="d0">overtime</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">overtime is a boolean function parameter used as a selector argument in calculateWeeklyPay to change the overtime pay calculation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Object Oriented Design">
  <data key="d0">Object Oriented Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Object Oriented Design is a set of design principles mentioned as being violated if report formatting is moved into HourlyEmployee.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Default Constructor">
  <data key="d0">Default Constructor</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Default Constructor is a code construct with no implementation cited as an example of clutter that should be removed.&lt;SEP&gt;Default Constructor is a degenerate constructor at line 213 that is deleted because the compiler generates an equivalent constructor.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Enum">
  <data key="d0">Enum</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Enum is a programming language feature cited as an example of artificial coupling when a general enum is placed inside a more specific class.&lt;SEP&gt;Enum is a Java language feature that can have methods and fields, making it more expressive than plain integer constants.&lt;SEP&gt;Enum is the recommended Java type for representing month constants instead of inheriting from a class of static final constants.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Scope">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Scope</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Vertical scope is the limited vertical span between local variable declaration and usage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Private Functions">
  <data key="d0">Private Functions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Private functions are class-scoped functions that should be defined just below first usage to limit vertical distance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Conventions">
  <data key="d0">Conventions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Conventions are naming and behavior choices that should be selected carefully and followed consistently.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unused Variables">
  <data key="d0">Unused Variables</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Unused variables are variables that are not used and are identified as clutter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uncalled Functions">
  <data key="d0">Uncalled Functions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Uncalled functions are functions that are never called and are identified as clutter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uninformative Comments">
  <data key="d0">Uninformative Comments</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Uninformative comments are comments that add no information and are identified as clutter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Source Files">
  <data key="d0">Source Files</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Source files are files containing source code that should be kept clean, organized, and free of clutter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constant">
  <data key="d0">Constant</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Constant is a fixed value that should be declared in an appropriate location.&lt;SEP&gt;A Constant is a named value used in code to replace magic numbers and make the meaning of numeric or status values explicit.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Specific Class">
  <data key="d0">Specific Class</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Specific class is a more specific code class into which general enums or static functions should not be placed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="General Purpose Static Function">
  <data key="d0">General Purpose Static Function</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">General purpose static function is a static function that should not be declared in specific classes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mutators">
  <data key="d0">Mutators</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Mutators are methods used in Feature Envy examples to manipulate data within another object.&lt;SEP&gt;Mutators are a category of methods mentioned under Method Names as requiring appropriate naming.&lt;SEP&gt;Mutators are mutator methods that should be named for their value and prefixed with Set according to the Javabean Standard.&lt;SEP&gt;Mutators are functions that change data and are demanded by Beans even for simple data structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internals">
  <data key="d0">Internals</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Internals are the internal variables and functions of a class that Feature Envy exposes to another class.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getTenthRate">
  <data key="d0">getTenthRate</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">getTenthRate is a method called on HourlyEmployee to obtain pay-rate data.&lt;SEP&gt;getTenthRate is a selector method used to obtain the rate per tenth in pay calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getTenthsWorked">
  <data key="d0">getTenthsWorked</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">getTenthsWorked is a method called on HourlyEmployee to obtain tenths worked.&lt;SEP&gt;getTenthsWorked is a selector method used to obtain worked tenths in pay calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getName">
  <data key="d0">getName</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">getName is a method called on HourlyEmployee to obtain the employee name.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="format">
  <data key="d0">format</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">format is a String method used to build the report text.&lt;SEP&gt;The declared method signature with String format and Object... args, described as clearly dyadic because variable args act as a list.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Report Format">
  <data key="d0">Report Format</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Report Format is the layout of employee report output that HourlyEmployee should not need to know.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Smaller Functions">
  <data key="d0">Smaller Functions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Smaller Functions are multiple smaller functions into which a large function should be split.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="overtimeRate">
  <data key="d0">overtimeRate</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">overtimeRate is a variable in calculateWeeklyPay that holds the multiplier used to compute overtime pay.&lt;SEP&gt;overtimeRate is a rate value or argument used in pay calculations, appearing in the initial snippet and in HourlyPayCalculator.calculatePay.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Time And A Half">
  <data key="d0">Time And A Half</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Time And A Half is the overtime pay condition selected by a true overtime argument.&lt;SEP&gt;Time And A Half is an overtime pay rate that pays overtime hours at one and a half times the straight-time rate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="response">
  <data key="d0">response</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">response is a variable name used to hold an HttpServletResponse object.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boolean Selector">
  <data key="d0">Boolean Selector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Boolean Selector is a boolean argument used to select overtime behavior in calculateWeeklyPay.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="straightPay">
  <data key="d0">straightPay</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">straightPay is a proposed method that returns pay for straight-time tenths by multiplying getTenthsWorked by getTenthRate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="overTimePay">
  <data key="d0">overTimePay</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">overTimePay is a proposed method that returns straight pay plus an overtime bonus for worked tenths above 400.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="overTimeBonus">
  <data key="d0">overTimeBonus</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">overTimeBonus is a private method that computes a rounded overtime bonus from overtime tenths and getTenthRate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="m_otCalc">
  <data key="d0">m_otCalc</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">m_otCalc is an obscured version of overTimePay that uses Hungarian notation, a magic number, and a dense expression.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="G16 Obscured Intent">
  <data key="d0">G16 Obscured Intent</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">G16 Obscured Intent is a guideline that says code should be expressive and avoid run-on expressions, Hungarian notation, and magic numbers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Run-On Expressions">
  <data key="d0">Run-On Expressions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Run-On Expressions are dense, impenetrable expressions that obscure the intent of code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PI">
  <data key="d0">PI</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">PI is a mathematical constant discussed as an example of choosing where to place code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Math">
  <data key="d0">Math</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Math is a class referenced as a possible location for PI and as owner of the static method Math.max.&lt;SEP&gt;Math is a Java class used in the isInRange code to compute minimum and maximum ordinal day boundaries.&lt;SEP&gt;Math is the calculation used to estimate lock-up frequency and infer a single unprotected ring-buffer usage.&lt;SEP&gt;Math is a Java utility class invoked through Math.min, Math.max, and Math.abs to compute numeric adjustments in the date methods.&lt;SEP&gt;Math is a Java utility class used by DayDate.isInRange to compute the minimum and maximum ordinal day values.&lt;SEP&gt;Math is a Java utility class referenced by RowColumnPagePrinter through a min call.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Math.max">
  <data key="d0">Math.max</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Math.max is a static method described as good because all data used by Math.max comes from two arguments.&lt;SEP&gt;Math.max is a static method used to calculate the right boundary from two ordinal day values in isInRange.&lt;SEP&gt;Math.max is a static Java utility method invoked to select the larger of two numeric values in date calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;Math.max is a Java standard method that returns the larger of two integer values, and isInRange uses it to determine the end serial value for a date range.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599048</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trigonometry">
  <data key="d0">Trigonometry</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Trigonometry is a possible class mentioned for placing the PI constant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Circle">
  <data key="d0">Circle</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Circle is a possible class mentioned for placing the PI constant.&lt;SEP&gt;A class returned by makeCircle, representing a geometric shape created from center and radius arguments.&lt;SEP&gt;A shape data structure in the procedural example containing a center Point and a radius.&lt;SEP&gt;Circle is a Java class that implements Shape and stores a center Point, a radius, and a PI constant, with an area method that returns PI times radius times radius.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="OVERTIME_RATE">
  <data key="d0">OVERTIME_RATE</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">OVERTIME_RATE is a constant that should be declared in the HourlyPayCalculator class.&lt;SEP&gt;OVERTIME_RATE is a constant double value set to 1.5 in PayrollConstants, representing the overtime multiplier.&lt;SEP&gt;OVERTIME_RATE is a constant in PayrollConstants with the value 1.5, used to calculate overtime pay.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="calculatePay">
  <data key="d0">calculatePay</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">calculatePay is a static method of HourlyPayCalculator that takes Employee and overtimeRate as arguments and is discussed as potentially inappropriate static.&lt;SEP&gt;calculatePay is a method that calculates a Money value for an Employee.&lt;SEP&gt;calculatePay is a payroll method declared abstract in Employee and implemented in HourlyEmployee to compute the amount of pay.&lt;SEP&gt;calculatePay is a method declared by Employee and implemented by HourlyEmployee to compute an amount of pay.&lt;SEP&gt;calculatePay is an abstract method of Employee that calculates pay and can be dispatched polymorphically through Employee.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
