<node id="Smalltalk Best Practice Patterns">
  <data key="d0">Smalltalk Best Practice Patterns</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Smalltalk Best Practice Patterns is a book by Kent Beck cited as a source for the guideline about using explanatory variables.&lt;SEP&gt;Smalltalk Best Practice Patterns is a book by Kent Beck cited in connection with G19 Use Explanatory Variables.&lt;SEP&gt;Smalltalk Best Practice Patterns is a book by Kent Beck published by Prentice Hall in 1997, listed in the bibliography.&lt;SEP&gt;Smalltalk Best Practice Patterns is a 1997 book by Kent Beck, published by Prentice Hall.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Common Closure Principle">
  <data key="d0">Common Closure Principle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Common Closure Principle is a software design principle specifically listed in the input.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PPP">
  <data key="d0">PPP</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">PPP is a referenced source cited for the Single Responsibility Principle, the Open Closed Principle, and the Common Closure Principle.&lt;SEP&gt;PPP is the abbreviation used for the 2002 book Agile Software Development: Principles, Patterns, and Practices.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Overtime">
  <data key="d0">Overtime</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Overtime is the extra time or pay calculation for hours beyond straight time in hourly pay.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Straight Time">
  <data key="d0">Straight Time</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Straight Time is the standard pay rate for regular hours without an overtime multiplier.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Time Card">
  <data key="d0">Time Card</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Time Card is a record of employee time that the TimeCard Module accepts and that can be used to calculate total hours.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Running Total">
  <data key="d0">Running Total</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Running Total is an accumulating total of hours that can be maintained as time cards are accepted.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Total Hours">
  <data key="d0">Total Hours</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Total Hours is the aggregate number of hours an employee worked, used in reports and time-card calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Math.round">
  <data key="d0">Math.round</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Math.round is a static method used in the code snippets to round calculated pay values.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Selector">
  <data key="d0">Selector</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Selector is an argument used to select the behavior of a function, which can be boolean, enum, integer, or another type.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hourly Pay">
  <data key="d0">Hourly Pay</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hourly Pay is the calculated compensation based on hours worked, including possible overtime or straight-time algorithms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Use Explanatory Variables">
  <data key="d0">Use Explanatory Variables</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Use Explanatory Variables is a programming guideline that recommends breaking calculations into intermediate values with meaningful variable names to improve readability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prefer Nonstatic Methods">
  <data key="d0">Prefer Nonstatic Methods</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prefer Nonstatic Methods is a general programming guideline that advises choosing nonstatic methods over static methods unless polymorphic behavior is impossible.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Static Method">
  <data key="d0">Static Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Static Method is a kind of method that should be used only when there is no chance the method will need to behave polymorphically.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nonstatic Method">
  <data key="d0">Nonstatic Method</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Nonstatic Method is a kind of method that is generally recommended when the desired behavior is uncertain or polymorphism may be needed.&lt;SEP&gt;A method category that has at least one local variable, this, representing the current object.&lt;SEP&gt;A nonstatic method has at least one local variable, the This Variable, which represents the current object.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Function Names Should Say What They Do">
  <data key="d0">Function Names Should Say What They Do</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Function Names Should Say What They Do is a programming guideline stating that function names should make a function's behavior clear without requiring its implementation or documentation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Understand The Algorithm">
  <data key="d0">Understand The Algorithm</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Understand The Algorithm is a programming guideline requiring a developer to know how a function works and why it is correct, rather than merely observing that it passes tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Make Logical Dependencies Physical">
  <data key="d0">Make Logical Dependencies Physical</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Make Logical Dependencies Physical is a programming guideline stating that dependencies between modules should be explicit and physical rather than assumed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HourlyReporter">
  <data key="d0">HourlyReporter</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">HourlyReporter is a Java class that gathers report data for hourly employees, builds line items, and uses a formatter to print pages of a report.&lt;SEP&gt;HourlyReporter is a Java class shown in HourlyReporter.java that creates page items and declares the PAGE_SIZE constant, giving it a logical dependency on page size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HourlyReportFormatter">
  <data key="d0">HourlyReportFormatter</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">HourlyReportFormatter is a Java class used by HourlyReporter to format a list of report line items for output.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
