  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Insufficient Tests">
  <data key="d0">Insufficient Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Insufficient Tests is a testing heuristic stating that a test suite remains incomplete while unexplored conditions or unvalidated calculations exist.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Use A Coverage Tool">
  <data key="d0">Use A Coverage Tool</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Use A Coverage Tool is a testing heuristic recommending coverage tools to reveal under-tested modules, classes, and functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Don't Skip Trivial Tests">
  <data key="d0">Don't Skip Trivial Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Don't Skip Trivial Tests is a testing heuristic stating that trivial tests are easy to write and have documentary value that exceeds the cost to produce the tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="An Ignored Test Is A Question About An Ambiguity">
  <data key="d0">An Ignored Test Is A Question About An Ambiguity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An Ignored Test Is A Question About An Ambiguity is a testing heuristic stating that an ignored or commented-out test can express uncertainty about unclear requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Boundary Conditions">
  <data key="d0">Test Boundary Conditions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Test Boundary Conditions is a testing heuristic advising special care in testing algorithm boundaries, where errors are common.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exhaustively Test Near Bugs">
  <data key="d0">Exhaustively Test Near Bugs</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Exhaustively Test Near Bugs is a testing heuristic recommending exhaustive testing of a function after a bug is found because bugs tend to congregate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Patterns Of Failure Are Revealing">
  <data key="d0">Patterns Of Failure Are Revealing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Patterns Of Failure Are Revealing is a testing heuristic stating that patterns in failing test cases can diagnose problems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Coverage Patterns Can Be Revealing">
  <data key="d0">Test Coverage Patterns Can Be Revealing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Test Coverage Patterns Can Be Revealing is a testing heuristic stating that observing which code is or is not executed by passing tests provides clues about failing tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tests Should Be Fast">
  <data key="d0">Tests Should Be Fast</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tests Should Be Fast is a testing heuristic stating that slow tests are likely to be dropped from a test suite when time pressure increases.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-17.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596259</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SerialDate">
  <data key="d0">SerialDate</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">SerialDate is an abstract Java date class in the org.jfree.date package of the JCommon library. It represents dates as a serial value, specifically a day count since December 30, 1899, and functions as a base abstraction for concrete date types, including SpreadsheetDate. SerialDate is described as immutable, implementing Serializable and Comparable, and is associated with MonthConstants, which it is said to implement and from which MonthConstants code was later moved. It has been the target of bug fixes, refactoring, professional review, and test coverage analysis. The name SerialDate has also been criticized for implying a particular implementation and for using “serial” where “ordinal” might be more descriptive.

In the source file SerialDate.Java and Listing B-1, SerialDate defines a broad API for date manipulation and validation. It declares abstract methods for conversion, retrieval, comparison, and description, and provides constants, array constants, a description field, and weekday codes such as SUNDAY, MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY, FRIDAY, and SATURDAY. Its methods support weekday and month-code validation, weekday-string conversion, month-name retrieval, quarter mapping, week-in-month-code handling, leap-year determination and counting, last-day-of-month calculation, date arithmetic, range checking, day-difference calculation, and conversion between serial values and year, month, and day components. Static factory methods, including SerialDate.createInstance, create, adjust, and query date instances from year, month, day, and weekday values; related methods include lastDayOfMonth and addMonths.

SerialDate is also extensively referenced in testing and rule-based date logic. SerialDateTests and SerialDateTest.java test creation, leap-year calculation, date arithmetic, month conversion, serialization, and methods such as SerialDate.createInstance and SerialDate.addMonths. BobsSerialDateTest.java uses SerialDate as the declared type of the newYears variable in testAddDays. The class is also used as a code example illustrating an interesting pattern of test failure. Its constants MONDAY, PRECEDING, NEAREST, and FOLLOWING, along with supported-year constants, are used by RelativeDayOfWeekRule, while its lastDayOfMonth method supports day validation.&lt;SEP&gt;SerialDate is a class in the example code that represents dates and provides date operations such as Add Months.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-0af28655754ee82c5f5f3411cf8f5c39-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-038&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-004</data>
