<node id="MonthConstants">
  <data key="d0">MonthConstants</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">MonthConstants is a Java constants type, described variously as an interface or a class, in the org.jfree.date package. It defines or holds static final month code constants for the calendar months, including JANUARY through DECEMBER and specifically JANUARY, NOVEMBER, OCTOBER, and DECEMBER. These constants are supplied for use in date-related APIs, such as creating SerialDate instances, validating month arguments, and writing assertions in tests.

The type is used by classes such as SerialDate, RegularTimePeriod, and SpreadsheetDate. In SerialDate-related code, it provides month codes for constructing dates, and SpreadsheetDate uses it to verify that a supplied month argument falls within the valid January-to-December range. SerialDateTests.java imports it to use constants such as NOVEMBER, OCTOBER, and JANUARY in test assertions, with DECEMBER also used in a SerialDate test. Its month constants are not equivalent to the month constants in java.util.Calendar.

According to change-history and change-log notes, MonthConstants was created or moved as a separate type out of SerialDate to centralize month constant definitions. Some commentary characterizes this kind of constants class or interface as an older Java idiom and recommends replacing it with an enum for better type safety and maintainability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-021&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-022&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-023&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-024&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604207</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Month">
  <data key="d0">Month</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Month is a Java date-component type, specifically a Java enumeration in the org.jfree.date API, that represents calendar months for use in JFree/JCommon date classes. It provides named constants for the twelve months, including JANUARY, FEBRUARY, MARCH, APRIL, and DECEMBER, and associates each constant with numeric indices 1 through 12, full or long month names, and short month names. The type also exposes a static DateFormatSymbols field and a LAST_DAY_OF_MONTH array, supporting date construction, calendar validation, and month-related calculations. In JCommon-related date processing, it serves as the type used to store the month component of SpreadsheetDate and to create month values from integers through Month.fromInt.

Functionally, Month supplies helper methods and accessors for working with month values. It supports quarter calculation, full-string and short-string representations through methods such as toString and toShortString, parsing from string or integer, case-insensitive matching via methods such as parse and matches, and integer conversion through methods such as fromInt and toInt. It also supports last-day retrieval, with a lastDay method and related calculations referenced in methods such as GetEndOfMonth and LastDayOfMonth. The month value is used by DayDate, DateUtil, DayDateFactory, and SpreadsheetDate to obtain month components, perform month arithmetic, retrieve a month from a base date, construct dates, and compute end-of-month or last-day values.

In the history of the date design, Month has also been described as an abstract calendar month value represented in SerialDate by integer codes and string names, and as a refactored enum replacing integer month codes. In an earlier refactored form it was an enum inside DayDate; it was later moved outside DayDate into its own class for consistency with Day, with associated changes such as the use of fromInt and the addition of a toInt accessor. These variations reflect the evolution and different supporting date classes of the same Month calendar-month concept rather than fundamentally different entities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-029&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-039&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-044</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
