  <data key="d0">Minus One Return Value</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Minus One Return Value is the -1 return value associated with stringToWeekdayCode and specifically described by its Javadoc.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="StringToWeekdayCode Javadoc">
  <data key="d0">StringToWeekdayCode Javadoc</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">StringToWeekdayCode Javadoc is the Javadoc for stringToWeekdayCode that is considered clutter except for describing the -1 return value.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="weekdayCodeToString">
  <data key="d0">weekdayCodeToString</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">weekdayCodeToString is a conversion method that converts a weekday code to a string and is moved into the Day enumeration and called toString.&lt;SEP&gt;weekdayCodeToString is a method that converts a weekday code to a weekday name string and is exercised by testWeekdayCodeToString.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-025</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599059</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getMonths">
  <data key="d0">getMonths</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">getMonths refers to two similar month-name retrieval functions that are collapsed into a single simplified function during refactoring.&lt;SEP&gt;A DateFormatSymbols method that returns full month names and is called by toString.&lt;SEP&gt;getMonths is a method referenced on DATE_FORMAT_SYMBOLS in SerialDate.Java to retrieve long month name values.&lt;SEP&gt;A method of DateFormatSymbols called by DateUtil.getMonthNames to retrieve the month names.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-042</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599013</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getMonthNames">
  <data key="d0">getMonthNames</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">getMonthNames is a simplified static method returning month names after collapsing duplicate getMonths functions and applying a more self-descriptive name.&lt;SEP&gt;getMonthNames is a static method moved from DayDate into DateUtil.&lt;SEP&gt;A static method in DateUtil that returns the month names from the dateFormatSymbols instance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-042</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599080</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="monthCodeToString">
  <data key="d0">monthCodeToString</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">monthCodeToString denotes methods that convert a month code to a string, are moved into the Month enum, and are renamed and simplified into string representation methods.&lt;SEP&gt;monthCodeToString is a static method in SerialDate.Java that returns a string representing a supplied month, using either the long or short month name from the default locale.&lt;SEP&gt;A static SerialDate method invoked in toString with the result of getMonth to produce a month code component of the date string.&lt;SEP&gt;A conversion method that maps a month code to a full month name or a three-letter abbreviation and throws IllegalArgumentException for invalid month code -1.&lt;SEP&gt;monthCodeToString is a function called in the test code that converts a month code to a string representation, as shown by round-trip assertions with stringToMonthCode.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-026&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-027</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599013</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Robert Simmons">
  <data key="d0">Robert Simmons</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Robert Simmons is an author referenced in the text who strongly recommends using final extensively in code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Final Keyword">
  <data key="d0">Final Keyword</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Final Keyword is a Java language keyword that the text describes as adding little value and much clutter in arguments and variable declarations, except for occasional final constants.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DateFormatSymbols">
  <data key="d0">DateFormatSymbols</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">DateFormatSymbols is a Java class used to obtain short and full weekday and month names for the Day and Month enumerations.&lt;SEP&gt;DateFormatSymbols is a Java class used as the type of the SerialDate DATE_FORMAT_SYMBOLS field.&lt;SEP&gt;DateFormatSymbols is a java.text class that supplies month, short month, weekday, and short weekday name arrays used by Month and Day.&lt;SEP&gt;DateFormatSymbols is a Java text class held as a static field in Month and used by Month.toString to retrieve month name strings.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java class from java.text that is instantiated in DateUtil and queried for month names via getMonths.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-040&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-041&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-042</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599006</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="quarter">
  <data key="d0">quarter</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">quarter is a method in the Month enum that returns the quarter calculated from the month index.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="toShortString">
  <data key="d0">toShortString</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">toShortString is a method in the Month enum that returns a short month name using DateFormatSymbols.&lt;SEP&gt;A method that returns the short month name from dateFormatSymbols.getShortMonths() using an index offset.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="parse">
  <data key="d0">parse</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A static Month method that parses a trimmed string into a Month by matching month names or converting an integer, throwing IllegalArgumentException for invalid input.&lt;SEP&gt;Parse is a static method in Day that parses a weekday string into a Day instance and throws IllegalArgumentException when the string is not a valid weekday name.&lt;SEP&gt;A private method in Args that initializes valid by parsing the schema, parsing arguments, and catching ArgsException.&lt;SEP&gt;Private Args method that initializes validity by checking for empty inputs, parsing schema and arguments, and checking unexpectedArguments.&lt;SEP&gt;Private method in Args that checks whether schema and args are both empty, calls parseSchema and parseArguments, and returns the valid field&lt;SEP&gt;parse is a private method in Args that returns true when the schema and argsList are empty, otherwise calls parseSchema and parseArguments and catches ArgsException.&lt;SEP&gt;A private method of Args that initiates argument parsing by invoking parseSchema and parseArguments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598396</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="make">
  <data key="d0">make</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Make is a static method in Day that creates or retrieves a Day instance from an integer index and throws IllegalArgumentException when the index is illegal.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Flag Argument">
  <data key="d0">Flag Argument</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Flag Argument is a code-smell or design practice in which a flag argument is passed to a function to select the format of output, and the text describes it as usually a bad idea.&lt;SEP&gt;Flag Argument is a boolean argument passed to a function, described as complicating the method signature and indicating that the function does more than one thing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DateFactory">
  <data key="d0">DateFactory</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A factory class referenced in an example that creates a DayDate object from a day, Month, and year.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="matches">
  <data key="d0">matches</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A private Month method that compares a string to full or short month names case-insensitively.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="isLeapYear">
  <data key="d0">isLeapYear</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A static method that determines whether a year is a leap year using modulo checks for four, one hundred, and four hundred.&lt;SEP&gt;isLeapYear is a static method moved from DayDate into DateUtil.&lt;SEP&gt;isLeapYear is a static method in SerialDate that determines whether a year in the range 1900 to 9999 is a leap year using divisibility rules for 4, 100, and 400.&lt;SEP&gt;isLeapYear is a function called in testIsLeapYear that determines whether a given year is a leap year, tested with multiple century-boundary and non-century years.&lt;SEP&gt;isLeapYear is a static method of SerialDate that determines whether a given year is a leap year, and it is used in serial number and day/month/year calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;A static method in DateUtil that determines whether a year is a leap year using boolean checks for division by 4, 100, and 400.&lt;SEP&gt;isLeapYear is a DateUtil method invoked with a year value to determine whether that year is a leap year.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-044</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599013</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="leapYearCount">
  <data key="d0">leapYearCount</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A function originally in DayDate around lines 519-536 that is pushed down because only two SpreadsheetDate methods call it.&lt;SEP&gt;leapYearCount is a static method in SerialDate that returns the number of leap years from 1900 to a specified year inclusive, with 1900 treated as not a leap year.&lt;SEP&gt;leapYearCount is a function called in testLeapYearCount that computes a leap-year count for a given year, with visible expected values of 0 for 1900 through 1903 and 1 for 1904 through 1906.&lt;SEP&gt;A method whose return values are asserted in BobsSerialDateTest.java for various years to verify cumulative leap-year counts.&lt;SEP&gt;leapYearCount is a static SerialDate method that calculates the number of leap years up to a given year, used in serial number calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;A static method in DateUtil that calculates a leap-year count using (year - 1896) / 4, (year - 1800) / 100, and (year - 1600) / 400.&lt;SEP&gt;leapYearCount is a DateUtil method invoked with a year value to count leap years relevant to SpreadsheetDate calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-027&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-028&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-042&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-044</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599013</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="toOrdinal">
  <data key="d0">toOrdinal</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A method renamed from toSerial that converts a DayDate to an ordinal value used by addDays.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="addMonths">
  <data key="d0">addMonths</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A DayDate method that becomes an instance method, uses explaining temporary variables, calls lastDayOfMonth and getYear, and constructs a result through DayDateFactory.&lt;SEP&gt;addMonths is a method of SerialDate whose bug was fixed on 27-Aug-2002 and again on 29-May-2003, with the 27-Aug-2002 fix acknowledged to N???levka Petr.&lt;SEP&gt;addMonths is a static method in SerialDate that creates a new date by adding a possibly negative number of months to a base SerialDate value and adjusts the day near month-end boundaries.&lt;SEP&gt;A method tested to return a new date after adding a specified number of months, with month-end clamping behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-028</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599006</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getYear">
  <data key="d0">getYear</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A method renamed from getYYY that returns the year component of a date.&lt;SEP&gt;getYear is a public SpreadsheetDate method that returns the year value held by SpreadsheetDate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-044</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599080</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="plusYears">
  <data key="d0">plusYears</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A DayDate method that returns a new date a given number of years later while clamping the day to the resulting month's last day.&lt;SEP&gt;plusYears is a date arithmetic method that originally contains duplication with plusMonths and is clarified by using correctLastDayOfMonth.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="plusDays">
  <data key="d0">plusDays</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A clarified DayDate method intended to replace addDays and make clear that the original date object is not mutated.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="plusMonths">
  <data key="d0">plusMonths</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A clarified DayDate method intended to replace addMonths and make clear that the original date object is not mutated.&lt;SEP&gt;plusMonths is a date arithmetic method that originally contains duplication with plusYears and is clarified by using correctLastDayOfMonth.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Explaining Temporary Variables">
  <data key="d0">Explaining Temporary Variables</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A refactoring technique applied to addMonths to make the month-arithmetic algorithm more transparent with descriptive intermediate variables.&lt;SEP&gt;A refactoring guideline used to make complicated day-of-week algorithms clearer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Method Naming Ambiguity">
  <data key="d0">Method Naming Ambiguity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The risk that a statement such as date.addDays(7) suggests mutation of the date object even when the method returns a new DayDate instance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-Mutating Date Operation">
  <data key="d0">Non-Mutating Date Operation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The behavior in which date operations such as plusDays, plusMonths, and plusYears leave the original DayDate unchanged and return a new instance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790596802</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="toSerial">
  <data key="d0">toSerial</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A method that was called by the addDays method and renamed to toOrdinal to clarify the production of an ordinal date value.&lt;SEP&gt;toSerial is a SerialDate method used to obtain the serial day-number representation of a base date.&lt;SEP&gt;An abstract SerialDate method that returns the serial number for a date.&lt;SEP&gt;toSerial is a method invoked on a SerialDate instance to obtain the serial number representing the date.&lt;SEP&gt;toSerial is a method that returns the serial number for a date, where 1 January 1900 equals 2, corresponding almost to the numbering systems used in Microsoft Excel for Windows and Lotus 1-2-3.&lt;SEP&gt;toSerial is a SerialDate method that returns the serial number for a SerialDate instance, used by range and conversion methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f738bd6b0c75b6d085747dd812187c7e-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-031&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-16.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599015</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getYYY">
  <data key="d0">getYYY</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
