<node id="Args">
  <data key="d0">Args</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Args is a Java class, defined in Args.java and identified in the com.objectmentor.utilities.getopts package, that functions as a command-line argument parser and validation module. It is constructed with a format or schema string and an array of command-line arguments, then parses those arguments against the schema while maintaining internal state such as the schema, raw argument strings, an argsList, validity, error state or error codes, unexpected arguments, and found or parsed arguments. Internally, Args maintains a map of ArgumentMarshaler instances keyed by argument characters and delegates marshalling and value conversion for boolean, string, integer, and double arguments to those marshalers. It stores typed argument values and exposes accessor methods such as has, cardinality, getDouble, getInt, and isValid for querying parsed values.

During parsing, Args reports schema or argument problems, including unexpected arguments and invalid values, by tracking error state and generating error messages; one description identifies ArgsException as the exception used for parsing problems. Although one description refers to a rough draft of the class, its overall responsibility is consistent: parse and store command-line arguments according to a schema, expose retrieved values, and surface validation errors. A refactoring note also indicates that Args originally contained error-message formatting code that was later moved elsewhere.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598308</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NoSuchElementException">
  <data key="d0">NoSuchElementException</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">NoSuchElementException is a Java exception caught by StringArgumentMarshaler and IntegerArgumentMarshaler when an Iterator has no remaining element during argument marshaling.&lt;SEP&gt;NoSuchElementException is an exception caught when an Iterator has no next element.&lt;SEP&gt;NoSuchElementException is a caught exception indicating that the current argument iterator does not contain a required parameter.&lt;SEP&gt;NoSuchElementException is a Java exception class caught by setIntArg and setStringArg when no next argument is available.&lt;SEP&gt;NoSuchElementException is an exception caught by argument marshalers when the argument iterator has no remaining value.&lt;SEP&gt;NoSuchElementException is a Java exception caught while parsing argument values and converted into an ArgsException with a MISSING_DOUBLE error code in the shown code fragment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-018</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598308</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Iterator">
  <data key="d0">Iterator</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Iterator is a Java standard interface used as the currentArgument type in ArgumentMarshaler set methods to supply successive command-line argument tokens.&lt;SEP&gt;Traversal abstraction proposed for passing to set functions instead of passing both args and currentArgument.&lt;SEP&gt;Iterator is a Java collection interface used to traverse the current argument tokens.&lt;SEP&gt;Iterator is a Java collection interface used as the type of currentArgument in ArgumentMarshaler.set to provide successive argument tokens.&lt;SEP&gt;Iterator is the Java iterator type implemented by IntegerIterator for sequential access to values.&lt;SEP&gt;Iterator is a Java interface described as inherently not thread-safe because it was never designed for use by multiple threads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="parseArgumentCharacter">
  <data key="d0">parseArgumentCharacter</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">parseArgumentCharacter is a method that resolves an argument character to an ArgumentMarshaler, records the argument character in argsFound, invokes the marshaler set method, and propagates ArgsException with the argument identifier set.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598308</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ArgsException">
  <data key="d0">ArgsException</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ArgsException is a Java exception class, typically implemented as a private nested exception class within the Args utility in the com.objectmentor.utilities.args package, that extends Exception and is used to consolidate and report argument-processing failures. It is thrown by Args when command-line argument parsing, validation, argument setting, marshalling, or marshaler conversion fails, including when a format string is invalid or when an argument value is missing, invalid, or cannot be set. Specific cases described include missing or invalid integer arguments, missing string arguments, and failures raised by argument-setting methods such as setIntArg and setStringArg and caught during parse operations.

ArgsException stores diagnostic details about the failed argument, including an error code associated with ErrorCode values or an ErrorCode enumeration, an optional error argument identifier, and an optional error parameter. It provides constructors and accessor methods, including getErrorArgumentId and setErrorArgumentId, along with methods for accessing the stored values. Its errorMessage method generates or formats a user-readable error message, often from canned error messages based on the ErrorCode value.

ArgsException was introduced during refactoring to move exception-related code out of Args and centralize argument errors. In that refactored design, it serves as the sole exception thrown by Args for argument errors, providing a consistent mechanism for reporting invalid, missing, or unprocessable argument conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-019&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-020&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-021</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598319</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ArgumentMarshaler">
  <data key="d0">ArgumentMarshaler</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ArgumentMarshaler is a Java type used by the Args component to represent, marshal, and store parsed command-line argument values of different data types. The descriptions present it as both a class-like and an interface-like abstraction, which likely reflects an evolving design concept: a private inner or base class introduced into Args for marshaling argument values, and alternatively or later a Java interface implemented by concrete argument marshalers.

In its class-based form, ArgumentMarshaler is described as a private inner class of Args that initially stores boolean, string, and integer argument values and later becomes an abstract private class or base class. In that role, it defines common marshaling behavior and declares abstract set and get operations for storing and retrieving argument values. It serves as the value type in Args’s marshalers map and as the parameter type for set functions that convert parsed argument values, and it is referenced during argument setting in Args.

In its interface-based form, ArgumentMarshaler is a Java interface, sometimes described as private, that declares set(Iterator&lt;String&gt;) and get() for argument parse state, with set throwing ArgsException. Concrete implementations use it to convert command-line argument strings into typed values, allowing Args to handle many argument types through a common marshaler abstraction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-020</data>
