  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException">
  <data key="d0">ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException is a standard Java exception type that is caught in argument-processing code, particularly by the setIntArg and setStringArg methods or other set argument methods used by Args. It is raised when the program attempts to access an argument value that is not present in the args array.

Specifically, the exception occurs when the current argument index or currentArgument is out of bounds, meaning the args array has no more elements at that position. In this situation, a required or expected argument parameter—such as an integer or string argument—cannot be read, so the missing value is detected and the ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException is caught to handle the invalid argument access.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-013</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598429</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="setArgument">
  <data key="d0">setArgument</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">setArgument is a private method in the Args class that processes an argument character by retrieving the corresponding ArgumentMarshaler from the marshalers collection and dispatching value assignment for the current argument. It determines whether the character denotes a Boolean, string, or integer argument and invokes the appropriate handling path, such as setBooleanArg, setStringArg, or setIntArg, to assign the argument value.

In its error handling, setArgument responds to an ArgsException by recording failure state, setting the valid flag to false, and rethrowing or propagating the exception together with the argument identifier for context. The method has also been discussed as a place to avoid an explicit type-case by consolidating the behavior into a single call to ArgumentMarshaler.set, which would allow the retrieved marshaler to perform the appropriate assignment directly.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-020</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598434</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="setStringArg">
  <data key="d0">setStringArg</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">setStringArg is a private method in the Args class that processes and stores a string command-line argument. It advances the currentArgument index, reads the next value from the args array, and sets the string value through the associated ArgumentMarshaler, including cases where the marshaler or stored argument state is referenced in stringArgs. In effect, it consumes the next available command-line token and passes it to the marshaler’s set method.

When no further argument is available, setStringArg handles the missing value as a string-argument error. It records or sets an error state, identifies the condition as MISSING_STRING, and can raise an argument error; specifically, it may throw an ArgsException when an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException occurs because the argument array is exhausted. It has also been described as a helper method previously used for string arguments and later replaced by a set method, while its original purpose remained the marshaling and error handling of string arguments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598441</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NumberFormatException">
  <data key="d0">NumberFormatException</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">NumberFormatException is a standard Java exception that arises when a String cannot be parsed into a requested numeric type. In the provided descriptions, it is associated with parsing integer strings and with argument-processing logic in which a supplied parameter cannot be converted to an integer or double.

In the described code, NumberFormatException is caught in several argument-handling locations. The setIntArg method catches it when an integer argument parameter cannot be converted to an integer. The Args class catches it when a command-line parameter cannot be converted to an integer. It is also caught inside IntegerArgumentMarshaler, and more broadly by argument marshalers, when an integer or double parameter cannot be parsed from its string form.

In one shown code fragment, a NumberFormatException caught while parsing argument values is converted into an ArgsException with an INVALID_DOUBLE error code, indicating that the failure involved an argument that could not be interpreted as a double.&lt;SEP&gt;NumberFormatException is a Java exception type caught in stringToMonthCode when a trimmed string cannot be parsed as an integer.&lt;SEP&gt;NumberFormatException is a Java exception class caught in Month.parse when an integer parse of the input string fails.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-009&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-016&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-017&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-018&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-015&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-041</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599013</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="setBooleanArg">
  <data key="d0">setBooleanArg</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">setBooleanArg is a private helper method associated with the Args class that stores or sets a Boolean value for a given argument character in the booleanArgs structure. It is invoked during argument parsing, particularly by setArgument when the supplied argument character is designated as a Boolean argument and the value true should be recorded. In this role, setBooleanArg uses the ArgumentMarshaler stored in booleanArgs for that character to set the Boolean value, effectively invoking the marshaler’s set operation with the string value "true".

The method is described as handling cases where the relevant ArgumentMarshaler is an instance of BooleanArgumentMarshaler. In implementations where parsing may fail, its call is placed inside a try block that catches ArgsException, allowing the Boolean argument to be set to true while suppressing or ignoring the resulting exception. It is also described as accepting an ArgumentMarshaler and an iterator, forwarding the true value to the marshaler, and being prepared to be deployed through an abstract method in ArgumentMarshaller.

Although several descriptions characterize setBooleanArg as an active component of Boolean argument processing, another notes that it was previously used as a helper method for Boolean arguments and later eliminated during refactoring. Therefore, setBooleanArg should be understood as a formerly used private Args method that managed Boolean argument storage through booleanArgs and ArgumentMarshaler calls, with its later removal indicating that its functionality was superseded by a different or refactored argument-handling mechanism.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-011&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-013&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-014&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ff1f4d3804316cc277d01b65a1e72073-chunk-015</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-14.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598451</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
