<node id="Client/Server Example">
  <data key="d0">Client/Server Example</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The Client/Server Example is an appendix example describing a simple client/server application in which a server waits on a socket and a client sends a request.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Client/Server Application">
  <data key="d0">Client/Server Application</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Client/Server Application is a simple application composed of a server and a client that communicate over sockets.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Server Application">
  <data key="d0">Server Application</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Server Application is a simplified Java server that creates a ServerSocket on port 8009, accepts incoming sockets, processes connections, and can be modified to use threading.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Client Application">
  <data key="d0">Client Application</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Client Application is Java client code that connects to a server on localhost, sends a message, receives a reply, and closes the socket.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ServerSocket">
  <data key="d0">ServerSocket</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ServerSocket is a Java class used by the server to listen for incoming client connections.&lt;SEP&gt;Java server socket type used by a closeIgnoringException overload that closes the socket while ignoring IOException.&lt;SEP&gt;ServerSocket is a Java network class imported from java.net and used by Server to create a listening socket with a timeout and accept incoming client sockets.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MessageUtils">
  <data key="d0">MessageUtils</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">MessageUtils is a utility class whose methods send and receive messages over a Socket.&lt;SEP&gt;Java class in the common package that provides static methods for sending and receiving string messages over sockets.&lt;SEP&gt;MessageUtils is a common utility class imported by Server and used to receive and send messages over a Socket.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Performance Test">
  <data key="d0">Performance Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Performance Test is a test method that starts a group of threads and asserts that the tested behavior completes within 10,000 milliseconds.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="I/O">
  <data key="d0">I/O</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">I/O is a class of system activity involving sockets, database connections, and virtual memory swapping that can make a process I/O bound.&lt;SEP&gt;I/O refers to input/output operations, including retrieving pages, in the throughput calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Threading">
  <data key="d0">Threading</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Threading is a technique of creating threads to process work concurrently, used in the server example to improve throughput when work is I/O bound.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Runnable">
  <data key="d0">Runnable</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Runnable is an anonymous Java object that reads a message from a socket, sends a processed reply, and closes the socket.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java interface representing a task that can be executed by a Thread or Executor.&lt;SEP&gt;Runnable is a Java interface whose implementations can be executed by the Executor Framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Runnable is a Java construct used in the test to invoke takeNextId on ClassWithThreadingProblem.&lt;SEP&gt;Java interface implemented by an anonymous client handler that reads a message, sleeps, and sends a reply.&lt;SEP&gt;Runnable is a Java interface implemented by the Server class, as shown in the class declaration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-011</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Process Function">
  <data key="d0">Process Function</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The Process Function is the server method that handles an incoming socket and, in the threaded version, creates a thread to process the client.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Java Virtual Machine">
  <data key="d0">Java Virtual Machine</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Java Virtual Machine is the runtime environment that imposes a limit on the number of threads the server may create.&lt;SEP&gt;A runtime environment that executes bytecode and can interleave execution of threads.&lt;SEP&gt;A runtime environment whose instructions, frames, operand stack, and specification are discussed.&lt;SEP&gt;A software runtime environment whose instructions take parameters that are placed on an operand stack.&lt;SEP&gt;A separate runtime environment associated with a remote EJB interface.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Socket Connection Management">
  <data key="d0">Socket Connection Management</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Socket Connection Management is a responsibility of the server code to manage incoming socket connections.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
