</node>
<node id="I/O Bound">
  <data key="d0">I/O Bound</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">I/O Bound is the condition in which a system's operation is dominated by input/output waiting, such as sockets, database access, or virtual memory swapping, and can benefit from concurrency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Processor Bound">
  <data key="d0">Processor Bound</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Processor Bound is the condition in which a system's operation is dominated by computational work, such as numerical calculations, regular expression processing, or garbage collection, and is limited by available CPU cycles.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Event Polling Loop">
  <data key="d0">Event Polling Loop</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Event Polling Loop is a possible programming approach mentioned as an alternative for improving single-threaded performance, especially if the performance test fails.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
