  <data key="d0">LastIdUsed Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable incremented by the synchronized IncrementValue Method; when the variable has Long Type, every read/write becomes two operations instead of one.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Int Type">
  <data key="d0">Int Type</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A 32-bit integer data type associated with LastId Variable.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Execution Pathway">
  <data key="d0">Execution Pathway</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A possible ordering of thread operations, counted by permutations and affected by synchronization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Permutations">
  <data key="d0">Permutations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A counting method used to calculate possible orderings of repeated thread symbols after removing duplicates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Six Possible Paths">
  <data key="d0">Six Possible Paths</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The six possible ordering strings for two threads and two steps, listed as 1122, 1212, 1221, 2112, 2121, and 2211 and also expressed as A1B1A2B2, A1A2B1B2, A1A2B2B1, A2A1B1B2, A2A1B2B1, and A2B2A1B1.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Three Thread Sequence">
  <data key="d0">Three Thread Sequence</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A partial sequence of ordering strings for three threads, including 112233, 112323, 113223, 113232, 112233, 121233, 121323, 121332, 123132, and 123123.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Invalid String 111111">
  <data key="d0">Invalid String 111111</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">An ordering string that is invalid because it contains six instances of 1 and zero instances of 2 and 3.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Required Instance Rule">
  <data key="d0">Required Instance Rule</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A rule requiring N instances of each T thread symbol in a valid ordering string.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread Count T">
  <data key="d0">Thread Count T</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable T representing the number of threads in the ordering model.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Step Count N">
  <data key="d0">Step Count N</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A variable N representing the number of instances or steps for each thread in the ordering model.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Permutation Formula">
  <data key="d0">Permutation Formula</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A counting formula, (T*N)!/(N!**T), for possible execution paths after removing duplicates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Isomorph">
  <data key="d0">Isomorph</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An alternative ordering string produced by swapping identical thread symbols, treated as a duplicate in the path count.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Duplicate">
  <data key="d0">Duplicate</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A permutation that differs from another only by swapping identical thread symbols and is excluded from the distinct path count.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Swapping">
  <data key="d0">Swapping</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A procedure that exchanges identical thread instances within an ordering string, described as equivalent to the permutations of N.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="One Line Java Code">
  <data key="d0">One Line Java Code</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A simple Java code line stated to equate to eight lines of byte-code in the path-count example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Eight Lines Byte-Code">
  <data key="d0">Eight Lines Byte-Code</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The eight byte-code lines that correspond to one line of Java code in the simple path-count example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="12870 Execution Pathways">
  <data key="d0">12870 Execution Pathways</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The total number of possible execution pathways for one line of Java code equated to eight lines of byte-code with two threads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="2704156 Execution Pathways">
  <data key="d0">2704156 Execution Pathways</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The total number of possible orderings when LastIdUsed Variable has Long Type and each read/write becomes two operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Read/Write Operation">
  <data key="d0">Read/Write Operation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An operation on LastIdUsed Variable that becomes two operations instead of one when the variable has Long Type.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Synchronized">
  <data key="d0">Synchronized</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A synchronization mechanism that, when applied to getNextId, fixes the lost-increment concurrency problem.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java synchronization modifier applied to IncrementValue Method, limiting the possible execution pathways.&lt;SEP&gt;Synchronized is the Java synchronization construct used both in IntegerIterator methods and in the client-based locking example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Numeric Result">
  <data key="d0">Numeric Result</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A value obtained by a thread calling IncrementValue Method, where two threads could obtain the same numeric result before synchronization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Particular Processor">
  <data key="d0">Particular Processor</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A hardware processor on which a 64-bit assignment could be atomic, even though the JVM Specification does not require that behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Return Address">
  <data key="d0">Return Address</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A component included in a Frame for method invocation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Method Parameter">
  <data key="d0">Method Parameter</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A parameter passed into a method and included in the method's Frame.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Method Invocation">
  <data key="d0">Method Invocation</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The act of invoking a method, which in the described context creates a frame and involves an object that received the most recent message in the current thread.&lt;SEP&gt;An occurrence in which a method is invoked, requiring a Frame and enabling call-stack behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Recursive Invocation">
  <data key="d0">Recursive Invocation</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">A form of invocation supported by a call stack in modern languages.&lt;SEP&gt;A method invocation that is enabled by the call stack to allow recursive invocation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="This Variable">
  <data key="d0">This Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A local variable in a nonstatic method that represents the current object that received the most recent message in the current thread and caused the method invocation.&lt;SEP&gt;A local variable named this that represents the current object receiving the most recent message in the current thread.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Current Object">
  <data key="d0">Current Object</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The object that received the most recent message in the current thread and caused the method invocation.&lt;SEP&gt;The current object is the object that received the most recent message in the current thread and caused the method invocation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Java Virtual Machine Instruction">
  <data key="d0">Java Virtual Machine Instruction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An instruction in the Java Virtual Machine mentioned in the truncated Operand Stack definition.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Local Variable Array">
  <data key="d0">Local Variable Array</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">An array in a method-invocation frame where the receiver object is stored as the first variable in every instance method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Last-In First-Out">
  <data key="d0">Last-In First-Out</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A data structure ordering rule in which the most recently added item is processed first, followed by the operand stack.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Example">
  <data key="d0">Example</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A class or object type whose instance serves as the receiver object in the described method invocation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="resetId">
  <data key="d0">resetId</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A method whose byte-code loads the current object, pushes zero, and stores zero into the lastId field.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="lastId">
  <data key="d0">lastId</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">An object field that stores an identifier value and is read or updated by resetId and getNextId.&lt;SEP&gt;lastId is an integer field read by twoThreadsShouldFailEventually to verify the expected identifier increment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constant Value 0">
  <data key="d0">Constant Value 0</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The integer constant zero pushed onto the operand stack by ICONST_0 and stored into lastId by resetId.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constant Value 1">
  <data key="d0">Constant Value 1</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The integer constant one pushed onto the operand stack by ICONST_1 to increment lastId in getNextId.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Increment Operator">
  <data key="d0">Increment Operator</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The plus-plus operator, available as pre- or post-increment, which is commonly but mistakenly assumed to be atomic.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrent Read/Update Issue">
  <data key="d0">Concurrent Read/Update Issue</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A concurrency problem in which interleaved reads and updates by multiple threads can cause a lost increment or unsafe shared-state modification.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Memory Model">
  <data key="d0">Memory Model</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A model of memory behavior that helps determine which accesses to shared objects and values are safe in concurrent code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ALOAD">
  <data key="d0">ALOAD</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A bytecode instruction that loads a local variable, such as the This Variable, onto the operand stack.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ICONST_0">
  <data key="d0">ICONST_0</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A bytecode instruction that pushes the integer constant zero onto the operand stack.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PUTFIELD">
  <data key="d0">PUTFIELD</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A bytecode instruction that stores a stack value into the field value of an object referenced on the operand stack.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DUP">
  <data key="d0">DUP</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A bytecode instruction that copies the top value on the operand stack.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GETFIELD">
  <data key="d0">GETFIELD</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A bytecode instruction that retrieves the value of a field from the object referenced on the operand stack and places that value on the stack.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ICONST_1">
  <data key="d0">ICONST_1</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A bytecode instruction that pushes the integer constant one onto the operand stack.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IADD">
  <data key="d0">IADD</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A bytecode instruction that adds the top two integer values on the operand stack and stores the result back on the stack.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DUP_X1">
  <data key="d0">DUP_X1</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A bytecode instruction that duplicates a value and places it before the This Variable on the operand stack.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IRETURN">
  <data key="d0">IRETURN</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A bytecode instruction that returns the top value on the operand stack.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread Pool">
  <data key="d0">Thread Pool</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A pool of threads used by the Executor framework for sophisticated execution.&lt;SEP&gt;Thread Pool is a concurrent execution model used by the Executor Framework to pool, resize, and recreate threads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ExecutorClientScheduler.java">
  <data key="d0">ExecutorClientScheduler.java</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Java file that demonstrates use of the Executor framework.&lt;SEP&gt;ExecutorClientScheduler.java is a code file cited as a demonstration of the Executor Framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Function Invocation">
  <data key="d0">Function Invocation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Function invocation is a basic call operation that modern languages support through a call stack, mentioned alongside method invocation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Method Scope">
  <data key="d0">Method Scope</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Method scope is the scope of a method in which local variables are defined.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Instance Method">
  <data key="d0">Instance Method</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An instance method always has the This Variable as the first variable placed in the local variable array.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lost Increment">
  <data key="d0">Lost Increment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A lost increment is a concurrency outcome in which an increment performed by one thread is overwritten by another thread, so one increment is lost.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constant Value 42">
  <data key="d0">Constant Value 42</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Constant Value 42 is the assumed starting value held by lastId in the getNextId example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Constant Value 43">
  <data key="d0">Constant Value 43</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Constant Value 43 is the value produced by incrementing 42 and stored or returned in the getNextId example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread Interference">
  <data key="d0">Thread Interference</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Thread interference is the phenomenon of threads stepping on each other during concurrent execution, demonstrated by the byte-code example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hand-Written Thread Pool">
  <data key="d0">Hand-Written Thread Pool</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A hand-written thread pool is a manually created thread pool alternative to using the Executor framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread Interruption">
  <data key="d0">Thread Interruption</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Thread interruption is the event in which a thread executing byte-code instructions is interrupted after some instructions have completed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shared Value">
  <data key="d0">Shared Value</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A shared value is a value accessible by multiple threads and relevant to concurrent read/update safety.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Possible Orderings">
  <data key="d0">Possible Orderings</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Possible Orderings is the quantity 4.38679733629e+24 possible orderings for ten threads executing the three resetId instructions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Future">
  <data key="d0">Future</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Future is a concurrent programming construct supported by the Executor Framework that allows submitted work to be executed and later queried for a result.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Callable">
  <data key="d0">Callable</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Callable is a Java interface resembling Runnable whose implementations can return a result for multithreaded solutions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="processRequest">
  <data key="d0">processRequest</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">processRequest is a sample method that submits a makeExternalCall object, performs local processing, and blocks until the submitted Future completes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="makeExternalCall">
  <data key="d0">makeExternalCall</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">makeExternalCall is a Callable object in the processRequest example that performs an external call and returns a string result.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ObjectWithValue">
  <data key="d0">ObjectWithValue</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ObjectWithValue is a sample class that demonstrates thread-safe integer value updates using both synchronization and a nonblocking AtomicInteger approach.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AtomicInteger">
  <data key="d0">AtomicInteger</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">AtomicInteger is an example resource that allows simultaneous use and can be used to sidestep mutual exclusion.&lt;SEP&gt;AtomicInteger is a Java 5 nonblocking class used in the ObjectWithValue example to increment and read an integer value.&lt;SEP&gt;AtomicInteger is a resource example that allows simultaneous use and can help sidestep mutual exclusion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AtomicBoolean">
  <data key="d0">AtomicBoolean</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">AtomicBoolean is a Java 5 nonblocking class mentioned as one of several examples for nonblocking updates.&lt;SEP&gt;AtomicBoolean is a class used as a shared flag in the concurrent test to record failure status.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AtomicReference">
  <data key="d0">AtomicReference</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">AtomicReference is a Java 5 nonblocking class mentioned as one of several examples for nonblocking updates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compare And Swap">
  <data key="d0">Compare And Swap</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Compare And Swap is an atomic processor operation that verifies a shared variable still has a known value before changing it and retries when the change fails.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Optimistic Locking">
  <data key="d0">Optimistic Locking</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Optimistic Locking is a database concurrency concept that is described as analogous to Compare And Swap.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pessimistic Locking">
  <data key="d0">Pessimistic Locking</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pessimistic Locking is a database concurrency concept that is described as analogous to the synchronized version of a thread-safe update.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread Safety">
  <data key="d0">Thread Safety</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Thread Safety is the property of handling concurrent access correctly and is used to classify classes and operations as safe or unsafe.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SimpleDateFormat">
  <data key="d0">SimpleDateFormat</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">SimpleDateFormat is a class identified as inherently not thread safe.&lt;SEP&gt;SimpleDateFormat is a Java class used by SerialDate to obtain date format symbols.&lt;SEP&gt;SimpleDateFormat is a Java class referenced in a comment as the source of the date and time format matched by a regular expression.&lt;SEP&gt;SimpleDateFormat is a Java class that is described as not thread safe, requiring independent instance creation in the example method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-012&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Java.util Containers">
  <data key="d0">Java.util Containers</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Java.util Containers are collection classes in the java.util package that are identified as inherently not thread safe at the container level.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Servlets">
  <data key="d0">Servlets</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Servlets are classes identified as inherently not thread safe.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HashTable">
  <data key="d0">HashTable</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">HashTable is a collection referenced in the input as requiring locking because multiple users may access it concurrently.&lt;SEP&gt;HashTable is a Java collection class whose individual methods may be thread safe but whose compound operations can be unsafe without additional coordination.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Modern Processor Design">
  <data key="d0">Modern Processor Design</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Modern Processor Design is the processor capability context that supports reliable nonblocking updates and atomic operations such as Compare And Swap.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Java 5 Virtual Machine">
  <data key="d0">Java 5 Virtual Machine</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Java 5 Virtual Machine is the Java 5 virtual machine that takes advantage of modern processor design to support nonblocking updates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="executorService">
  <data key="d0">executorService</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">executorService is a code object in the processRequest example that submits makeExternalCall through Submit and returns a Future.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Submit">
  <data key="d0">Submit</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Submit is a method called on executorService that accepts makeExternalCall and returns a Future.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Get">
  <data key="d0">Get</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Get is a method called on a Future result that blocks until the future completes and returns the result.&lt;SEP&gt;Get is a method prefix and accessor term prescribed by the Javabean Standard; Get is also listed as one retrieval word among equivalent method names.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Call">
  <data key="d0">Call</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Call is the method implemented by makeExternalCall that performs an external request and returns a string result.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PutIfAbsent">
  <data key="d0">PutIfAbsent</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">PutIfAbsent is a concurrent collection operation shown with ConcurrentHashMap and described as an operation available in Java.util.concurrent collections.&lt;SEP&gt;PutIfAbsent is a method called on ConcurrentHashMap that inserts a value only when a key is absent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intrinsic Locks">
  <data key="d0">Intrinsic Locks</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Intrinsic Locks are locks whose performance has improved across versions but are still described as costly, and they are associated with the synchronized keyword.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blocking">
  <data key="d0">Blocking</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Blocking is a concurrency behavior in which execution waits, associated with synchronization and with calling Get on a Future.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nonblocking">
  <data key="d0">Nonblocking</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Nonblocking is an update approach that assumes low contention, detects conflicts efficiently, and retries until an update succeeds.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ContainsKey">
  <data key="d0">ContainsKey</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">ContainsKey is a method called on HashTable in an example of a nonthread-safe compound operation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Put">
  <data key="d0">Put</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Put is a method called on HashTable in an example of a nonthread-safe compound operation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IncrementAndGet">
  <data key="d0">IncrementAndGet</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">IncrementAndGet is a method called on AtomicInteger in the nonblocking rewrite of ObjectWithValue.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Java.util.concurrent">
  <data key="d0">Java.util.concurrent</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Java.util.concurrent is a Java package whose collections provide operations such as PutIfAbsent for concurrent use.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IntegerIterator">
  <data key="d0">IntegerIterator</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">IntegerIterator is a Java class that implements Iterator&lt;Integer&gt; and provides synchronized methods HasNext, Next, and GetNextValue over a nextValue field.&lt;SEP&gt;IntegerIterator is the iterator class referenced as the starting point for changes that remove duplication with server-based locking.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HasNext">
  <data key="d0">HasNext</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">HasNext is a synchronized method of IntegerIterator that returns true while nextValue is less than 100000.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Next">
  <data key="d0">Next</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Next is a synchronized method of IntegerIterator that returns nextValue, increments it, and throws IteratorPastEndException when nextValue equals 100000.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GetNextValue">
  <data key="d0">GetNextValue</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">GetNextValue is a synchronized method of IntegerIterator that returns the current nextValue.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IteratorPastEndException">
  <data key="d0">IteratorPastEndException</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">IteratorPastEndException is an exception thrown by the Next method of IntegerIterator when the iterator has passed its end.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dependencies Between Methods">
  <data key="d0">Dependencies Between Methods</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dependencies Between Methods is a concurrency issue in which a client relies on multiple method calls whose state changes can interact unsafely.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tolerate Failure">
  <data key="d0">Tolerate Failure</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Tolerate Failure is a strategy for accepting a harmless failure, such as catching an exception and cleaning up.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Terminal Time-Sharing System">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Terminal Time-Sharing System</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Multi-Terminal Time-Sharing System is a multi-terminal accounting system that used client-based locking, ran accounting software for Local 705, and connected to terminals by phone lines and modems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Accounting Software">
  <data key="d0">Accounting Software</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Accounting Software is software used by the Multi-Terminal Time-Sharing System to process accounting data and union dues postings for Local 705.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Local 705">
  <data key="d0">Local 705</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Local 705 is a trucker's union local whose accounting data and union dues postings were handled by the described system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Local 705 Headquarters">
  <data key="d0">Local 705 Headquarters</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Local 705 Headquarters is the headquarters location where data entry clerks worked at terminals connected to the Multi-Terminal Time-Sharing System.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Entry Clerks">
  <data key="d0">Data Entry Clerks</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Data Entry Clerks are the people at Local 705 Headquarters who typed union dues postings into terminals.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Terminals">
  <data key="d0">Terminals</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Terminals are the devices used by data entry clerks to enter union dues postings and connected to the Multi-Terminal Time-Sharing System.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dedicated Phone Lines">
  <data key="d0">Dedicated Phone Lines</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Dedicated Phone Lines are communication lines connecting the terminals to the computer running the Multi-Terminal Time-Sharing System.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="600bps Half-Duplex Modems">
  <data key="d0">600bps Half-Duplex Modems</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">600bps Half-Duplex Modems are modems used to connect the terminals to the computer over dedicated phone lines.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Raised-Floor Environment-Controlled Room">
  <data key="d0">Raised-Floor Environment-Controlled Room</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Raised-Floor Environment-Controlled Room is the room containing the computer, located 50 miles north of Local 705 Headquarters.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ring-Buffer Counter">
  <data key="d0">Ring-Buffer Counter</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Ring-Buffer Counter is a counter in the system that got out of sync with its pointer and caused terminal lock-ups.&lt;SEP&gt;Ring-Buffer Counter is a counter value associated with the ring buffer that indicated the buffer was full even when the pointer indicated it was empty.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread 1">
  <data key="d0">Thread 1</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Thread 1 is one of two example threads that shares an IntegerIterator, calls HasNext, and later calls Next after preemption.&lt;SEP&gt;Thread 1 is an example thread that wants both Resource 1 and Resource 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread 2">
  <data key="d0">Thread 2</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Thread 2 is one of two example threads that shares an IntegerIterator, calls HasNext, and calls Next while causing the iteration state to change.&lt;SEP&gt;Thread 2 is an example thread that wants both Resource 2 and Resource 1.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Union Dues Postings">
  <data key="d0">Union Dues Postings</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Union Dues Postings are dues records entered by data entry clerks into terminals for Local 705 accounting.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Terminal Lock-Up">
  <data key="d0">Terminal Lock-Up</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Terminal Lock-Up is an intermittent fault in which terminals connected to the multi-terminal time-sharing system stopped responding.&lt;SEP&gt;Terminal Lock-Up is an incident in which a terminal stops displaying because the associated ring buffer is both empty and full.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shared Resource">
  <data key="d0">Shared Resource</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shared Resource is a software resource used in many places in a codebase that required client-based locking before use.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pointer">
  <data key="d0">Pointer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pointer is a value associated with the ring buffer that indicated the buffer was empty during the lock-up condition.&lt;SEP&gt;Pointer is the component that should remain synchronized with the ring-buffer counter in the multi-terminal time-sharing system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reboot">
  <data key="d0">Reboot</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Reboot is a procedure initially used to clear terminal lock-ups but considered difficult to coordinate because all terminals had to be shut down.&lt;SEP&gt;Reboot is a recovery procedure initially used to clear terminal lock-ups, although coordinating reboots was difficult.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Terminal">
  <data key="d0">Terminal</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Terminal is a device whose display locked up and stopped accepting or showing output when the associated ring buffer became inconsistent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ring Buffer">
  <data key="d0">Ring Buffer</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Ring Buffer is a software buffer that controlled terminal output and could enter an inconsistent state described as both empty and full.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Front Panel Switch">
  <data key="d0">Front Panel Switch</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Front Panel Switch is a physical switch on the computer that could be read and thrown to trigger a trap function for resetting a locked ring buffer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Trap Function">
  <data key="d0">Trap Function</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Trap Function is a program routine that detected a thrown front panel switch and reset a ring buffer that was both empty and full.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scheduler">
  <data key="d0">Scheduler</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Scheduler is a system component enhanced with a function that checked all ring buffers once per minute and reset any inconsistent buffer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Local">
  <data key="d0">Local</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Local is a person who called when a terminal lock-up occurred, allowing the operators to walk to the computer room and flip a switch.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Computer Room">
  <data key="d0">Computer Room</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Computer Room is the location where the computer and its front panel switches were situated and where a switch was flicked to clear a lock-up.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Winter Of 1971">
  <data key="d0">Winter Of 1971</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Winter Of 1971 is the time period in Chicago when the operators learned that client-based locking is problematic.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IntegerIteratorServerLocked">
  <data key="d0">IntegerIteratorServerLocked</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">IntegerIteratorServerLocked is a Java class that implements server-based locking for an iterator by exposing a synchronized method that returns the next value or null.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GetNextOrNull">
  <data key="d0">GetNextOrNull</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">GetNextOrNull is a synchronized method in IntegerIteratorServerLocked that returns the next integer value or null when no value remains.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PageReader">
  <data key="d0">PageReader</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">PageReader is a class that returns the contents of one web page when given a URL, using an HTTP method and an HTTP client.&lt;SEP&gt;PageReader is a component that reads and parses pages for a URL, and PageIterator delegates page retrieval to a PageReader instance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="URL">
  <data key="d0">URL</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">URL is a uniform resource locator that identifies a page to be read by PageReader and supplied by URLIterator.&lt;SEP&gt;URL is a resource identifier iterated by URLIterator and passed to PageReader.getPageFor for page retrieval.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GetMethod">
  <data key="d0">GetMethod</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">GetMethod is an HTTP method object used by PageReader to request the response body of a page.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HttpMethod">
  <data key="d0">HttpMethod</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">HttpMethod is a type or class for HTTP methods used by PageReader, with GetMethod being an instance representing a GET request.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HTTP Client">
  <data key="d0">HTTP Client</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">HTTP Client is the HTTP client used by PageReader to execute a GetMethod and retrieve a page response.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PageIterator">
  <data key="d0">PageIterator</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">PageIterator is a class that provides page contents by delegating to PageReader while iterating over URLs from URLIterator.&lt;SEP&gt;PageIterator is a Java class that holds a PageReader and a URLIterator, provides a synchronized getNextPageOrNull method, and delegates getPageFor calls to PageReader.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="URLIterator">
  <data key="d0">URLIterator</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">URLIterator is an iterator that supplies URLs to PageIterator for sequential page reading.&lt;SEP&gt;URLIterator is an iterator over URL values used by PageIterator, and PageIterator calls hasNext and next on URLIterator to select the next URL.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Summary Report">
  <data key="d0">Summary Report</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Summary Report is a report printed after all pages are read and statistics are accumulated.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Monolithic Assembly Language Code">
  <data key="d0">Monolithic Assembly Language Code</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Monolithic Assembly Language Code is the assembly-language code whose listings were examined to locate a single unprotected use of the ring buffer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Headquarters">
  <data key="d0">Headquarters</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Headquarters is the organization that had to be called to coordinate terminal shutdown and restart.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Screen">
  <data key="d0">Screen</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Screen is the display surface on which terminal output would appear when the ring buffer is not inconsistent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Output">
  <data key="d0">Output</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Output is the data sent to the terminal screen and controlled by the ring buffer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Debugging">
  <data key="d0">Debugging</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Debugging is the multi-week process used to identify the cause of terminal lock-ups.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lock-Up Frequency">
  <data key="d0">Lock-Up Frequency</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Lock-Up Frequency is the calculated rate of terminal lock-ups that matched a single faulty ring-buffer usage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Search Tools">
  <data key="d0">Search Tools</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Search Tools are automated search utilities that were unavailable while examining assembly-language listings.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross References">
  <data key="d0">Cross References</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Cross References are index or link data that were unavailable for locating code usages during debugging.&lt;SEP&gt;Cross References is the document section that lists the remaining cross references for Smells and Heuristics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-044</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Listings">
  <data key="d0">Listings</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Listings are pages of monolithic assembly-language code that had to be examined manually.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Faulty Usage">
  <data key="d0">Faulty Usage</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Faulty Usage is the single unprotected use of the ring buffer that caused terminal lock-ups.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread-Safe">
  <data key="d0">Thread-Safe</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Thread-Safe is the property of an object or collection protected against concurrent thread access.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shared Variables">
  <data key="d0">Shared Variables</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Shared Variables are variables used by multiple threads or clients, and the locking of those variables is hidden in the server.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Locking Policy">
  <data key="d0">Locking Policy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Locking Policy is the rule for locking shared state and is centralized in the server under server-based locking.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread-Safe Server">
  <data key="d0">Thread-Safe Server</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Thread-Safe Server is a server object protected for use by multiple threads.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Nonthread Safe Server">
  <data key="d0">Nonthread Safe Server</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Nonthread Safe Server is a server object without thread-safe protection that can be used in single-threaded deployment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Single-Threaded Deployment">
  <data key="d0">Single-Threaded Deployment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Single-Threaded Deployment is a deployment scenario in which a thread-safe server can be replaced by a nonthread-safe server.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getNextPageOrNull">
  <data key="d0">getNextPageOrNull</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">getNextPageOrNull is a synchronized method of PageIterator that returns the next page or null based on URLIterator.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getPageFor">
  <data key="d0">getPageFor</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">getPageFor is a method used by PageReader and PageIterator to return page contents for a URL.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="hasNext">
  <data key="d0">hasNext</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">hasNext is a method used to test whether an iterator has another element and is mentioned as the behavior replaced by a null check.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="next">
  <data key="d0">next</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">next is a method used to retrieve the next element from an iterator, including the next URL from URLIterator.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Statistics">
  <data key="d0">Statistics</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Statistics are values accumulated by parsing pages before a summary report is printed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Net">
  <data key="d0">Net</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Net is the network used to read page contents from a list of URLs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Phone">
  <data key="d0">Phone</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Phone is the communication device used by Local to report a terminal lock-up.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Weekends">
  <data key="d0">Weekends</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Weekends are the weekend work periods when Local sometimes worked and motivated the scheduler function.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Response Body">
  <data key="d0">Response Body</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Response Body is the HTTP response content returned by a GetMethod and used by PageReader.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="nextValue">
  <data key="d0">nextValue</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">nextValue is the private Integer field in IntegerIteratorServerLocked that tracks the next integer to return.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Single-Threaded Calculation">
  <data key="d0">Single-Threaded Calculation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Single-Threaded Calculation is a page-processing calculation assuming one second for page I/O, 0.5 seconds for parsing, and total execution time of 1.5 seconds multiplied by the number of pages.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Multithreaded Calculation">
  <data key="d0">Multithreaded Calculation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Multithreaded Calculation is a page-processing calculation using three threads to overlap I/O-bound page reading with process-bound parsing, idealized to two pages per second and three times the single-threaded throughput.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure A-1">
  <data key="d0">Figure A-1</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure A-1 shows a snapshot of 13 pages in a single-threaded solution, corresponding to about 19.5 seconds.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure A-2">
  <data key="d0">Figure A-2</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure A-2 shows three concurrent threads in the multithreaded solution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure A-3">
  <data key="d0">Figure A-3</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure A-3 illustrates circular wait with threads T1 and T2 and resources R1 and R2.&lt;SEP&gt;Figure A-3 is a figure referenced before the explanation that all four conditions must hold for Deadlock to be possible.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lock &amp; Wait">
  <data key="d0">Lock &amp; Wait</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Lock &amp; Wait is a deadlock condition in which a thread will not release a resource until the thread has acquired all other required resources and completed work.&lt;SEP&gt;Lock &amp; Wait is one of the four conditions that must hold for Deadlock to be possible.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="No Preemption">
  <data key="d0">No Preemption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">No Preemption is a deadlock condition in which one thread cannot take a resource away from another thread, and the holding thread must release the resource.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Circular Wait">
  <data key="d0">Circular Wait</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Circular Wait is a deadlock condition also called the deadly embrace, in which threads wait in a cycle for resources held by other threads.&lt;SEP&gt;Circular Wait is one of the four conditions that must hold for Deadlock to be possible.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Create Operation">
  <data key="d0">Create Operation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Create Operation is an operation that acquires a master repository connection and a database connection, talks to the master repository, and stores work in the local work-in-process database.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Update Operation">
  <data key="d0">Update Operation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Update Operation is an operation that acquires a database connection and then a master repository connection, reads from the work-in-process database, and sends data to the master repository.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Master Repository">
  <data key="d0">Master Repository</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Master Repository is an external repository accessed through MQ connections by Create Operation and Update Operation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MQ Connections">
  <data key="d0">MQ Connections</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">MQ Connections is a pool of MQ connections to the Master Repository with a size of ten.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Work-In-Process Database">
  <data key="d0">Work-In-Process Database</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Work-In-Process Database is a local database holding work-in-process data used by Create Operation and Update Operation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Synchronized Block">
  <data key="d0">Synchronized Block</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Synchronized Block is a small block in PageIterator that contains the critical section.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="T1">
  <data key="d0">T1</data>
  <data key="d1">thread</data>
  <data key="d2">T1 is a thread in the circular wait example that holds R1 and requires R2.&lt;SEP&gt;T1 is an index entry labeled T1 that is listed with the page references 16-273, 16-274, and 17-334.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-045</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="T2">
  <data key="d0">T2</data>
  <data key="d1">thread</data>
  <data key="d2">T2 is a thread in the circular wait example that holds R2 and requires R1.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="R1">
  <data key="d0">R1</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">R1 is a resource in the circular wait example held by T1 and required by T2.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="R2">
  <data key="d0">R2</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">R2 is a resource in the circular wait example held by T2 and required by T1.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deadly Embrace">
  <data key="d0">Deadly Embrace</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Deadly Embrace is another name for Circular Wait in the deadlock discussion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CPU">
  <data key="d0">CPU</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">CPU is the processor resource used for parsing pages in the throughput calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Record Lock">
  <data key="d0">Record Lock</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Record Lock is a common example of a resource subject to mutual exclusion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="File Open For Write">
  <data key="d0">File Open For Write</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">File Open For Write is a common example of a resource subject to mutual exclusion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Preemption">
  <data key="d0">Preemption</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Preemption is presented as one of the conditions associated with Deadlock, and breaking preemption is described as a way to avoid Deadlock.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Breaking Mutual Exclusion">
  <data key="d0">Breaking Mutual Exclusion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Breaking Mutual Exclusion is a deadlock-avoidance strategy that sidesteps the mutual exclusion condition by using simultaneously usable resources, increasing resource availability, or checking resources before seizing any.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Breaking Lock &amp; Wait">
  <data key="d0">Breaking Lock &amp; Wait</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Breaking Lock &amp; Wait is a deadlock-avoidance strategy that refuses to wait, checks each resource before seizing it, and restarts after releasing resources if a busy resource is encountered.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Breaking Preemption">
  <data key="d0">Breaking Preemption</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Breaking Preemption is a deadlock-avoidance strategy that allows threads to request that other threads release resources, often through a request mechanism.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Breaking Circular Wait">
  <data key="d0">Breaking Circular Wait</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Breaking Circular Wait is a common deadlock-prevention approach that forces threads to allocate resources in a consistent order.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CPU Scheduling Algorithms">
  <data key="d0">CPU Scheduling Algorithms</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">CPU Scheduling Algorithms are scheduling procedures, and simplistic versions make Livelock especially likely.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CPU Utilization">
  <data key="d0">CPU Utilization</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">CPU Utilization is a measure of CPU usage that can become low due to Starvation and high but useless due to Livelock.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Request Mechanism">
  <data key="d0">Request Mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Request Mechanism is a mechanism used to ask a resource owner to release a busy resource during preemption-based deadlock avoidance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Global Ordering Of Resources">
  <data key="d0">Global Ordering Of Resources</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Global Ordering Of Resources is a convention in which all threads allocate resources in an agreed global order to prevent Circular Wait.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resource 1">
  <data key="d0">Resource 1</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Resource 1 is an example resource wanted by Thread 1 and requested by Thread 2.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Resource 2">
  <data key="d0">Resource 2</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Resource 2 is an example resource wanted by Thread 2 and requested by Thread 1.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ClassWithThreadingProblem">
  <data key="d0">ClassWithThreadingProblem</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ClassWithThreadingProblem is a Java class that contains nextId and a takeNextId method with a threading problem.&lt;SEP&gt;A software class with a threading problem, referenced by lastId, used in a test to demonstrate that concurrent access can produce an incorrect ending value.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="takeNextId">
  <data key="d0">takeNextId</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">takeNextId is a method in ClassWithThreadingProblem that returns nextId and increments nextId.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ClassWithThreadingProblemTest">
  <data key="d0">ClassWithThreadingProblemTest</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ClassWithThreadingProblemTest is a JUnit test class that demonstrates the threading problem in ClassWithThreadingProblem.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="twoThreadsShouldFailEventually">
  <data key="d0">twoThreadsShouldFailEventually</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">twoThreadsShouldFailEventually is a test method that starts two threads calling takeNextId and checks whether the identifier was incremented as expected.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="nextId">
  <data key="d0">nextId</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">nextId is an integer field in ClassWithThreadingProblem that is incremented by takeNextId.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Listing A-2">
  <data key="d0">Listing A-2</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Listing A-2 is a code listing that contains the JUnit test class ClassWithThreadingProblemTest.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Thread Balancing Algorithms">
  <data key="d0">Thread Balancing Algorithms</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Thread Balancing Algorithms are algorithms for balancing thread execution, and simplistic hand-written versions are mentioned as a context where Livelock can occur.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Embedded Devices">
  <data key="d0">Embedded Devices</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Embedded Devices are devices mentioned as an example of environments where simplistic CPU scheduling algorithms may be used.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Simultaneous Use">
  <data key="d0">Simultaneous Use</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Simultaneous Use is the ability to use resources concurrently, and it is an approach for breaking Mutual Exclusion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TANSTAAFL">
  <data key="d0">TANSTAAFL</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">TANSTAAFL is a referenced principle meaning that there is no such thing as a free lunch, used to summarize tradeoffs in deadlock avoidance.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Isolating Thread-Related Code">
  <data key="d0">Isolating Thread-Related Code</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Isolating Thread-Related Code is a practice that allows tuning and experimentation to determine the best deadlock-avoidance strategies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrent Update Problem">
  <data key="d0">Concurrent Update Problem</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A defect scenario in which concurrent threads interfere while updating shared state, causing rare incorrect final values.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ClassWithThreadingProblem Test">
  <data key="d0">ClassWithThreadingProblem Test</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A test procedure that creates a single instance of ClassWithThreadingProblem, starts two threads, waits for completion, compares an ending ID with an expected ID, and attempts to expose a threading issue.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Monte Carlo Testing">
  <data key="d0">Monte Carlo Testing</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A testing approach that repeatedly runs flexible tests with randomly varied tuning values to expose rare failures, including threading failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Continuous Integration Server">
  <data key="d0">Continuous Integration Server</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A server that can repeatedly run tests, including flexible threading tests, continuously and early in development.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Anonymous Inner Class">
  <data key="d0">Anonymous Inner Class</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An anonymous class created in the threading test to encapsulate behavior that uses the single instance of ClassWithThreadingProblem; Thread instances execute this class through a runnable reference.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Iteration Count">
  <data key="d0">Iteration Count</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A numeric tuning parameter specifying how many times the test loop executes; increasing the iteration count increases the chance of detecting rare threading failures but also increases execution time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Non-Thread-Safe Code">
  <data key="d0">Non-Thread-Safe Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Code that is not safe for concurrent access; ConTest instruments such code to make failures more likely.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Threading Issues">
  <data key="d0">Threading Issues</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">General concurrency defects in software, including rare timing failures; ConTest improves the ability to find threading issues.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Server">
  <data key="d0">Test Server</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A server suggested for running flexible threading tests repeatedly as part of Monte Carlo Testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tuning Values">
  <data key="d0">Tuning Values</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Numerical parameters, such as iteration counts and load settings, that can be randomly changed during Monte Carlo Testing to expose failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Target Deployment Platforms">
  <data key="d0">Target Deployment Platforms</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The deployment environments on which tests should be run repeatedly and continuously; longer failure-free runs increase confidence.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Varying Loads">
  <data key="d0">Varying Loads</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Workload variations applied to a test machine to simulate production-like conditions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ConTest Development Team">
  <data key="d0">ConTest Development Team</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">The team that developed ConTest, mentioned as a party with which the authors have no direct relationship.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Loop Values After ConTest">
  <data key="d0">Loop Values After ConTest</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The observed loop values 13, 23, 0, 54, 16, 14, 6, 69, 107, 49, and 2 from runs after ConTest instrumentation, indicating earlier and more reliable failures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-009</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clean Synchronization">
  <data key="d0">Clean Synchronization</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Clean Synchronization is a discipline for keeping concurrent code clean and preventing concurrent update.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="I/O-Bound System">
  <data key="d0">I/O-Bound System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An I/O-Bound System is a system whose throughput can be enhanced by using threads, as described in the chapter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Instrumenting Code">
  <data key="d0">Instrumenting Code</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Instrumenting Code is a strategy for exposing concurrent problems by modifying code to reveal failures during test runs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Instrumented Classes">
  <data key="d0">Instrumented Classes</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Instrumented Classes are code classes whose instrumented form failed much earlier and with much greater reliability during test runs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Loop Values After Instrumentation">
  <data key="d0">Loop Values After Instrumentation</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Loop Values After Instrumentation are the values 13, 23, 0, 54, 16, 14, 6, 69, 107, 49, and 2 from several runs of an instrumented test.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Success Rate Change">
  <data key="d0">Success Rate Change</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Success Rate Change is the change from roughly one failure in ten million iterations to roughly one failure in thirty iterations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrent Problems">
  <data key="d0">Concurrent Problems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrent Problems are issues in concurrent code that can be exposed through instrumenting code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Concurrent Programming in Java: Design Principles and Patterns">
  <data key="d0">Concurrent Programming in Java: Design Principles and Patterns</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Concurrent Programming in Java: Design Principles and Patterns is a book by Doug Lea recommended as a starting point for learning more about concurrent systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-010</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790598945</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Server.java">
