  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="testCreateInstanceFromJavaDate">
  <data key="d0">testCreateInstanceFromJavaDate</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">testCreateInstanceFromJavaDate is a JUnit test method in BobsSerialDateTest that verifies createInstance produces expected dates from GregorianCalendar Date objects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Supported Date Range">
  <data key="d0">Supported Date Range</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Supported Date Range is the date range supported by SpreadsheetDate.java, from 1-Jan-1900 to 31-Dec-9999.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-032</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Article Q181370">
  <data key="d0">Article Q181370</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Article Q181370 is a Microsoft support article referenced for additional information about Excel recognizing 1900 as a leap year.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Excel 1900 Leap Year Bug">
  <data key="d0">Excel 1900 Leap Year Bug</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Excel 1900 Leap Year Bug is a deliberate defect in Excel that treats 1900 as a leap year, introducing the non-existent date 29-Feb-1900 and affecting serial day numbers.&lt;SEP&gt;A deliberate error in Microsoft Excel that treats 1900 as a leap year by including a nonexistent 29 February 1900.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-043</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="calcSerial">
  <data key="d0">calcSerial</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">calcSerial is a SpreadsheetDate method invoked to compute and synchronize the serial field from the day, month, and year values.&lt;SEP&gt;calcSerial is a private SerialDate method that calculates a serial number from day, month, and year values, using leap-year counts and aggregate month-day tables.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="calcDayMonthYear">
  <data key="d0">calcDayMonthYear</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">calcDayMonthYear is a SpreadsheetDate method invoked to synchronize the day, month, and year fields from a supplied serial number.&lt;SEP&gt;calcDayMonthYear is a method invoked to synchronize the day, month, and year values with the serial number in the SpreadsheetDate implementation.&lt;SEP&gt;calcDayMonthYear is a private SerialDate method that calculates the day, month, and year fields from a serial number by estimating the year, adjusting for leap days, and using aggregate month-day tables.&lt;SEP&gt;calcDayMonthYear is a private SpreadsheetDate method that derives year, month, and day from the ordinal day using year-hunting, month-hunting, and aggregate-day calculations.&lt;SEP&gt;A SpreadsheetDate method invoked from the serial constructor to derive day, month, and year from the ordinal day.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-033&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-043&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-044</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SpreadsheetDate Day-Month-Year Constructor">
  <data key="d0">SpreadsheetDate Day-Month-Year Constructor</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">SpreadsheetDate Day-Month-Year Constructor creates a SpreadsheetDate instance from day, month, and year values, validates those arguments, calculates the serial value, and initializes the description to null.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SpreadsheetDate Serial Constructor">
  <data key="d0">SpreadsheetDate Serial Constructor</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">SpreadsheetDate Serial Constructor creates a SpreadsheetDate instance from a serial day number, validates that the serial value is within the allowed bounds, and synchronizes the day, month, and year values.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="29-Feb-1900">
  <data key="d0">29-Feb-1900</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">29-Feb-1900 is a non-existent calendar date introduced by Excel's 1900 leap-year convention as an extra day.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Microsoft Support URL Q181370">
  <data key="d0">Microsoft Support URL Q181370</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Microsoft Support URL Q181370 is the support.microsoft.com address referenced in the SpreadsheetDate comment for information about the Excel 1900 leap-year bug.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-033</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="equals">
  <data key="d0">equals</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">equals is a method that returns true only when the compared object is an instance of SerialDate and has the same serial number as the SpreadsheetDate instance.&lt;SEP&gt;equals is a public SpreadsheetDate method that checks whether an Object is an instanceof DayDate and compares the ordinalDay values of both instances.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-044</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="hashCode">
  <data key="d0">hashCode</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">hashCode is a method that returns a hash code equal to the serial number obtained by toSerial.&lt;SEP&gt;hashCode is a public SpreadsheetDate method that returns the getOrdinalDay value as the hash code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-034&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-044</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compare Method">
  <data key="d0">Compare Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Compare Method is a public method that takes another SerialDate and returns the integer difference in days between the current SerialDate serial value and the other SerialDate serial value.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compare-To Method">
  <data key="d0">Compare-To Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Compare-To Method is a public method corresponding to the compareTo operation, accepts an Object, casts the argument to SerialDate, and delegates to Compare Method to return a negative, zero, or positive integer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Is-On Method">
  <data key="d0">Is-On Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Is-On Method is a public method that returns true when the current SerialDate represents the same date as the specified SerialDate by comparing serial values.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Is-Before Method">
  <data key="d0">Is-Before Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Is-Before Method is a public method that returns true when the current SerialDate represents an earlier date than the specified SerialDate by comparing serial values.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Is-On-Or-Before Method">
  <data key="d0">Is-On-Or-Before Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Is-On-Or-Before Method is a public method that returns true when the current SerialDate represents the same date as, or an earlier date than, the specified SerialDate by comparing serial values.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Is-After Method">
  <data key="d0">Is-After Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Is-After Method is a public method that returns true when the current SerialDate represents a later date than the specified SerialDate by comparing serial values.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Is-On-Or-After Method">
  <data key="d0">Is-On-Or-After Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Is-On-Or-After Method is a public method that returns true when the current SerialDate represents the same date as, or a later date than, the specified SerialDate by comparing serial values.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Is-In-Range Method">
  <data key="d0">Is-In-Range Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Is-In-Range Method is a public method that determines whether the current SerialDate lies within an inclusive range bounded by two SerialDate values, regardless of the order of the boundary dates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Include-Aware Is-In-Range Method">
  <data key="d0">Include-Aware Is-In-Range Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Include-Aware Is-In-Range Method is a public method that determines whether the current SerialDate lies within a range bounded by two SerialDate values, using a supplied include code to control endpoint inclusion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="To-Serial Method">
  <data key="d0">To-Serial Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">To-Serial Method is a method invoked on a SerialDate object to obtain the serial value used for date comparison operations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Include-Both Constant">
  <data key="d0">Include-Both Constant</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Include-Both Constant is a constant referenced as SerialDate.INCLUDE_BOTH that indicates both range endpoints are included when testing whether a SerialDate is within a range.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Comparable Interface">
  <data key="d0">Comparable Interface</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Comparable Interface is a Java interface whose required comparison method is implemented by Compare-To Method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Serial Value">
  <data key="d0">Serial Value</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Serial Value is the integer serial representation of a SerialDate used to compute date differences and test date equality, order, and range membership.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Date Difference In Days">
  <data key="d0">Date Difference In Days</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Date Difference In Days is the integer difference between two SerialDate serial values, representing how many days separate the two dates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Endpoint Inclusion Code">
  <data key="d0">Endpoint Inclusion Code</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Endpoint Inclusion Code is a value supplied to a range-testing method that controls whether the start and end boundary dates are included in the tested Date Range.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Date Range">
  <data key="d0">Date Range</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Date Range is an interval defined by two SerialDate boundary values, used to test whether a SerialDate falls inside the specified inclusive or endpoint-controlled range.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-035</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MonthConstants.FEBRUARY">
  <data key="d0">MonthConstants.FEBRUARY</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">MonthConstants.FEBRUARY is a constant representing February, used in calcSerial to determine when a leap-day adjustment is required.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SerialDate.INCLUDE_BOTH">
  <data key="d0">SerialDate.INCLUDE_BOTH</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">SerialDate.INCLUDE_BOTH is an include code indicating that both the start and end boundary dates are included in a SerialDate range check.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SerialDate.INCLUDE_FIRST">
  <data key="d0">SerialDate.INCLUDE_FIRST</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">SerialDate.INCLUDE_FIRST is an include code indicating that only the first or start boundary date is included in a SerialDate range check.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SerialDate.INCLUDE_SECOND">
  <data key="d0">SerialDate.INCLUDE_SECOND</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">SerialDate.INCLUDE_SECOND is an include code indicating that only the second or end boundary date is included in a SerialDate range check.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="1-Jan-1900 = 2">
  <data key="d0">1-Jan-1900 = 2</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">1-Jan-1900 = 2 is the statement that 1 January 1900 corresponds to serial number 2 in the SerialDate serial-number calculation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-036</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599067</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RelativeDayOfWeekRule.java">
  <data key="d0">RelativeDayOfWeekRule.java</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">RelativeDayOfWeekRule.java is a Java source file listed as Listing B-6, associated with the JCommon class library and copyrighted by Object Refinery Limited and Contributors.&lt;SEP&gt;RelativeDayOfWeekRule.java is the Java source code file and listing that contains the RelativeDayOfWeekRule class, documentation, copyright notice, and version control identification.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java source-code listing identified as Listing B-6, continued, that contains the implementation of RelativeDayOfWeekRule.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-036&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-037&lt;SEP&gt;doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-038</data>
