<node id="Narrator">
  <data key="d0">Narrator</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">The Narrator is the unnamed speaker in the text and attended the Agile Conference in Denver in 2005, learned test-driven development in 1999, and wears a green wrist band as a reminder to write clean code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-046</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599080</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Elisabeth Hedrickson">
  <data key="d0">Elisabeth Hedrickson</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Elisabeth Hedrickson is a person associated with handing the Test Obsessed Wrist Band to the Narrator at the Agile Conference.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-046</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599080</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Agile Conference">
  <data key="d0">Agile Conference</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The Agile Conference is an event held in Denver in 2005 where the Narrator and Elisabeth Hedrickson were present.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-046</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599080</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Denver">
  <data key="d0">Denver</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Denver is the location where the Agile Conference took place in 2005.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-046</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599080</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lance Armstrong">
  <data key="d0">Lance Armstrong</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Lance Armstrong is a person known in the text for making a similar green wrist band popular.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-046</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599080</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Obsessed Wrist Band">
  <data key="d0">Test Obsessed Wrist Band</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">The Test Obsessed Wrist Band is a green wrist band labeled Test Obsessed that Elisabeth Hedrickson handed to the Narrator at the Agile Conference.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-046</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599080</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Professional Ethics">
  <data key="d0">Professional Ethics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Professional Ethics is the set of professional values the Narrator associates with writing the best code and wearing the Test Obsessed Wrist Band.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-046</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599089</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Green Wrist Band">
  <data key="d0">Green Wrist Band</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Green Wrist Band is the kind of wrist band that Lance Armstrong made popular and that the Test Obsessed Wrist Band resembles.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-b96a37e68a1f0fd9f9aae3b5629b124f-chunk-046</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-18.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790599089</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Meaningful Names">
  <data key="d0">Meaningful Names</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Meaningful Names is a software engineering topic that concerns the use of clear, intention-revealing identifiers for software elements.&lt;SEP&gt;Meaningful Names are names that Dave Thomas says clean code has as part of its cleanliness and readability.&lt;SEP&gt;Meaningful Names are code names that clearly convey meaning and form part of expressiveness.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Software">
  <data key="d0">Software</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Software is the general digital artifact in which named variables, functions, classes, packages, files, and directories appear.&lt;SEP&gt;Software is a system whose structure can make radical change economically feasible when its concerns are separated effectively.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Package">
  <data key="d0">Package</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A package is a named software grouping that is one of the elements named throughout software.&lt;SEP&gt;A Java grouping that can provide package-scope access when a test in the same package needs to call a function or access a variable.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="War File">
  <data key="d0">War File</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A war file is a named packaged software file mentioned as one of the software artifacts that should be named carefully.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ear File">
  <data key="d0">Ear File</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An ear file is a named packaged software file mentioned as one of the software artifacts that should be named carefully.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intention-Revealing Names">
  <data key="d0">Intention-Revealing Names</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Intention-revealing names are identifiers chosen to make the purpose, behavior, and usage of a software element explicit without requiring explanatory comments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Comment">
  <data key="d0">Comment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A comment is an explanatory note in code that indicates a name may fail to reveal intent when the comment is needed to explain the name.&lt;SEP&gt;A code annotation used in software source code that can convey intent, rationale, or redundant or misleading information.&lt;SEP&gt;A textual note in source code, including Javadocs, position markers, closing brace comments, attributions, and commented-out code.&lt;SEP&gt;Comment is a textual explanation in code whose purpose is to explain code that does not explain itself.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Implicity">
  <data key="d0">Implicity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Implicity is the degree to which code context is not explicit in the code itself, making meaning depend on outside knowledge.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GetThem">
  <data key="d0">GetThem</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">GetThem is a Java function example that returns a list of items selected from TheList but whose purpose is obscured by unclear names.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TheList">
  <data key="d0">TheList</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">TheList is an original Java variable name representing a list whose actual role is unclear until the list is renamed to GameBoard.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GameBoard">
  <data key="d0">GameBoard</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">GameBoard is a renamed Java variable representing the mine sweeper game board as a list of cells.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cell">
  <data key="d0">Cell</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cell is a software representation of one item on the game board, originally an array of ints and later a class with an intention-revealing method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Status Value">
  <data key="d0">Status Value</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A status value is a cell state stored in the zeroth subscript of a cell, where a specific value indicates a flagged state.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Flagged">
  <data key="d0">Flagged</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Flagged is the cell state indicated by the status value 4 in the mine sweeper code example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GetFlaggedCells">
  <data key="d0">GetFlaggedCells</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">GetFlaggedCells is an improved Java function that returns cells whose flagged state is indicated, using more explicit names and methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IsFlagged">
  <data key="d0">IsFlagged</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">IsFlagged is an intention-revealing method on Cell that hides the underlying magic number used to indicate the flagged state.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Avoid Disinformation">
  <data key="d0">Avoid Disinformation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Avoiding disinformation is a naming rule that prohibits false clues or entrenched meanings that can mislead readers about code behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="False Clues">
  <data key="d0">False Clues</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">False clues are misleading hints in code, such as inappropriate type suffixes or ambiguous abbreviations, that obscure the true meaning of code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unix">
  <data key="d0">Unix</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Unix is a family of operating-system platforms referenced as an entrenched meaning that can make names such as hp, aix, and sco disinformative.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hp">
  <data key="d0">Hp</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Hp is a named Unix platform or variant that can be a misleading variable name outside the established Unix meaning.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Aix">
  <data key="d0">Aix</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Aix is a named Unix platform or variant that can be a misleading variable name outside the established Unix meaning.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sco">
  <data key="d0">Sco</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Sco is a named Unix platform or variant that can be a misleading variable name outside the established Unix meaning.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hypotenuse">
  <data key="d0">Hypotenuse</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypotenuse is a mathematical concept mentioned as an example of a domain where the abbreviation hp would be misleading because of the Unix association.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AccountList">
  <data key="d0">AccountList</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">AccountList is a potentially misleading name for a grouping of accounts when the grouping is not actually a List.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AccountGroup">
  <data key="d0">AccountGroup</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">AccountGroup is an alternative name proposed for a grouping of accounts to avoid falsely suggesting a List container.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Accounts">
  <data key="d0">Accounts</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Accounts is a plain alternative name proposed for a grouping of accounts to avoid falsely suggesting a List container.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mine Sweeper Game">
  <data key="d0">Mine Sweeper Game</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mine sweeper game is the assumed application context in which GameBoard and Cell represent the board and the cells on the board.</data>
