<node id="accountGroup">
  <data key="d0">accountGroup</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">accountGroup is a suggested alternative name for a group of accounts that avoids implying a specific List type.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="accounts">
  <data key="d0">accounts</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">accounts is a plain suggested name for a collection of accounts that avoids encoding the container type.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="XYZControllerForEfficientHandlingOfStrings">
  <data key="d0">XYZControllerForEfficientHandlingOfStrings</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">XYZControllerForEfficientHandlingOfStrings is an example class name that is visually and lexically very similar to a storage-focused variant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="XYZControllerForEfficientStorageOfStrings">
  <data key="d0">XYZControllerForEfficientStorageOfStrings</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">XYZControllerForEfficientStorageOfStrings is an example class name that differs only subtly from the handling-focused variant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Automatic Code Completion">
  <data key="d0">Automatic Code Completion</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Automatic Code Completion is an editor or IDE feature that offers possible identifier completions after a developer types part of a name.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Information">
  <data key="d0">Information</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Information is the communicative value produced when similar concepts are spelled or named consistently.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Disinformation">
  <data key="d0">Disinformation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Disinformation is the misleading effect produced by inconsistent, ambiguous, or subtly different names.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lower-Case L">
  <data key="d0">Lower-Case L</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Lower-Case L is a variable-name character that visually resembles the numeric constant one.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Uppercase O">
  <data key="d0">Uppercase O</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Uppercase O is a variable-name character that visually resembles the numeric constant zero.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="One">
  <data key="d0">One</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">One is the numeric constant that can be confused with the lower-case l character in code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Zero">
  <data key="d0">Zero</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Zero is the numeric constant that can be confused with the uppercase O character in code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interpreter">
  <data key="d0">Interpreter</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Interpreter is a software execution tool that can accept code with arbitrary but syntactically valid name differences.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Meaningful Distinctions">
  <data key="d0">Meaningful Distinctions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Meaningful Distinctions is the principle that different names should correspond to different meanings.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Number-Series Naming">
  <data key="d0">Number-Series Naming</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Number-Series Naming is a naming practice using sequences such as a1, a2, and aN that satisfies compilers but conveys little intention.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Intentional Naming">
  <data key="d0">Intentional Naming</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Intentional Naming is the practice of choosing names that reveal author intent and make distinctions meaningful.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="copyChars">
  <data key="d0">copyChars</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">copyChars is a Java method that copies one character array into another.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="source">
  <data key="d0">source</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">source is an argument name proposed for the first parameter of copyChars.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="destination">
  <data key="d0">destination</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">destination is an argument name proposed for the second parameter of copyChars.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Product">
  <data key="d0">Product</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Product is an example class used to illustrate how adding generic suffixes can create names without different meanings.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ProductInfo">
  <data key="d0">ProductInfo</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">ProductInfo is an example class name that adds the noise word Info to Product without creating a clear meaning difference.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ProductData">
  <data key="d0">ProductData</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">ProductData is an example class name that adds the noise word Data to Product without creating a clear meaning difference.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Noise Words">
  <data key="d0">Noise Words</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Noise Words are redundant generic terms such as Info, Data, and Object that make names different without making them mean something different.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prefix Conventions">
  <data key="d0">Prefix Conventions</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Prefix Conventions are naming patterns such as using a for local variables and the for function arguments, acceptable when they make meaningful distinctions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NameString">
  <data key="d0">NameString</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">NameString is an example type name that adds a type-like word to Name without an obvious benefit.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Name">
  <data key="d0">Name</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Name is a simple example type name that may be disinformative if used for an unexpected type such as a floating-point number.&lt;SEP&gt;An argument or value referred to as the thing being written, which writeField identifies specifically as a field.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Customer">
  <data key="d0">Customer</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Customer is a class name presented as a good, readable example containing generationTimestamp, modificationTimestamp, and recordId.&lt;SEP&gt;Customer is an example class representing a customer domain concept.&lt;SEP&gt;Customer is an example noun or noun phrase class name recommended for classes and objects.&lt;SEP&gt;Customer is a Java example object used in the statement customer.setName("mike").</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CustomerObject">
  <data key="d0">CustomerObject</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">CustomerObject is a code name discussed in relation to a customer's payment history and to the distinction between similar names.&lt;SEP&gt;CustomerObject is an example class name that adds the redundant noise word Object to Customer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getActiveAccount">
  <data key="d0">getActiveAccount</data>
