  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BASIC">
  <data key="d0">BASIC</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">BASIC is a programming language whose early versions allowed only a letter plus one digit, contributing to name-length constraints.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Windows C API">
  <data key="d0">Windows C API</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Windows C API is a legacy software context in which values were integer handles, long pointers, void pointers, or various string implementations, and compilers did not check types.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Modern Languages">
  <data key="d0">Modern Languages</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Modern Languages are programming languages with richer type systems and compilers that remember and enforce types, reducing the need for name encodings.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Editing Environments">
  <data key="d0">Editing Environments</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Editing Environments are development tools that can detect type errors in Java and highlight or colorize member variables to make them distinct.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Member Prefixes">
  <data key="d0">Member Prefixes</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Member Prefixes are the practice of prefixing member variables with m_ and are described as no longer needed when classes and functions are small and editors distinguish members.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Part">
  <data key="d0">Part</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Part is a Java class example that originally used the member variable m_dsc for a textual description and later used description without the prefix.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interfaces and Implementations">
  <data key="d0">Interfaces and Implementations</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Interfaces and Implementations are a special case for naming encodings, illustrated by an abstract factory for shapes and the names IShapeFactory and ShapeFactory.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IShapeFactory">
  <data key="d0">IShapeFactory</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">IShapeFactory is a legacy interface name with an I prefix that is considered at best a distraction and at worst too much information.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ShapeFactory">
  <data key="d0">ShapeFactory</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">ShapeFactory is the preferred unadorned name for a shape factory interface, allowing users to know the concept without being told about an interface.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ShapeFactoryImp">
  <data key="d0">ShapeFactoryImp</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">ShapeFactoryImp is a preferable implementation name if the implementation must be encoded rather than the interface.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="CShapeFactory">
  <data key="d0">CShapeFactory</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">CShapeFactory is described as a hideous implementation naming alternative for a shape factory implementation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mental Mapping">
  <data key="d0">Mental Mapping</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mental Mapping is the undesirable practice in which readers mentally translate names into other familiar names, often arising from using neither problem domain terms nor solution domain terms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Single-Letter Variable Names">
  <data key="d0">Single-Letter Variable Names</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Single-Letter Variable Names are names such as i, j, k, or c that are poor choices in most contexts because such names act as placeholders requiring mental mapping.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Loop Counter">
  <data key="d0">Loop Counter</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Loop Counter is a variable whose scope may be very small and may traditionally use single-letter names such as i, j, or k, but not l.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clarity">
  <data key="d0">Clarity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Clarity is the principle that clarity is king and professional programmers write code that others can understand.&lt;SEP&gt;A readability attribute involving clear expression.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Class Names">
  <data key="d0">Class Names</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Class Names are names for classes and objects that should be nouns or noun phrases such as Customer, WikiPage, Account, and AddressParser, while avoiding Manager, Processor, Data, or Info and verbs.&lt;SEP&gt;Class Names are class identifiers discussed in the naming guidance, and Class Names should avoid generic words and verb forms.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Method Names">
  <data key="d0">Method Names</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Method Names are names for methods that should be verbs or verb phrases such as postPayment, deletePage, or save; accessors, mutators, and predicates are also mentioned.&lt;SEP&gt;Method Names is the section topic that sets guidelines for naming methods, including verb or verb phrase names and prefixed accessors, mutators, and predicates.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Compilers">
  <data key="d0">Compilers</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Compilers are software tools that check, remember, and enforce types in programming languages; in the Windows C API era the compiler did not check types, while in modern languages compilers remember and enforce types.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Type Systems">
  <data key="d0">Type Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Type systems are the rules and type definitions of a programming language; modern languages have much richer type systems than older languages.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Strong Typing">
  <data key="d0">Strong Typing</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Strong Typing is the property that objects are strongly typed, as in Java, reducing the need for type encodings in names.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Type Error">
  <data key="d0">Type Error</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Type Error is a type mismatch or invalid type usage that editing environments can detect long before compilation in Java.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603331</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Problem Domain Terms">
  <data key="d0">Problem Domain Terms</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Problem Domain Terms are terms drawn from the problem domain; using them in names helps avoid mental mapping.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Solution Domain Terms">
  <data key="d0">Solution Domain Terms</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Solution Domain Terms are terms drawn from the solution domain; using them in names helps avoid mental mapping.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Professional Programmer">
  <data key="d0">Professional Programmer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Professional Programmer is a programmer role that understands that clarity is king and writes code that others can understand.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Smart Programmer">
  <data key="d0">Smart Programmer</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Smart Programmer is a programmer role that may demonstrate mental juggling abilities and is contrasted with the Professional Programmer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Predicates">
  <data key="d0">Predicates</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Predicates are a category of methods mentioned under Method Names as requiring appropriate naming.&lt;SEP&gt;Predicates are predicate methods that should be named for their value and prefixed with Is according to the Javabean Standard.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="WikiPage">
  <data key="d0">WikiPage</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">WikiPage is an example noun or noun phrase class name recommended for classes and objects.&lt;SEP&gt;Wiki page object obtained from PageData and used to obtain PageCrawler and inherited pages.&lt;SEP&gt;WikiPage is a type representing a wiki page stored in the page field and returned by PageCrawler in the snippet.&lt;SEP&gt;A wiki page object used in page hierarchy and symbolic link tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Account">
  <data key="d0">Account</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Account is an example noun or noun phrase class name recommended for classes and objects.&lt;SEP&gt;Account is a software domain object represented by a collection in Bank and BankImpl.&lt;SEP&gt;Banking domain object stored in a collection inside the Bank entity and associated back to Bank when added.&lt;SEP&gt;Account is a Java class type referenced as the element type in the setAccounts method of EBJ3 Bank EJB.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AddressParser">
  <data key="d0">AddressParser</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">AddressParser is an example noun or noun phrase class name recommended for classes and objects.&lt;SEP&gt;AddressParser is a class name identifier presented as a noun-based software naming example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Manager">
  <data key="d0">Manager</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Manager is an example word to avoid in the name of a class.&lt;SEP&gt;Manager is a generic class name term that is discouraged in some class names and also suggested as a consistent suffix for similar concepts.&lt;SEP&gt;Manager is a type representing a Manager implementation associated with ContainerBase in Tomcat.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="postPayment">
  <data key="d0">postPayment</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">postPayment is an example verb or verb phrase method name.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="deletePage">
  <data key="d0">deletePage</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">deletePage is an example verb or verb phrase method name.&lt;SEP&gt;deletePage is a command function that deletes a page and returns an error code used to test success.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="save">
  <data key="d0">save</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">save is an example verb or verb phrase method name.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PostPayment">
  <data key="d0">PostPayment</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">PostPayment is a method name example recommended as a verb phrase for method naming.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DeletePage">
  <data key="d0">DeletePage</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">DeletePage is a method name example recommended as a verb phrase for method naming.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Save">
  <data key="d0">Save</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Save is a method name example recommended as a verb for method naming.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Is">
  <data key="d0">Is</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Is is a method prefix and predicate term prescribed by the Javabean Standard.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Javabean Standard">
  <data key="d0">Javabean Standard</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Javabean Standard is a naming standard referenced for accessors, mutators, and predicates, with prefixes Get, Set, and Is.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Complex">
  <data key="d0">Complex</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Complex is a class name example used to show a static factory method creation alternative to a constructor call.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FromRealNumber">
  <data key="d0">FromRealNumber</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">FromRealNumber is a static factory method example used to create a Complex value from a real number.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HolyHandGrenade">
  <data key="d0">HolyHandGrenade</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">HolyHandGrenade is a clever function name example that the guidance says is less clear than a direct deletion name.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DeleteItems">
  <data key="d0">DeleteItems</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">DeleteItems is a direct function name alternative proposed for the operation of deleting items.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Whack">
  <data key="d0">Whack</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Whack is a colloquial method name example discouraged because Whack means Kill.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Kill">
  <data key="d0">Kill</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Kill is a direct method name proposed as the clearer meaning for the colloquial name Whack.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EatMyShorts">
  <data key="d0">EatMyShorts</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">EatMyShorts is a culture-dependent joke method name example discouraged because EatMyShorts means Abort.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abort">
  <data key="d0">Abort</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Abort is a direct method name proposed as the clearer meaning for the joke name EatMyShorts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fetch">
  <data key="d0">Fetch</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Fetch is a retrieval method name listed as potentially equivalent to Retrieve and Get.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Retrieve">
  <data key="d0">Retrieve</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Retrieve is a retrieval method name listed as potentially equivalent to Fetch and Get.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="One Word Per Concept">
  <data key="d0">One Word Per Concept</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">One Word Per Concept is a naming principle requiring a single consistent word for a single abstract concept across classes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603332</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
