</node>
<node id="Oregon">
  <data key="d0">Oregon</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Oregon is the location of Kent Beck's home, where a 1999 programming session with Kent Beck took place.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Listing 3-3">
  <data key="d0">Listing 3-3</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Listing 3-3 is a code listing described as difficult to meaningfully shrink because its statements do not span multiple abstraction levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Listing 3-3 is a re-refactored code listing for HtmlUtil.java that contains a short function for including setups and teardowns into test pages.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PageData">
  <data key="d0">PageData</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">PageData is a data type used as a parameter in renderPageWithSetupsAndTeardowns, isTestPage, includeSetupAndTeardownPages, and getHtml.&lt;SEP&gt;Data object used by SetupTeardownIncluder to access WikiPage, attributes, HTML, content, and updated content.&lt;SEP&gt;PageData is a type representing page data obtained from WikiPage and stored in pageData.&lt;SEP&gt;Data associated with a WikiPage, including properties.&lt;SEP&gt;PageData is a data object returned by PageOne.getData and committed back to PageOne through commit.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="isTestPage">
  <data key="d0">isTestPage</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">isTestPage is a method called with PageData to determine whether a page is a test page.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="includeSetupAndTeardownPages">
  <data key="d0">includeSetupAndTeardownPages</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">includeSetupAndTeardownPages is a method called with PageData and a boolean isSuite to include setups and teardowns when the page is a test page.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getHtml">
  <data key="d0">getHtml</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">getHtml is a method called on PageData to retrieve or render the page HTML.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Small Function Rule">
  <data key="d0">Small Function Rule</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Small Function Rule is the principle that functions should be small, and even smaller, based on decades of programming experience rather than cited research.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Screen Full Rule">
  <data key="d0">Screen Full Rule</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Screen Full Rule is an older guideline stating that a function should be no bigger than a screen-full, originally discussed in the context of VT100 screens.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Do One Thing Principle">
  <data key="d0">Do One Thing Principle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Do One Thing Principle is the advice that functions should perform one task, perform that task well, and perform only that task; the steps of a function should be one level of abstraction below the stated name of the function.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blocks And Indenting">
  <data key="d0">Blocks And Indenting</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Blocks And Indenting is the guideline that blocks within if, else, while, and similar statements should be one line long, often a function call, and that a function's indent level should not exceed one or two levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="1999 Programming Session">
  <data key="d0">1999 Programming Session</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">The 1999 Programming Session was a programming visit to Kent Beck's home in Oregon, during which Kent Beck showed the Sparkle program.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TO4">
  <data key="d0">TO4</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">TO4 is a brief paragraph structure mentioned as a way to describe a function by stating its task and the steps at the next level of abstraction.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="One Level Of Abstraction">
  <data key="d0">One Level Of Abstraction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">One Level Of Abstraction is the guideline that a function is doing one thing when the function's steps are one level below the stated name of the function.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Setups And Teardowns">
  <data key="d0">Setups And Teardowns</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Setups And Teardowns are the setup and teardown pages or code included into a test page by includeSetupAndTeardownPages.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fairy Godmother">
  <data key="d0">Fairy Godmother</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Fairy Godmother is the fictional character in Cinderella whose magic wand effect is used as a comparison for the Sparkle visual effect.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Magic Wand">
  <data key="d0">Magic Wand</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Magic Wand is the wand associated with the Fairy Godmother in Cinderella, whose sparkle effect Sparkle resembles.</data>
