<node id="Polymorphic Objects">
  <data key="d0">Polymorphic Objects</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Polymorphic Objects are objects created by a tolerated switch statement that appears once and is hidden behind an inheritance relationship.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Input Argument">
  <data key="d0">Input Argument</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Input Argument is an argument that carries information into a function and is presented as easier to understand than an output argument.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Output Argument">
  <data key="d0">Output Argument</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Output Argument is an argument that carries information out of a function and is described as harder to understand than input arguments and return values.&lt;SEP&gt;Output Argument is a function argument that receives changed state rather than serving purely as input, as demonstrated by appendFooter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Return Value">
  <data key="d0">Return Value</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Return Value is the expected channel through which a function returns information to the caller.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Monadic Function">
  <data key="d0">Monadic Function</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Monadic Function is a function with a single argument, presented as easier to understand than a function with two arguments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dyadic Function">
  <data key="d0">Dyadic Function</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Dyadic Function is a function with two arguments, described as harder to understand than a monadic function but sometimes appropriate when arguments form ordered components.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Common Monadic Forms">
  <data key="d0">Common Monadic Forms</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Common Monadic Forms are the recurring single-argument function patterns of asking a question about an argument, transforming it, or treating the call as an event.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Question Form">
  <data key="d0">Question Form</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Question Form is a monadic function pattern in which a function asks a question about its single argument, such as checking whether a file exists.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transformation">
  <data key="d0">Transformation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Transformation is a monadic function pattern in which a function operates on its input argument, changes it into something else, and should return the result.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Event Form">
  <data key="d0">Event Form</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Event Form is a less common monadic function pattern in which an input argument has no output argument and the call is interpreted as an event that alters system state.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Command Query Separation">
  <data key="d0">Command Query Separation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Command Query Separation is a referenced design principle related to choosing clear names and consistent contexts for the different monadic function forms.&lt;SEP&gt;Command Query Separation is a principle that a function should either change state or answer a question, but not both.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SetupTeardownIncluder">
  <data key="d0">SetupTeardownIncluder</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">SetupTeardownIncluder is a named software component referenced as the owner of the render method and associated with page rendering in the example.&lt;SEP&gt;Java class in package fitnesse.html that renders PageData and inserts setup and teardown page includes when the page is a test page.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-70106d3c6c60a2a04b530f45336de970-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-03.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790603783</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
