<node id="P. J. Plaugher">
  <data key="d0">P. J. Plaugher</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Person attributed, together with Brian W. Kernighan, to the quoted principle about not commenting bad code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Don't Comment Bad Code-Rewrite It">
  <data key="d0">Don't Comment Bad Code-Rewrite It</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Quoted principle advising rewriting bad code instead of commenting it; the text cites KP78, page 144, for the quote.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="KP78">
  <data key="d0">KP78</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Cited reference associated with the quote from Brian W. Kernighan and P. J. Plaugher; the citation points to page 144.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Schindlers List">
  <data key="d0">Schindlers List</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Work referenced in the text to contrast comments with something not pure good; comments are explicitly not like Schindler’s List.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Programming Languages">
  <data key="d0">Programming Languages</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Abstract systems used to express program intent in code; the text suggests that more expressive programming languages would reduce the need for comments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Object Mentor, Inc">
  <data key="d0">Object Mentor, Inc</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Organization identified as the copyright holder in the example comment header for FitNesse source files.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="HTTP_DATE_REGEXP">
  <data key="d0">HTTP_DATE_REGEXP</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Java constant holding a regular expression for an HTTP date format; the text uses the constant to illustrate a comment becoming separated from the constant.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Legal Comments">
  <data key="d0">Legal Comments</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Category of comments written for legal reasons, such as copyright and authorship statements at the start of source files.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Copyright">
  <data key="d0">Copyright</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Legal statement of rights in the example comment header, identified as belonging to Object Mentor, Inc.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Authorship Statements">
  <data key="d0">Authorship Statements</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Statements identifying authors in source-file comment headers; the text lists authorship statements alongside copyright as legal comments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clear and Expressive Code">
  <data key="d0">Clear and Expressive Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Code style that conveys intent directly and is preferred over cluttered code with many comments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Explain Yourself in Code">
  <data key="d0">Explain Yourself in Code</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Principle advising programmers to express intent through code, often by creating functions that state the same thing as a comment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inaccurate Comments">
  <data key="d0">Inaccurate Comments</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Comments that are wrong or misleading due to age or separation from code; the text says inaccurate comments are worse than no comments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Necessary Evil">
  <data key="d0">Necessary Evil</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An abstract characterization applied to comments, which the text describes as at best a necessary evil rather than pure good.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Corporate Coding Standards">
  <data key="d0">Corporate Coding Standards</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Organizational rules that sometimes force programmers to write certain comments for legal reasons, such as copyright and authorship statements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Standard Comment Header">
  <data key="d0">Standard Comment Header</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A comment header placed at the beginning of every FitNesse source file; the header contains a copyright statement and is collapsed by an IDE to reduce clutter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Good Comments">
  <data key="d0">Good Comments</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A category of comments considered necessary or beneficial, including legal comments, though the best comment is the one not written.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Misinformation">
  <data key="d0">Misinformation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">False or inaccurate information that old comments can propagate, making such comments damaging.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Truth">
  <data key="d0">Truth</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Accurate information about what code does; the text states truth can only be found in the code.&lt;SEP&gt;Truth is the honest state of code and schedule that managers want and programmers must not misrepresent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IsEligibleForFullBenefits">
  <data key="d0">IsEligibleForFullBenefits</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A method in a Java code example that states whether an employee is eligible for full benefits.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Object Mentor, Inc.">
  <data key="d0">Object Mentor, Inc.</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Object Mentor, Inc. is a company identified in a copyright statement as the copyright holder for FitNesse source files.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GNU General Public License Version 2">
  <data key="d0">GNU General Public License Version 2</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">GNU General Public License Version 2 is a standard license referenced in the FitNesse comment header as the terms under which the code is released.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Comment Header">
  <data key="d0">Comment Header</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Comment Header is a standard comment placed at the beginning of every FitNesse source file, containing a copyright and license reference.&lt;SEP&gt;Comment Header is a header comment for a function that is usually less desirable than a well-chosen name for a small function.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-007</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Copyright Statement">
  <data key="d0">Copyright Statement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Copyright Statement is a legal notice included in a source-file comment to record copyright ownership.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Authorship Statement">
  <data key="d0">Authorship Statement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Authorship Statement is a statement of authorship considered necessary and reasonable for legal comments in source files.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Informative Comments">
  <data key="d0">Informative Comments</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Informative Comments is a comment style that provides basic information about code, such as the return value of an abstract method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Responder">
  <data key="d0">Responder</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Responder is a type returned by the abstract method responderInstance in an informative-comment example.&lt;SEP&gt;Responder is a responder type instantiated as SerializedPageResponder to create responses from a context and a request.&lt;SEP&gt;A software type assigned to the responder variable when SerializedPageResponder is instantiated.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pattern">
  <data key="d0">Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Pattern is a Java class used in an example to compile a regular expression that matches a time and date format.&lt;SEP&gt;Pattern is a Java class from java.util.regex used in BoldWidget to compile a regular expression for bold markers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Time And Date Format">
  <data key="d0">Time And Date Format</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Time And Date Format is the format string kk:mm:ss EEE, MMM dd, yyyy described in a comment as the intended target of a regular expression.</data>
