  <data key="d0">Uncross Integers Up To</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A static method that creates a crossedOut boolean array sized maxValue + 1 and sets indices 2 through crossedOut.length - 1 to false.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross Out Multiples">
  <data key="d0">Cross Out Multiples</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A static method that obtains determineIterationLimit, then iterates i from 2 through limit and calls crossOutMultiplesOf(i) when notCrossed(i) is true.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Determine Iteration Limit">
  <data key="d0">Determine Iteration Limit</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A static method that computes a double iterationLimit as Math.sqrt(crossedOut.length) and returns the integer value, with a comment stating every multiple has a prime factor not larger than the root of the array size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross Out Multiples Of">
  <data key="d0">Cross Out Multiples Of</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A static method that marks crossedOut[multiple] true for multiple values beginning at 2*i and increasing by i until multiple reaches crossedOut.length.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Not Crossed">
  <data key="d0">Not Crossed</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A static method that returns whether crossedOut[i] equals false.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Put Uncrossed Integers Into Result">
  <data key="d0">Put Uncrossed Integers Into Result</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A static method that assigns result a new int array of length numberOfUncrossedIntegers, then stores uncrossed integer values starting at 2 into result.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Number Of Uncrossed Integers">
  <data key="d0">Number Of Uncrossed Integers</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A static method that counts integer values i from 2 through crossedOut.length - 1 for which notCrossed(i) is true.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Crossed Out">
  <data key="d0">Crossed Out</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A boolean array named crossedOut that stores crossed-out status for integer indices, with length used in loop bounds and square-root limit calculation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Result">
  <data key="d0">Result</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">An integer array named result that holds the uncrossed integer values produced by the algorithm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Iteration Limit">
  <data key="d0">Iteration Limit</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">An integer value named limit or iterationLimit derived from the square root of crossedOut.length and used as the upper bound for crossing-out iteration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Square Root">
  <data key="d0">Square Root</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mathematical operation used by Determine Iteration Limit via Math.sqrt, and discussed as a possible time-saving conceit for limiting iteration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="First Comment">
  <data key="d0">First Comment</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A code comment described as redundant because the comment resembles the generatePrimes function, but also described as easing the reader into the algorithm.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Second Argument">
  <data key="d0">Second Argument</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A rationale described as almost certainly necessary for explaining why the square root is used as the loop limit.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="The Elements of Programming Style">
  <data key="d0">The Elements of Programming Style</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A book cited in the bibliography, identified as 2d. ed., published by McGraw-Hill in 1978.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="1978">
  <data key="d0">1978</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A publication year cited in the bibliography entry for The Elements of Programming Style.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Prime Factor">
  <data key="d0">Prime Factor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Prime Factor is a prime divisor of a multiple in the crossedOut array, stated in the Determine Iteration Limit comment as always less than or equal to the square root of the array size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Array Size">
  <data key="d0">Array Size</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Array Size is the length of the crossedOut array, appearing as crossedOut.length in loop bounds and as the operand of Math.sqrt in Determine Iteration Limit.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604159</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Math.sqrt">
  <data key="d0">Math.sqrt</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Math.sqrt is the Java library method called in Determine Iteration Limit to compute the square root of crossedOut.length before the value is converted to an integer.&lt;SEP&gt;Math.sqrt is a mathematical library method used by Quadratic.root1 to compute the square root of a determinant.&lt;SEP&gt;Math.sqrt is a Java standard library method used to compute the square root of the determinant in the Quadratic root calculations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ecee1e439eb5fb52d346911e1683e490-chunk-008&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-04.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Code Formatting">
  <data key="d0">Code Formatting</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Code formatting is the practice of applying consistent visual rules to source code so that code communicates structure, orderliness, and attention to detail.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Professional Developer">
  <data key="d0">Professional Developer</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">A professional developer is a software developer who treats communication, code readability, and consistent formatting as core responsibilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Communication">
  <data key="d0">Communication</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Communication is identified in the input as the professional developer's first order of business and as the purpose of code formatting.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Extensibility">
  <data key="d0">Extensibility</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Extensibility is the quality that allows code to be expanded or adapted, and extensibility is influenced by coding style and readability.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Formatting Rules">
  <data key="d0">Formatting Rules</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Formatting rules are simple, agreed-upon conventions that govern how code is visually arranged, and formatting rules should be applied consistently.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Automated Formatting Tool">
  <data key="d0">Automated Formatting Tool</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An automated formatting tool is a software tool that can apply formatting rules to code on behalf of developers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Formatting">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Formatting</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Vertical formatting is the aspect of code formatting concerned with the top-to-bottom arrangement, size, and organization of source files.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vertical Openness Between Concepts">
  <data key="d0">Vertical Openness Between Concepts</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Vertical Openness Between Concepts is a code-formatting principle that uses blank lines to separate lines representing expressions, clauses, and complete thoughts so that distinct concepts are visually separated.&lt;SEP&gt;Vertical openness between concepts is the use of visual separation, such as blank lines, to divide complete thoughts or groups of lines in code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Blank Lines">
  <data key="d0">Blank Lines</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Blank lines are empty lines used as a simple formatting technique to separate the package declaration, imports, functions, and conceptual groups in code.&lt;SEP&gt;Blank Lines are empty lines used to separate groups of code lines that represent distinct complete thoughts, acting as visual cues in source code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Newspaper Metaphor">
  <data key="d0">Newspaper Metaphor</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The newspaper metaphor compares a source file to a well-written newspaper article, with high-level concepts at the top and increasing detail below.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Newspaper Article">
  <data key="d0">Newspaper Article</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">A newspaper article is a written work whose vertical structure, from headline through synopsis to details, is used as an analogy for source file organization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="File Size">
  <data key="d0">File Size</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">File size is a quantitative measure of the length of a source file, and the input discusses ranges and distributions of file sizes across projects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 5-1">
  <data key="d0">Figure 5-1</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 5-1 is a figure titled "File length distributions LOG scale (box height = sigma)" that depicts minimum, maximum, box, and mean file lengths for several projects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="File Length Distributions">
  <data key="d0">File Length Distributions</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">File length distributions are the statistical patterns of source file lengths across seven Java projects, shown with minimums, maximums, boxes, and means.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604435</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Time and Money">
  <data key="d0">Time and Money</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Time and Money is one of seven projects depicted in the file length distribution figure and is grouped with JUnit and FitNesse as using relatively small files.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JDepend">
  <data key="d0">JDepend</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">JDepend is one of seven projects depicted in the file length distribution figure, though the input does not specify whether the files are small or large.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Average File Size In FitNesse">
  <data key="d0">Average File Size In FitNesse</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">The average file size in FitNesse is about 65 lines, with about one-third of the files between 40 and 100+ lines, a largest file of about 400 lines, and a smallest file of 6 lines.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Log Scale">
  <data key="d0">Log Scale</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A log scale is a non-linear scale in which small vertical differences in Figure 5-1 imply very large differences in absolute file size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Standard Deviation">
  <data key="d0">Standard Deviation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Standard deviation is used loosely in Figure 5-1 to size the boxes, showing approximately one-third of files around the mean despite a non-normal distribution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mean">
  <data key="d0">Mean</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The mean is the middle value in each box of Figure 5-1 and represents the average file size for a project.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter 5: Formatting">
  <data key="d0">Chapter 5: Formatting</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Chapter 5: Formatting is a chapter that discusses code formatting, vertical formatting, file size, and the newspaper metaphor for source file organization.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Listing 5-1">
  <data key="d0">Listing 5-1</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Listing 5-1 is a Java code example for BoldWidget.java that uses blank lines to separate the package declaration, imports, and functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Listing 5-1 is a code listing used as an example of blank lines separating a package declaration, import statements, and functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Package Declaration">
  <data key="d0">Package Declaration</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">A package declaration is a source-file code element that declares a package, and it is separated by blank lines in Listing 5-1.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Import Statement">
  <data key="d0">Import Statement</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">An import statement is a source-file code element that imports code dependencies, and import statements are separated by blank lines in Listing 5-1.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Visual Layout">
  <data key="d0">Visual Layout</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Visual layout is the visual arrangement of code, and blank lines are described as having a profound effect on it.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Complete Thought">
  <data key="d0">Complete Thought</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A complete thought is represented by a group of lines, and such thoughts should be separated from each other with blank lines.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Small File">
  <data key="d0">Small File</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A small file is a source file with a relatively small size, and small files are described as usually easier to understand than large files.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Large File">
  <data key="d0">Large File</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A large file is a source file with a relatively large size, and large files are compared with small files as harder to understand.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Class Size">
  <data key="d0">Class Size</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Class size is a quantitative measure of the size of a class, and in Java file size is closely related to class size.&lt;SEP&gt;A software design concern regarding the appropriate responsibilities and methods for a class. Class name, concise description, and the Single Responsibility Principle help determine class size.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-001</data>
