<node id="120-Character Limit">
  <data key="d0">120-Character Limit</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">120-Character Limit is a personal line-width limit stated in the text, with lines beyond it considered probably careless.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal White Space">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal White Space</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Horizontal White Space is blank space in source code used to emphasize relationships, separate elements, and accentuate operator precedence.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assignment Operators">
  <data key="d0">Assignment Operators</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Assignment Operators are assignment symbols in measureLine that are surrounded by white space to emphasize the left and right elements of assignments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Operator Precedence">
  <data key="d0">Operator Precedence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Operator Precedence is the relative priority of operators in expressions and is accentuated by white space in the Quadratic example.&lt;SEP&gt;Operator Precedence is the rule that determines how operators bind in expressions, and spacing can be used to make it visually apparent.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Function Call Dependencies">
  <data key="d0">Function Call Dependencies</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Function Call Dependencies are dependencies in which one function calls another and are directed downward in Vertical Ordering.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Direct Dependence">
  <data key="d0">Direct Dependence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Direct Dependence is a cause of Conceptual Affinity in which one function calls another or uses a variable.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Similar Operation">
  <data key="d0">Similar Operation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Similar Operation is a cause of Conceptual Affinity in which a group of functions performs variations of the same basic task.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="White Space">
  <data key="d0">White Space</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">White Space is a coding convention used to separate arguments and accentuate the precedence of operators in expressions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Code Reformatting Tools">
  <data key="d0">Code Reformatting Tools</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Code Reformatting Tools are software programs that reformat source code and often impose uniform spacing without regard to operator precedence or alignment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Horizontal Alignment">
  <data key="d0">Horizontal Alignment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Horizontal Alignment is the practice of lining up variable names in declarations or rvalues in assignment statements, which is argued to misdirect attention.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Indentation">
  <data key="d0">Indentation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Indentation is a visual convention that shows the hierarchical scopes of a source file, making classes, methods, and blocks easier to scan.&lt;SEP&gt;Indentation is a formatting rule that makes code structure visible by arranging variables, constructors, accessors, and methods in clearly nested scopes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scope Hierarchy">
  <data key="d0">Scope Hierarchy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Scope Hierarchy is the nested structure of a source file, from file-level declarations through classes, methods, and blocks, where names are declared and interpreted.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="root1">
  <data key="d0">root1</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">root1 is a static method in Quadratic that returns the positive root using the formula (-b + Math.sqrt(determinant)) / (2*a).</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-ae06dfc90c814559f684ae92cc598260-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-05.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604436</data>
