<node id="Chapter 6: Objects And Data Structures">
  <data key="d0">Chapter 6: Objects And Data Structures</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The chapter discusses private variables, data abstraction, objects and data structures, and contrasting procedural and polymorphic shape examples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Abstraction">
  <data key="d0">Data Abstraction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The principle of representing data through abstract interfaces so users can manipulate the essence of data without knowing implementation details.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Structure">
  <data key="d0">Data Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An entity that exposes data and has no meaningful functions.&lt;SEP&gt;A software structure that exposes data, typically through public variables, and has no significant behavior; it is easy to add new behaviors to but hard to add new data structures to existing functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data/Object Anti-Symmetry">
  <data key="d0">Data/Object Anti-Symmetry</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The observation that the definitions of objects and data structures are complementary virtual opposites with far-reaching implications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Private Variables">
  <data key="d0">Private Variables</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Variables that are kept private to prevent other code from depending on the variables and to preserve freedom to change type or implementation.&lt;SEP&gt;Private Variables are hidden internal variables associated with objects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Getters And Setters">
  <data key="d0">Getters And Setters</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Accessor and mutator functions that programmers often add automatically to objects, exposing private variables as if public.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cartesian Plane">
  <data key="d0">Cartesian Plane</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The coordinate plane on which the Point examples represent locations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rectangular Coordinates">
  <data key="d0">Rectangular Coordinates</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The coordinate representation using x and y values that the concrete Point class exposes directly.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Polar Coordinates">
  <data key="d0">Polar Coordinates</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The coordinate representation using radius and angle values that the abstract Point interface can support through getR, getTheta, and setPolar.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Access Policy">
  <data key="d0">Access Policy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The behavioral contract enforced by methods, allowing independent reads but requiring coordinated settings of data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Vehicle">
  <data key="d0">Vehicle</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Java interface representing vehicle fuel data, shown with concrete gallon-based accessors and an abstract percentage-based accessor.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fuel Level">
  <data key="d0">Fuel Level</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Quantitative vehicle data indicating how much fuel is available, represented either in gallons or as a percentage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gallon">
  <data key="d0">Gallon</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A concrete unit used in the Vehicle example to express fuel tank capacity and gasoline amount.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Percentage">
  <data key="d0">Percentage</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An abstract unit used in the Vehicle example to express remaining fuel without exposing underlying volume or capacity.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Procedural Shape">
  <data key="d0">Procedural Shape</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A procedural design example in which a Geometry class operates on shape classes that are simple data structures without behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geometry">
  <data key="d0">Geometry</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Java class that contains an area function computing the area of Square, Rectangle, and Circle objects using instanceof checks and throwing NoSuchShapeException.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Square">
  <data key="d0">Square</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A shape data structure in the procedural example containing a topLeft Point and a side length.&lt;SEP&gt;Square is a Java class that implements Shape and stores a top-left Point and a side length, with an area method that returns side times side.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Rectangle">
  <data key="d0">Rectangle</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A shape data structure in the procedural example containing a topLeft Point, height, and width.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectangle is a Java class that implements Shape and stores a top-left Point, a height, and a width, with an area method that returns height times width.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shape">
  <data key="d0">Shape</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Java interface implemented by shape classes in the polymorphic solution.&lt;SEP&gt;Shape is a common software type or interface implemented by Square, Rectangle, and Circle, enabling polymorphic behavior through the area method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Polymorphic Shapes">
  <data key="d0">Polymorphic Shapes</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An object-oriented design example in which shape classes implement Shape and expose a polymorphic area method without a central Geometry class.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Area Function">
  <data key="d0">Area Function</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A function or method that computes the area of a shape, appearing as a Geometry function and as a polymorphic shape method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Perimeter Function">
  <data key="d0">Perimeter Function</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A hypothetical function that could be added to Geometry without affecting the procedural shape classes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="NoSuchShapeException">
  <data key="d0">NoSuchShapeException</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An exception thrown by Geometry.area when the supplied shape is not one of the recognized shapes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hiding Implementation">
  <data key="d0">Hiding Implementation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hiding implementation is the practice of concealing variable types, storage, or coordinate representation behind abstractions rather than exposing variables through simple accessors.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abstract Interfaces">
  <data key="d0">Abstract Interfaces</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Abstract interfaces are object-oriented contracts that let users manipulate the essence of data without knowing its implementation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Listing 6-6">
  <data key="d0">Listing 6-6</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Listing 6-6 is a code listing that presents polymorphic shape classes in Java.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Geometry Class">
  <data key="d0">Geometry Class</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Geometry Class is a class described as unnecessary in the polymorphic object-oriented shape solution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="area()">
  <data key="d0">area()</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">area() is a polymorphic method implemented by Square, Rectangle, and Circle to compute the area of each shape.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Visitor">
  <data key="d0">Visitor</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Visitor is an object-oriented design technique mentioned as a way around the object-oriented and procedural dichotomy, carrying independent costs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dual-Dispatch">
  <data key="d0">Dual-Dispatch</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Dual-Dispatch is a design technique mentioned alongside Visitor as a way around the object-oriented and procedural dichotomy, carrying independent costs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Procedural Code">
  <data key="d0">Procedural Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Procedural Code is code that uses data structures, making it easy to add new functions and hard to add new data structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Object-Oriented Code">
  <data key="d0">Object-Oriented Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Object-Oriented Code is code that uses objects and classes, making it easy to add new classes and hard to add new functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Structures">
  <data key="d0">Data Structures</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Data Structures are software entities that expose data and have no significant behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Data Structures are structures used by procedural code, where adding new functions is easy but adding new structures is hard.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Train Wreck">
  <data key="d0">Train Wreck</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Train Wreck is a code style involving chained method calls that is generally considered sloppy and may be assessed under Law of Demeter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getOptions">
  <data key="d0">getOptions</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">getOptions is a method called on ctxt in the example to obtain an Options object.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getScratchDir">
  <data key="d0">getScratchDir</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">getScratchDir is a method called on the return value of getOptions to obtain a ScratchDir object.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="getAbsolutePath">
  <data key="d0">getAbsolutePath</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">getAbsolutePath is a method called on the return value of getScratchDir to obtain an absolute path string.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ctxt">
  <data key="d0">ctxt</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">ctxt is an object in the example that includes options and is involved in a chained call.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Options">
  <data key="d0">Options</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An object exposed by Ctxt that references a Scratch Directory and exposes an absolute path attribute in the discussed code.&lt;SEP&gt;Options is a structure or object in the example that may contain a scratch directory and may be treated as an object or data structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ScratchDir">
  <data key="d0">ScratchDir</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ScratchDir is a directory object in the example that provides an absolute path and is involved in a chained call.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Apache Framework">
  <data key="d0">Apache Framework</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Apache Framework is a software framework in which the example code is reported to be found.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Beans">
  <data key="d0">Beans</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Beans is a framework or standard mentioned as demanding accessors and mutators even for simple data structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Accessor Function">
  <data key="d0">Accessor Function</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Accessor Function is a function that exposes internal data and can make Law of Demeter analysis less clear.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="New Functions">
  <data key="d0">New Functions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">New functions are additions to a code base that procedural code can add easily and object-oriented code can add only with changes to existing classes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="New Classes">
  <data key="d0">New Classes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">New classes are additions to an object-oriented code base that do not require changes to existing functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="New Data Types">
  <data key="d0">New Data Types</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">New data types are additions that complex systems may want at times, for which objects and object-oriented code are appropriate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="New Data Structures">
  <data key="d0">New Data Structures</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">New data structures are additions that procedural code finds hard to make because all functions must change.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="New Shape">
  <data key="d0">New Shape</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A new shape is a shape class added to an object-oriented solution without changing existing functions.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Existing Functions">
  <data key="d0">Existing Functions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Existing functions remain unaffected when a new shape is added in the object-oriented solution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Shape Classes">
  <data key="d0">Shape Classes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Shape classes are the classes such as Square, Rectangle, and Circle that must be changed when a new function is added.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Procedural Program">
  <data key="d0">Procedural Program</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A procedural program is a program structure that Visitor or dual-dispatch techniques can generally return to.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Complex System">
  <data key="d0">Complex System</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A complex system is a system in which both new data types and new functions may need to be added at different times.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Simple Data Structures">
  <data key="d0">Simple Data Structures</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Simple data structures are data structures with no behavior that naturally expose internal structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Everything Is An Object">
  <data key="d0">Everything Is An Object</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Everything Is An Object is the idea that all entities are objects, which the text identifies as a myth.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Method F">
  <data key="d0">Method F</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Method F is the method of Class C in the precise statement of Law of Demeter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Class C">
  <data key="d0">Class C</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Class C is the class whose instance variables and methods are referenced in the precise statement of Law of Demeter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Object Created By F">
  <data key="d0">Object Created By F</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Object Created By F is an object created by Method F and allowed to be used by Method F.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Object Passed As Argument">
  <data key="d0">Object Passed As Argument</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Object Passed As Argument is an object passed as an argument to Method F and allowed to be used by Method F.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Instance Variable">
  <data key="d0">Instance Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Instance Variable is a member variable of Class C that may hold an object.&lt;SEP&gt;Instance Variable is a class-level variable whose number and use by methods are discussed as central to evaluating class cohesion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Object Held In Instance Variable">
  <data key="d0">Object Held In Instance Variable</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Object Held In Instance Variable is an object held in an instance variable of Class C and allowed to be used by Method F.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Absolute Path">
  <data key="d0">Absolute Path</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A path value representing the full location of a directory or file in the discussed code.&lt;SEP&gt;Absolute Path is a file system path value associated with ScratchDir and returned by getAbsolutePath.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Variables">
  <data key="d0">Public Variables</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Public Variables are directly exposed variables that data structures could use to make structure clearer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Public Functions">
  <data key="d0">Public Functions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Public Functions are exposed operations associated with objects.&lt;SEP&gt;A category of class functions that should follow the list of variables and can be followed by private utilities.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Containing Module">
  <data key="d0">Containing Module</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Containing Module is the module that knows how to navigate through ctxt, Options, and ScratchDir in the train wreck example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calling Function">
  <data key="d0">Calling Function</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Calling Function is the function that navigates through multiple objects in the train wreck example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mature Programmers">
  <data key="d0">Mature Programmers</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Mature Programmers are experienced programmers who recognize that Everything Is An Object is a myth and sometimes prefer simple data structures with procedures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ctxt">
  <data key="d0">Ctxt</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A software object in the discussed module that contains Options and Scratch Directory information and is proposed to create a scratch file stream.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scratch Directory">
  <data key="d0">Scratch Directory</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A file-system directory whose absolute path is needed to build the path of a named scratch file.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Scratch File">
  <data key="d0">Scratch File</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A named file created in a Scratch Directory through a file stream, derived from the intent to use the Scratch Directory path.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FileOutputStream">
  <data key="d0">FileOutputStream</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Java output stream object used to write to a file path in the discussed code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BufferedOutputStream">
  <data key="d0">BufferedOutputStream</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A Java buffered output stream object constructed around a FileOutputStream to provide buffered writing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hybrid Structure">
  <data key="d0">Hybrid Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A design structure that is half object and half data structure, containing significant functions plus public variables or public accessors and mutators, making both new functions and new data structures hard to add.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Transfer Object">
  <data key="d0">Data Transfer Object</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A data structure, often a class with public variables and no functions, used to transfer data between sources such as databases and application code.&lt;SEP&gt;Data Transfer Object is a pattern in EJB2 beans used for structs with no behavior that hold data and often lead to redundant types and boilerplate copying code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bean">
  <data key="d0">Bean</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A common software style in which private variables are manipulated by getters and setters, providing quasi-encapsulation for data-like structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Active Record">
  <data key="d0">Active Record</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Active Record is a likely representation of hidden internal data inside objects.&lt;SEP&gt;A special form of data transfer object that is a data structure with public or bean-accessed variables and navigational methods such as save and find.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Database">
  <data key="d0">Database</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A data source that may provide raw data translated into data transfer objects or active records.&lt;SEP&gt;Database is a data store used by BankProxyHandler to retrieve and save account data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Application Code">
  <data key="d0">Application Code</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Code in an application that receives objects produced by translation stages such as data transfer objects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Business Rule">
  <data key="d0">Business Rule</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Application logic that should reside in separate objects rather than in an Active Record.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Accessors And Mutators">
  <data key="d0">Accessors And Mutators</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Accessors And Mutators are getter and setter methods, or public access and mutation methods, that expose private variables in beans and hybrid structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Database Table">
  <data key="d0">Database Table</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Database Table is a structured data source from which Active Records are typically direct translations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hiding Structure">
  <data key="d0">Hiding Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Hiding Structure is the design principle that objects should hide their internal structure and should not be navigated through to obtain internal details.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Output Directory">
  <data key="d0">Output Directory</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Output Directory is a local variable in the Java code that stores the absolute path of the scratch directory and is used to construct file paths.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Business Rules">
  <data key="d0">Business Rules</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Business Rules are operational rules that can be contained in separate objects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Behavior">
  <data key="d0">Behavior</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Behavior is functional activity exposed by objects and potentially added to objects or data structures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Procedures">
  <data key="d0">Procedures</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Procedures are computational approaches paired with data types when adding new behaviors is the priority.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Types">
  <data key="d0">Data Types</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Data Types are categories of data whose addition influences the choice between objects and other approaches.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Software Developers">
  <data key="d0">Software Developers</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Software Developers are practitioners who select objects or data types and procedures based on flexibility requirements.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-cf9b0cb392cf6be9fae672222887f4bd-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-06.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604467</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="1999">
  <data key="d0">1999</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">1999 is the publication year of Refactoring: Improving the Design of Existing Code.</data>
