</node>
<node id="Return Codes">
  <data key="d0">Return Codes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Return codes are an error reporting approach in which a caller checks a value returned by a method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Error Flag">
  <data key="d0">Error Flag</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An error flag is an error reporting approach in which a flag is set to indicate that an error occurred.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Try-Catch-Finally Statement">
  <data key="d0">Try-Catch-Finally Statement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A try-catch-finally statement is a programming construct that defines a scope for code that can abort and resume in a catch or finally block.&lt;SEP&gt;Try-Catch-Finally Statement is the try-catch-finally construct shown for a third-party library call that catches DeviceResponseException, ATM1212UnlockedException, and GMXError.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Device Shutdown Algorithm">
  <data key="d0">Device Shutdown Algorithm</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Device Shutdown Algorithm is the core procedure for shutting down a device, separated from error handling in the improved example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Listing 7-1">
  <data key="d0">Listing 7-1</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Listing 7-1 is a code listing showing DeviceController using non-exception error handling checks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Listing 7-2">
  <data key="d0">Listing 7-2</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Listing 7-2 is a code listing showing DeviceController using exceptions for device shutdown errors.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="FileNotFoundException">
  <data key="d0">FileNotFoundException</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">FileNotFoundException is a Java exception type thrown by the FileInputStream constructor when a file cannot be found.&lt;SEP&gt;FileNotFoundException is the specific exception type that the FileInputStream constructor can throw and that retrieveSection catches.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="StorageException">
  <data key="d0">StorageException</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">StorageException is an exception type thrown by retrieveSection to report retrieval errors.&lt;SEP&gt;StorageException is an exception class created in the sample code to report a retrieval error when FileNotFoundException occurs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="retrieveSection">
  <data key="d0">retrieveSection</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">retrieveSection is a method that retrieves a stored section and may throw StorageException when file access fails.&lt;SEP&gt;retrieveSection is a method that creates a FileInputStream from sectionName, closes the stream, handles FileNotFoundException, and returns an empty ArrayList of RecordedGrip.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="sectionStore">
  <data key="d0">sectionStore</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">sectionStore is a code object used in the unit test example to call retrieveSection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="RecordedGrip">
  <data key="d0">RecordedGrip</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">RecordedGrip is a data type used as an element type in the list returned by retrieveSection.&lt;SEP&gt;RecordedGrip is a type used as the element type of the ArrayList returned by retrieveSection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="retrieveSectionShouldThrowOnInvalidFileName">
  <data key="d0">retrieveSectionShouldThrowOnInvalidFileName</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">retrieveSectionShouldThrowOnInvalidFileName is a unit test method that verifies retrieveSection throws an exception for an invalid file name.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Chapter 7: Error Handling">
  <data key="d0">Chapter 7: Error Handling</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Chapter 7: Error Handling is a chapter in a clean code book that explains techniques for handling errors while preserving readable and robust code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Use Exceptions Rather Than Return Codes">
  <data key="d0">Use Exceptions Rather Than Return Codes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Use Exceptions Rather Than Return Codes is a coding guideline presented as a section heading, recommending exceptions over manual return-code or flag checks.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Write Your Try-Catch-Finally Statement First">
  <data key="d0">Write Your Try-Catch-Finally Statement First</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Write Your Try-Catch-Finally Statement First is a coding guideline presented as a section heading, recommending that try-catch-finally structure be designed before implementing exception-prone logic.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="retrieveDeviceRecord">
  <data key="d0">retrieveDeviceRecord</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">retrieveDeviceRecord is a method called during device shutdown to obtain a DeviceRecord for a device handle.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transaction">
  <data key="d0">Transaction</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Transaction is a concept used as an analogy to explain that try-catch-finally code should leave a program in a consistent state after an exception.&lt;SEP&gt;Transaction is a cross-cutting concern in EJB2 that defines transactional behavior and can be declared in deployment descriptors.&lt;SEP&gt;Transaction is a concern handled by EJB Architecture and listed as cross-cutting application infrastructure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unit Test">
  <data key="d0">Unit Test</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Unit Test is a testing practice used in the example to verify that retrieveSection throws StorageException for an invalid file name.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TDD">
  <data key="d0">TDD</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">TDD is a test-driven development approach used to add behavior inside the try block after defining the Try-Catch Structure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Checked Exceptions">
  <data key="d0">Checked Exceptions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Checked Exceptions are exception types that require handling or declaration in method signatures and can force cascading changes through calling code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unchecked Exceptions">
  <data key="d0">Unchecked Exceptions</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Unchecked Exceptions are exception types recommended over Checked Exceptions for general application development because Checked Exceptions create dependency costs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Open/Closed Principle">
  <data key="d0">Open/Closed Principle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Open/Closed Principle is a design principle violated when Checked Exceptions force lower-level changes to alter higher-level method signatures.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C#">
  <data key="d0">C#</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">C# is a programming language mentioned as not having Checked Exceptions while still allowing Robust Software.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Stack Trace">
  <data key="d0">Stack Trace</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Stack Trace is diagnostic information obtainable from any Java exception that indicates a call path but does not convey the intent of the failed operation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exception Classes">
  <data key="d0">Exception Classes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Exception Classes are categories of exceptions that should be defined around how callers need to catch errors rather than only around source or type.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Encapsulation">
  <data key="d0">Encapsulation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Encapsulation is a design property broken when every function in the path of a Checked Exception must know details of a low-level exception.&lt;SEP&gt;The practice of keeping variables and utility functions private, with protected or package-scope access as a last resort.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-4482a25ecfcd0fad59918839a6f47f2a-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-10.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606393</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ACMEPort">
  <data key="d0">ACMEPort</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ACMEPort is a Third-Party Library object instantiated with value 12 and opened in a sample try-catch-finally statement.&lt;SEP&gt;ACMEPort is an API class wrapped by LocalPort and associated with port opening and device response behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DeviceResponseException">
  <data key="d0">DeviceResponseException</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">DeviceResponseException is an exception type caught after an ACMEPort operation and passed to reportPortError and Logger.&lt;SEP&gt;DeviceResponseException is an exception thrown by ACMEPort and translated by LocalPort into PortDeviceFailure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ATM1212UnlockedException">
  <data key="d0">ATM1212UnlockedException</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ATM1212UnlockedException is an exception type caught after an ACMEPort operation and passed to reportPortError and Logger.&lt;SEP&gt;ATM1212UnlockedException is an exception thrown by ACMEPort and translated by LocalPort into PortDeviceFailure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GMXError">
  <data key="d0">GMXError</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">GMXError is an exception type caught after an ACMEPort operation and reported with a log message.&lt;SEP&gt;GMXError is an exception thrown by ACMEPort and translated by LocalPort into PortDeviceFailure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="reportPortError">
  <data key="d0">reportPortError</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">reportPortError is a method called for each caught exception in the sample code to record a port error.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Third-Party Library">
  <data key="d0">Third-Party Library</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Third-Party Library is a software library whose call is covered by the sample try-catch-finally statement and whose exceptions include DeviceResponseException, ATM1212UnlockedException, and GMXError.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Method Signature">
  <data key="d0">Method Signature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Method Signature is the declared interface of a method, including any throws clause that may be altered when Checked Exceptions are introduced lower in a call chain.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Calling Hierarchy">
  <data key="d0">Calling Hierarchy</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Calling Hierarchy is the chain of functions calling one another from top to bottom, through which Checked Exception changes can propagate.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Try-Catch Structure">
  <data key="d0">Try-Catch Structure</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Try-Catch Structure is the try-catch-finally construct used to define error handling scope and to build behavior in the try block.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transaction Scope">
  <data key="d0">Transaction Scope</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Transaction Scope is the scope of the try block whose transaction nature should be maintained while adding logic between stream creation and close.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Robust Software">
  <data key="d0">Robust Software</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Robust Software is the goal of exception handling and software design, described as achievable without Checked Exceptions in several languages.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Error Messages">
  <data key="d0">Error Messages</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Error Messages are informative messages that exceptions should carry to identify the failed operation and the type of failure.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Caller's Needs">
  <data key="d0">Caller's Needs</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Caller's Needs are the concerns of the code that catches exceptions, especially how exceptions are caught, and they should guide the definition of exception classes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Critical Library">
  <data key="d0">Critical Library</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Critical Library is a software library whose operations are critical enough that callers must catch exceptions, making checked exceptions potentially useful.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="General Application Development">
  <data key="d0">General Application Development</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">General Application Development is the common software development context in which the dependency costs of checked exceptions outweigh their benefits.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Standard Error Handling">
  <data key="d0">Standard Error Handling</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Standard Error Handling is the routine work of recording an error and ensuring execution can proceed, independent of the specific exception cause.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Error Context">
  <data key="d0">Error Context</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Error Context is sufficient information in an exception to determine the source and location of an error and the operation that failed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-98268aa1e2e5692cb0faaf2b6fae4a09-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-07.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604703</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LocalPort">
  <data key="d0">LocalPort</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">LocalPort is a Java wrapper class that encapsulates ACMEPort and translates device-related exceptions into PortDeviceFailure during port operations.</data>
