  <data key="d0">Clear Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The clear() operation available on Java.util.Map that allows a user to remove all entries from a map.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sensor">
  <data key="d0">Sensor</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A software type representing a sensor and intended to be stored in a map of sensor objects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Generics">
  <data key="d0">Generics</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Java language feature that supports type parameters such as a Map specialized for Sensor to make type intent clearer.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Sensors Class">
  <data key="d0">Sensors Class</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A class that encapsulates a Map and exposes a tailored getById operation for retrieving sensor objects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boundary Interface">
  <data key="d0">Boundary Interface</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An interface used at a system boundary, such as Map, that may introduce unnecessary capability and coupling into an application.&lt;SEP&gt;The Log4j boundary interface from which application code can be isolated by an encapsulating logger class.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Maintenance Change">
  <data key="d0">Maintenance Change</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The need to update many call sites when a widely passed interface, such as Map, changes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="GetById Method">
  <data key="d0">GetById Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The public getById method of Sensors that returns a Sensor by identifier and hides map access and casting.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Design And Business Rules">
  <data key="d0">Design And Business Rules</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Application-specific rules that a boundary class such as Sensors can enforce to prevent misuse.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Type Constraint">
  <data key="d0">Type Constraint</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A restriction on the kinds of objects that may be stored in a collection, which Map does not reliably enforce.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Casting">
  <data key="d0">Casting</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">The operation of converting a retrieved Object to a specific type, such as Sensor, when accessing a map.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 8-1">
  <data key="d0">Figure 8-1</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 8-1 is a figure in the chapter that lists the methods available on Java.util.Map.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Put Method">
  <data key="d0">Put Method</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Put Method is the put operation of Java.util.Map that adds a key-value pair to a map.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Map Of Sensor">
  <data key="d0">Map Of Sensor</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Map Of Sensor is a generic Java Map specialized to store Sensor objects and is used in the sensor examples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interface Provider">
  <data key="d0">Interface Provider</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Interface Provider is the role that supplies an interface and strives for broad applicability across many environments and audiences.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Interface User">
  <data key="d0">Interface User</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Interface User is the role that consumes an interface and wants it focused on particular needs.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Foreign Code">
  <data key="d0">Foreign Code</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Foreign Code is code that an application team does not fully control and must integrate with its own code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Internal Components">
  <data key="d0">Internal Components</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Internal Components are components or subsystems produced by teams in the company and integrated with the application's own code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Company">
  <data key="d0">Company</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Company is the organization whose teams produce components or subsystems for use in the software systems.&lt;SEP&gt;An anonymous company that wrote a popular killer app in the late 1980s, rushed the product to market, accumulated bad code, and went out of business.&lt;SEP&gt;Companies that can be brought down, driven into bankruptcy, or lose hundreds of millions of dollars because of messes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Third-Party Package">
  <data key="d0">Third-Party Package</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">External software package selected instead of a custom-built component; it exposes an API that the application calls for added functionality.&lt;SEP&gt;A third-party package is external code used by a team, which can change with new releases, introduce risk, and require verification that the package works as expected.&lt;SEP&gt;Third-Party Package is a purchased package that provides functionality to a software system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Framework">
  <data key="d0">Framework</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Framework is a third-party software framework that provides reusable functionality and interfaces.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Implementation Detail">
  <data key="d0">Implementation Detail</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Implementation Detail is a design choice that should be hidden from users, such as whether generics are used.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Tailored Interface">
  <data key="d0">Tailored Interface</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Tailored Interface is an interface constrained to the needs of an application and easier to understand and harder to misuse.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Design Convention">
  <data key="d0">Design Convention</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Design Convention is a convention that only particular types of objects can be stored in a Map.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Close Family Of Classes">
  <data key="d0">Close Family Of Classes</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Close Family Of Classes is a class or related group of classes where a boundary interface should be kept.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Using Third-Party Code">
  <data key="d0">Using Third-Party Code</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Using Third-Party Code is a section in Chapter 8: Boundaries that discusses interface tension and the use of Java.util.Map in applications.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Exploring and Learning Boundaries">
  <data key="d0">Exploring and Learning Boundaries</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Exploring and Learning Boundaries is a section in Chapter 8: Boundaries that discusses starting with third-party packages and testing third-party code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Log4j">
  <data key="d0">Log4j</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Apache third-party Java logging package used as the example external API; the package contains logging components such as Logger, Appender, ConsoleAppender, PatternLayout, and BasicConfigurator.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Apache">
  <data key="d0">Apache</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Organization named in the input as associated with the Log4j package.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Learning Tests">
  <data key="d0">Learning Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Tests written as controlled experiments to explore and verify understanding of a third-party API; they call the API as intended by the application and encode discovered behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Learning tests are precise experiments written to gain knowledge about an API, verify third-party packages, and detect behavioral differences when new releases occur. Learning tests are described as free and as having a positive return on investment.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Jim Newkirk">
  <data key="d0">Jim Newkirk</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Person credited in the input with naming the described tests as learning tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BeckTDD">
  <data key="d0">BeckTDD</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Referenced work cited with pages 136 through 137 in connection with the discussion of learning tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LogTest.java">
  <data key="d0">LogTest.java</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Java listing containing unit tests for Log4j, including tests for basic logging, adding a ConsoleAppender with a stream, and adding a ConsoleAppender without a stream.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Appender">
  <data key="d0">Appender</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Log4j component that receives log output and is associated with an output stream; the input describes a lack of output stream as an issue.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ConsoleAppender">
  <data key="d0">ConsoleAppender</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Log4j Appender that writes log output to the console; it can be constructed with a PatternLayout and an explicit system out stream.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="PatternLayout">
  <data key="d0">PatternLayout</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Log4j component that formats log messages using the pattern %p %t %m%n in the input examples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BasicConfigurator">
  <data key="d0">BasicConfigurator</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Log4j component invoked in the LogTest.java listing to configure basic logging behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Positive Return On Investment">
  <data key="d0">Positive Return On Investment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Benefit attributed to learning tests, which detect behavioral differences in new releases and verify expected third-party behavior after integration.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Third-Party API">
  <data key="d0">Third-Party API</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">External API exposed by a third-party package; learning tests call this API to verify expected behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Custom-Built Logger">
  <data key="d0">Custom-Built Logger</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Internal logger alternative to Apache Log4j; it can also act as a custom logger class that encapsulates Log4j usage.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Output Stream">
  <data key="d0">Output Stream</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Stream associated with a Log4j Appender for writing log output; the input notes an Appender lacking an output stream.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Root Logger">
  <data key="d0">Root Logger</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Log4j root logger obtained through Logger.getRootLogger() and cleaned up in LogTest.java.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="SystemOut">
  <data key="d0">SystemOut</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">ConsoleAppender constant or argument representing the system out stream used in the Log4j logging examples.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boundary Tests">
  <data key="d0">Boundary Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Boundary tests are tests that ease migration at a boundary and help prevent a team from staying with an old version longer than necessary.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Outbound Tests">
  <data key="d0">Outbound Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Outbound tests are a set of tests that exercise an interface in the same way production code does and support a clean boundary.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transmitter">
  <data key="d0">Transmitter</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Transmitter is a subsystem in a radio communications system that a team knew little about before the interface was defined. Transmitter can be keyed on a frequency and emit an analog representation of data from a stream.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Radio Communications System">
  <data key="d0">Radio Communications System</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Radio Communications System is a software system for which a team developed software and which includes the Transmitter subsystem.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Communications Controller">
  <data key="d0">Communications Controller</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Communications Controller is application code insulated from the Transmitter API by an application-specific interface and testable with a Fake Transmitter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transmitter Adapter">
  <data key="d0">Transmitter Adapter</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Transmitter Adapter is an adapter written after the Transmitter API was defined to bridge an application-specific Transmitter interface to the actual Transmitter API. Transmitter Adapter encapsulates interaction with the API and provides a single place to change when the API evolves.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Fake Transmitter">
  <data key="d0">Fake Transmitter</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Fake Transmitter is a suitable substitute used to test Communications Controller classes through a testing seam.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transmitter API">
  <data key="d0">Transmitter API</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Transmitter API is the initially undefined and externally controlled API for the Transmitter subsystem, later made available for use.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Adapter Pattern">
  <data key="d0">Adapter Pattern</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Adapter Pattern is a design pattern referenced in Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object Oriented Software that encapsulates interaction with an external API and provides a single place to change when the API evolves.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Seam">
  <data key="d0">Seam</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Seam is a convenient test seam in the code that allows testing Communications Controller classes using a Fake Transmitter and supports boundary tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Driven Development">
  <data key="d0">Test Driven Development</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Test Driven Development is a book by Kent Beck published by Addison-Wesley in 2003.&lt;SEP&gt;Test Driven Development is a software discipline that has made a profound impact on the industry and is tied to clean code through the requirement that code have tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Test Driven Development is a development method that asks programmers to write unit tests before writing production code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Working Effectively with Legacy Code">
  <data key="d0">Working Effectively with Legacy Code</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Working Effectively with Legacy Code is a book published by Addison-Wesley in 2004 and referenced for more information about seams.&lt;SEP&gt;A book authored by Michael Feathers.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-d58f62a4832abf3c8bbf730215895c6d-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-01.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606209</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Boundary">
  <data key="d0">Boundary</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Boundary is a separation between known and unknown code, where knowledge may be incomplete, change occurs, and clean separation with tests is needed.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clean Boundaries">
  <data key="d0">Clean Boundaries</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Clean boundaries are boundaries supported by clear separation, tests that define expectations, wrappers or adapters, and few references to third-party particulars.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transmitter Interface">
  <data key="d0">Transmitter Interface</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Transmitter Interface is an application-specific interface, named Transmitter in the text, that expresses the desired boundary behavior for Transmitter and includes the Transmit method.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Transmit">
  <data key="d0">Transmit</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Transmit is a method on the Transmitter Interface that takes a frequency and a data stream and directs the transmitter to key on the provided frequency and emit an analog representation of the data.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Frequency">
  <data key="d0">Frequency</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Frequency is the provided value used to key the transmitter in the desired Transmitter behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Data Stream">
  <data key="d0">Data Stream</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Data Stream is the stream of data passed to Transmit and converted into an analog representation by the transmitter.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Analog Representation">
  <data key="d0">Analog Representation</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Analog Representation is the analog form of the data coming from the Data Stream that the transmitter is expected to emit.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Migration">
  <data key="d0">Migration</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">Migration is the process of moving away from an old version of code or dependency, which boundary tests help ease.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Old Version">
  <data key="d0">Old Version</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Old Version is the previously used version of code or dependency that a team might retain too long without boundary tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="New Release">
  <data key="d0">New Release</data>
  <data key="d1">event</data>
  <data key="d2">New Release is a release of a third-party package that may introduce behavioral differences and risk.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Behavioral Differences">
  <data key="d0">Behavioral Differences</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Behavioral Differences are differences in observed behavior that learning tests can detect after a new release of a third-party package.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Risk">
  <data key="d0">Risk</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Risk is the potential for incompatibility or problems introduced when a third-party package changes or releases a new version.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Change">
  <data key="d0">Change</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Change is the occurrence of modification at boundaries or in third-party code, which good software designs and clean boundaries accommodate.&lt;SEP&gt;The act of modifying code, architecture, or design; tests enable change.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Good Software Designs">
  <data key="d0">Good Software Designs</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Good Software Designs are software designs that accommodate change without large investments or rework.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clear Separation">
  <data key="d0">Clear Separation</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Clear Separation is the separation of code at boundaries that supports clean boundaries and reduces dependency on third-party particulars.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Known">
  <data key="d0">Known</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Known is the part of the codebase or system that a team understands and controls before a boundary.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Unknown">
  <data key="d0">Unknown</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Unknown is the part of the codebase or system that lies beyond a boundary and is not yet defined or understood.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790604868</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Gamma Et Al">
  <data key="d0">Gamma Et Al</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">Gamma Et Al is the author group cited for Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object Oriented Software.&lt;SEP&gt;Gamma Et Al is the author group listed for the Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object Oriented Software book.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-207d517b4dd92803d5557692307191fb-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-08.txt&lt;SEP&gt;chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606383</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ray Ozzie">
  <data key="d0">Ray Ozzie</data>
  <data key="d1">person</data>
  <data key="d2">Ray Ozzie is a person identified as CTO of Microsoft Corporation and is quoted describing complexity as harmful to developers and difficult for product planning, building, and testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Microsoft Corporation">
  <data key="d0">Microsoft Corporation</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Microsoft Corporation is a corporation identified in the input as the organization where Ray Ozzie serves as CTO.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Complexity">
  <data key="d0">Complexity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Complexity is characterized as reducing developer vitality and making products difficult to plan, build, and test, motivating the system-level design discussion.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Software Teams">
  <data key="d0">Software Teams</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Software Teams are groups of developers whose organization is compared to city management, while the software systems used by the teams often lack equivalent separation of concerns and abstraction levels.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Software Systems">
  <data key="d0">Software Systems</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Software Systems are digital systems that software teams build and maintain, and that require clean design at higher abstraction levels, especially at the system level.&lt;SEP&gt;Systems unique compared with physical systems, and software system architectures can grow incrementally.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="System Level">
  <data key="d0">System Level</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">System Level is the higher level of abstraction addressed in the chapter, where construction, use, startup, and dependency resolution must remain organized and clean.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Startup Process">
  <data key="d0">Startup Process</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Startup Process is the phase in which application objects are constructed and dependencies are wired together before normal runtime operation begins.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Runtime Logic">
  <data key="d0">Runtime Logic</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Runtime Logic is the operational behavior of a software system after startup, separate from object construction and dependency wiring.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lazy Initialization/Evaluation">
  <data key="d0">Lazy Initialization/Evaluation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Lazy Initialization/Evaluation is an idiom that delays construction until first use, can speed startup and avoid null returns, but may mix construction logic with runtime behavior.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Service">
  <data key="d0">Service</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Service is a software dependency returned by a code example's getService method and used as the abstraction for an implementation such as MyServiceImpl.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MyServiceImpl">
  <data key="d0">MyServiceImpl</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">MyServiceImpl is a concrete software implementation referenced in a lazy initialization example as the object assigned to a Service dependency.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Double">
  <data key="d0">Test Double</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Test Double is a testing substitute used to replace heavyweight or concrete dependencies, such as a service implementation, during unit testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Mock Object">
  <data key="d0">Mock Object</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Mock Object is a testing object mentioned alongside Test Double that can be assigned to a service field to support unit testing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Order">
  <data key="d0">Order</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Order is a software object in an order processing example that can contain LineItem instances.&lt;SEP&gt;A software object that receives LineItem instances in an order processing system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Figure 11-1">
  <data key="d0">Figure 11-1</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Figure 11-1 is a figure titled Separating construction in main() that illustrates how main builds objects and passes them to the application.&lt;SEP&gt;A figure captioned Separating construction in main() that illustrates separation of construction in the main routine.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Modularity">
  <data key="d0">Modularity</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Modularity is described as a property that cities have through appropriate levels and that should be maintained in the startup process to avoid scattered, duplicated setup.&lt;SEP&gt;Modularity is the quality of separating a system into coherent, reusable parts. Aspect-Oriented Programming is described as a general-purpose approach to restoring modularity for cross-cutting concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;Modularity is a design property that, together with separation of concerns, enables decentralized management and decision making in large systems.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Hotel Under Construction">
  <data key="d0">Hotel Under Construction</data>
  <data key="d1">location</data>
  <data key="d2">Hotel Under Construction is a new hotel that is currently a bare concrete box with a construction crane and elevator outside and is expected to become a finished, clean building used by different people.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Order Processing System">
  <data key="d0">Order Processing System</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Order Processing System is a software system in which the application must create LineItem instances to add to an Order and can use Abstract Factory to separate construction details from application code.&lt;SEP&gt;An order processing application system in which the application creates LineItem instances to add to an Order.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Separation of Main">
  <data key="d0">Separation of Main</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Separation of Main is a design technique that moves all construction aspects to main or modules called by main and designs the rest of the system assuming objects have already been constructed and wired.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Get Service">
  <data key="d0">Get Service</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Get Service is a Java example method that returns a Service and lazily constructs MyServiceImpl when the service field is null, creating a hard-coded dependency and mixing construction with runtime logic.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Abstract Factory2">
  <data key="d0">Abstract Factory2</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A design pattern that gives an application control of when to build LineItem instances while separating construction details from application code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="OrderProcessing">
  <data key="d0">OrderProcessing</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An application component in an order processing system that creates LineItem instances, controls LineItem construction, and can provide application-specific constructor arguments.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Main Routine">
  <data key="d0">Main Routine</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A program entry point or main-side component from which dependencies point toward the OrderProcessing application.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LineItemFactoryImplementation">
  <data key="d0">LineItemFactoryImplementation</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An implementation located on the main side that holds the capability to build a LineItem.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Inversion of Control">
  <data key="d0">Inversion of Control</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A control mechanism that moves secondary responsibilities from one object to other objects dedicated to the purpose.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JNDI Lookups">
  <data key="d0">JNDI Lookups</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">A partial implementation of Dependency Injection in which an object asks a Directory Server to provide a service matching a particular name.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Directory Server">
  <data key="d0">Directory Server</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A server that provides a named service during JNDI Lookups.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="MyService">
  <data key="d0">MyService</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A service object retrieved from a Directory Server using the name NameOfMyService.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DI Container">
  <data key="d0">DI Container</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A container that instantiates required objects and uses constructor arguments or setter methods to wire dependencies.&lt;SEP&gt;DI Container is the dependency injection container that instantiates major objects and wires them together on demand.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spring Framework">
  <data key="d0">Spring Framework</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A software framework described as the best known DI Container for Java.&lt;SEP&gt;Spring Framework is a framework with features that make incorporation of annotation-based aspects easier for teams with limited AspectJ experience.&lt;SEP&gt;A software framework referenced in the bibliography at springframework.org.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="XML Configuration File">
  <data key="d0">XML Configuration File</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A configuration file used to define which objects to wire together in a DI Container, such as the Spring Framework.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lazy-Initialization">
  <data key="d0">Lazy-Initialization</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An idiom that constructs an object only when needed and is still sometimes useful with Dependency Injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Lazy-Evaluation">
  <data key="d0">Lazy-Evaluation</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An optimization related to lazy instantiation that can be supported by DI Container mechanisms such as factories or proxies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Iterative and Incremental Agility">
  <data key="d0">Iterative and Incremental Agility</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">An approach that implements today's stories and then refactors and expands the system to implement new stories.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="System Architecture">
  <data key="d0">System Architecture</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The architecture of a software system that can grow incrementally when proper Separation of Concerns is maintained.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EJB1">
  <data key="d0">EJB1</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An original enterprise bean architecture that did not separate concerns appropriately.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EJB2">
  <data key="d0">EJB2</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">EJB2 is a heavyweight Java Enterprise Beans architecture that requires beans to subclass container types and provide lifecycle methods, causing business logic to be tightly coupled to the EJB Container.&lt;SEP&gt;An original enterprise bean architecture that did not separate concerns appropriately.&lt;SEP&gt;Earlier EJB standard associated with tight-coupling problems and less clean code than EJB3.&lt;SEP&gt;EJB2 is an earlier EJB code architecture mentioned as original code and as invasive compared with annotation-based code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Entity Bean">
  <data key="d0">Entity Bean</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An in-memory representation of relational data, described as a table row.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bank">
  <data key="d0">Bank</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A persistent class for which an Entity Bean is considered in the EJB counterexample.&lt;SEP&gt;Bank is a Java interface and example application abstraction that defines methods for getting and setting a collection of Account objects.&lt;SEP&gt;Bank is the abstract implementation class for the Bank EJB in the com.example.banking package. It implements EntityBean, exposes address accessors and account collection methods, and includes addAccount logic that uses a JNDI lookup to create an account.&lt;SEP&gt;Banking domain object or model class that is proxied by a data access object and rewritten as an EJB3 entity with annotations.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Relational Data">
  <data key="d0">Relational Data</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">Data represented by an Entity Bean as a table row.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BankLocal">
  <data key="d0">BankLocal</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">BankLocal is a local EJB2 interface for the Bank EJB that exposes getter and setter methods for address fields and account collection operations.&lt;SEP&gt;An EJB2 local interface for a Bank EJB that extends EJBLocalObject.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EJBLocalObject">
  <data key="d0">EJBLocalObject</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">EJBLocalObject is the Java interface extended by BankLocal, marking BankLocal as a local EJB object.&lt;SEP&gt;An interface extended by the BankLocal EJB2 local interface.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
