  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Invoking Object">
  <data key="d0">Invoking Object</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">An object that actively resolves a dependency in JNDI Lookups without controlling the exact object returned.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dependency">
  <data key="d0">Dependency</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A required object or service that can be resolved actively through JNDI Lookups or injected passively through Dependency Injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dependency Management">
  <data key="d0">Dependency Management</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The management of object dependencies to which Inversion of Control is applied by Dependency Injection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Authoritative Mechanism">
  <data key="d0">Authoritative Mechanism</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A mechanism, usually a main routine or special-purpose container, that takes responsibility for instantiating dependencies.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Ephemeral Nature">
  <data key="d0">Ephemeral Nature</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The transient characteristic of Software Systems that makes incremental architecture growth possible.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bank EJB">
  <data key="d0">Bank EJB</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Bank EJB is an EJB2 entity bean representing a bank, with address fields and a collection of accounts whose data is handled by separate Account EJB components.&lt;SEP&gt;An EJB associated with a persistent Bank class and represented by a local interface in Listing 11-1.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JndiContext">
  <data key="d0">JndiContext</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A context object used to look up NameOfMyService and obtain MyService in the JNDI example.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EntityBean">
  <data key="d0">EntityBean</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">EntityBean is an EJB2 container type implemented by the Bank EJB implementation class, requiring abstract methods and container lifecycle methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Account EJB">
  <data key="d0">Account EJB</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Account EJB is a separate EJB2 bean that handles the data for an individual account owned by a Bank EJB.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AccountDTO">
  <data key="d0">AccountDTO</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">AccountDTO is a data transfer object passed to Bank EJB to represent account data and to support creation of an Account EJB.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="LocalHome">
  <data key="d0">LocalHome</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">LocalHome is a factory interface for creating EJB objects, described as the corresponding local home interface for the Bank EJB.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AccountHomeLocal">
  <data key="d0">AccountHomeLocal</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">AccountHomeLocal is a local home interface looked up in the InitialContext and used to create an Account EJB from an AccountDTO.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Deployment Descriptor">
  <data key="d0">Deployment Descriptor</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Deployment Descriptor is an XML descriptor for EJB2 components that specifies object-relational mapping details to a persistence store, transactional behavior, and security constraints.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EJB Container">
  <data key="d0">EJB Container</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">EJB Container is the heavyweight runtime environment to which EJB2 business logic is tightly coupled, requiring beans to subclass container types and implement lifecycle methods.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Persistence">
  <data key="d0">Persistence</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Persistence is a cross-cutting concern in EJB2 involving storing and retrieving objects, potentially using a particular DBMS or flat files with naming conventions and transactional semantics.&lt;SEP&gt;Persistence is a concern involving storing and retrieving data, and it is used as an example of an application infrastructure concern.&lt;SEP&gt;Cross-cutting concern mentioned in the required application infrastructure list.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Security">
  <data key="d0">Security</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Security is a cross-cutting concern in EJB2 whose behavior can be declared in deployment descriptors independently of source code.&lt;SEP&gt;Security is a concern handled by EJB Architecture and listed as cross-cutting application infrastructure.&lt;SEP&gt;Cross-cutting concern mentioned in the required application infrastructure list.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Aspect-Oriented Programming">
  <data key="d0">Aspect-Oriented Programming</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Aspect-Oriented Programming is a general-purpose approach to restoring modularity for cross-cutting concerns by using aspects to modify behavior at consistent points in a system.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspect-Oriented Programming is a general-purpose approach for restoring modularity for cross-cutting concerns by specifying behavior modifications at points in a system.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Aspect">
  <data key="d0">Aspect</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Aspect is a modular construct in Aspect-Oriented Programming that specifies points in a system whose behavior should be modified to support a particular concern.&lt;SEP&gt;Aspect is a modular construct in Aspect-Oriented Programming that specifies which points in a system should have their behavior modified consistently to support a particular concern.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Object-Relational Mapping">
  <data key="d0">Object-Relational Mapping</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Object-Relational Mapping is the mapping detail specified by XML deployment descriptors for mapping EJB objects to a persistence store.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Cross-Cutting Concern">
  <data key="d0">Cross-Cutting Concern</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Cross-Cutting Concern is a concern that cuts across the natural object boundaries of a domain, such as persistence, security, and transactions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cross-Cutting Concern is a system-wide concern such as persistence, transactions, security, caching, or failover that can be addressed through Aspect-Oriented Programming.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="InitialContext">
  <data key="d0">InitialContext</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">InitialContext is a JNDI context object used in the Bank addAccount method to look up the AccountHomeLocal home interface by its string name.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AccountLocal">
  <data key="d0">AccountLocal</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">AccountLocal is the local EJB interface for an Account object returned by AccountHomeLocal.create and added to the Bank account collection.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Bank Finder Methods">
  <data key="d0">Bank Finder Methods</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Bank Finder Methods are possible query methods that could be added to the Bank EJB for locating objects. They are mentioned but not shown in the excerpt.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="DBMS">
  <data key="d0">DBMS</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">DBMS is a database management system mentioned as a particular persistence target that can be used instead of flat files when persisting objects with consistent naming conventions and transactional semantics.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-002</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="EJB Architecture">
  <data key="d0">EJB Architecture</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">EJB Architecture is an architecture that handled persistence, security, and transactions and is described as having anticipated Aspect-Oriented Programming.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Java Proxy">
  <data key="d0">Java Proxy</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Java Proxy is a mechanism suitable for simple situations such as wrapping method calls in individual objects or classes; JDK dynamic proxies work with interfaces, while class proxying requires byte-code manipulation libraries.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Javassist">
  <data key="d0">Javassist</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Javassist is a byte-code manipulation library mentioned as an option for proxying classes.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BankImpl">
  <data key="d0">BankImpl</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">BankImpl is a Plain-Old Java Object that implements the Bank interface and contains business logic for getting and setting accounts.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="BankProxyHandler">
  <data key="d0">BankProxyHandler</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">BankProxyHandler is an InvocationHandler implementation that intercepts Bank method calls, adds persistence behavior, delegates to BankImpl, and interacts with a database.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="InvocationHandler">
  <data key="d0">InvocationHandler</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">InvocationHandler is a Java proxy API interface implemented by BankProxyHandler to handle method invocations made to a proxy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Proxy API">
  <data key="d0">Proxy API</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Proxy API is the Java proxy API that requires an InvocationHandler object to implement method calls made to a proxy.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Java Reflection API">
  <data key="d0">Java Reflection API</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Java Reflection API is the reflection API used by BankProxyHandler to map generic method invocations to corresponding methods in BankImpl.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Spring AOP">
  <data key="d0">Spring AOP</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Spring AOP is a Java framework that uses proxies internally, supports business logic written as POJOs, and incorporates cross-cutting concerns through declarative configuration files or APIs.&lt;SEP&gt;Spring AOP is a pure Java aspect-oriented programming approach described as sufficient for a large majority of aspect cases.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="JBoss AOP">
  <data key="d0">JBoss AOP</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">JBoss AOP is a Java framework that uses proxies internally to implement aspects in pure Java and whose library aspects can be specified declaratively.&lt;SEP&gt;JBoss AOP is a pure Java aspect-oriented programming approach described as sufficient for a large majority of aspect cases.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Domain">
  <data key="d0">Domain</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Domain is the business area or domain logic on which POJOs focus.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Business Logic">
  <data key="d0">Business Logic</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Business Logic is the core domain behavior represented by BankImpl and written as POJOs in Spring AOP.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Caching">
  <data key="d0">Caching</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Caching is a cross-cutting application infrastructure concern mentioned alongside persistence, transactions, security, and failover.&lt;SEP&gt;Cross-cutting concern mentioned in the required application infrastructure list.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Failover">
  <data key="d0">Failover</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Failover is a cross-cutting application infrastructure concern mentioned alongside persistence, transactions, security, and caching.&lt;SEP&gt;Cross-cutting concern mentioned in the required application infrastructure list.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AOSD">
  <data key="d0">AOSD</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">AOSD is a reference cited for general information on aspects.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AspectJ">
  <data key="d0">AspectJ</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">AspectJ is an extension of Java that provides first-class support for aspects as modularity constructs and a rich tool set for separating concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;AspectJ is an aspect-oriented programming technology referenced for AspectJ-specific information.&lt;SEP&gt;AspectJ is a technology that is excluded from the Pure Java approach.&lt;SEP&gt;A software artifact referenced by eclipse.org/aspectj and covered in the book Eclipse AspectJ.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-005&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-006</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Colyer">
  <data key="d0">Colyer</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Colyer is a reference cited for AspectJ-specific information.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Goetz">
  <data key="d0">Goetz</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Goetz is a reference cited for detailed examples of the Proxy API and its use.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="AOP Framework">
  <data key="d0">AOP Framework</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">AOP Framework is a framework that makes noninvasive behavior modifications to target code to support particular concerns in Aspect-Oriented Programming.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Target Code">
  <data key="d0">Target Code</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Target Code is the existing code whose behavior is modified noninvasively by an AOP framework without manual editing of the source code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Execution Point Of Interest">
  <data key="d0">Execution Point Of Interest</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Execution Point Of Interest is a system-wide execution point whose behavior can be specified or modified, and which is needed for a true Aspect-Oriented Programming solution.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Pure Java AOP Framework">
  <data key="d0">Pure Java AOP Framework</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Pure Java AOP Framework is a category of Java frameworks that use proxies internally to implement aspects in pure Java.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Declarative Configuration Files">
  <data key="d0">Declarative Configuration Files</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Declarative Configuration Files are configuration artifacts used to specify application infrastructure and cross-cutting concerns in frameworks such as Spring.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="User Stories">
  <data key="d0">User Stories</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Requirements statements that help ensure correct implementation and future maintenance and evolution of code.&lt;SEP&gt;User Stories are units of desired functionality whose efficient delivery should receive the majority of a team's creative efforts.&lt;SEP&gt;User Stories are requirement-level stories used to verify that business logic is implemented correctly and to guide maintenance and evolution of code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-004&lt;SEP&gt;doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Byte-Code Manipulation Library">
  <data key="d0">Byte-Code Manipulation Library</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Byte-Code Manipulation Library is a type of library, including CGLIB, ASM, and Javassist, used to proxy classes by manipulating byte code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Listing 11-3">
  <data key="d0">Listing 11-3</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Listing 11-3 is a code listing that shows the skeleton for a JDK proxy providing persistence support for the Bank application.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-f8b0f36a5c42d0808fec00a058082be8-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-11.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790605586</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
