<node id="C++ Program">
  <data key="d0">C++ Program</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A C++ Program written in the mid-1990s for an embedded real-time system that implemented a simple timer and used a Command class.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Embedded Real-Time System">
  <data key="d0">Embedded Real-Time System</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An embedded real-time system for which a C++ timer program was written in the mid-1990s.&lt;SEP&gt;Embedded Real-Time System is the application category used to describe the environment control system, with likely constrained computer and memory resources.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="ScheduleCommand">
  <data key="d0">ScheduleCommand</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">ScheduleCommand is a method of Timer that schedules a Command to execute in a new thread after a specified number of milliseconds.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Driver Program">
  <data key="d0">Driver Program</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Driver Program is a simple program cobbled together to listen to the keyboard and manually test the C++ timer program by scheduling commands that repeat typed characters later.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Source Package">
  <data key="d0">Source Package</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Source Package is a package into which unit tests and production code are checked in together.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Agile Movement">
  <data key="d0">Agile Movement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Agile Movement is a software development movement mentioned together with Test Driven Development as encouraging programmers to write automated unit tests.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Object Mentor">
  <data key="d0">Object Mentor</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Object Mentor is an organization associated with Robert C. Martin in the article citation.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="IEEE Software">
  <data key="d0">IEEE Software</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">IEEE Software is a publication in which the article Professionalism and Test-Driven Development appeared.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Professionalism and Test-Driven Development">
  <data key="d0">Professionalism and Test-Driven Development</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">Professionalism and Test-Driven Development is an article by Robert C. Martin in IEEE Software cited as the source for the Three Laws of TDD.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="First Law">
  <data key="d0">First Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">First Law of Test Driven Development states that production code may not be written until a failing unit test has been written.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Second Law">
  <data key="d0">Second Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Second Law of Test Driven Development states that no more unit test code should be written than is sufficient to fail, and not compiling counts as failing.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Third Law">
  <data key="d0">Third Law</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Third Law of Test Driven Development states that no more production code should be written than is sufficient to pass the currently failing unit test.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Failing Unit Test">
  <data key="d0">Failing Unit Test</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Failing Unit Test is a unit test that fails, required by the First Law before production code is written.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="TDD Cycle">
  <data key="d0">TDD Cycle</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">TDD Cycle is a short cycle, perhaps thirty seconds long, in which unit tests and production code are written together with unit tests just a few seconds ahead of production code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dirty Tests">
  <data key="d0">Dirty Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Test code with a messy, tangled structure that becomes harder to maintain and can function like absent tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Dirty Tests are unit tests written with poor naming, design, partitioning, or other low quality standards, making them harder to maintain as production code evolves.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Coached Team">
  <data key="d0">Coached Team</data>
  <data key="d1">organization</data>
  <data key="d2">Coached Team is a software team coached by the author. The team explicitly allowed unit tests to be maintained to lower quality standards than production code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="C++ Programming Language">
  <data key="d0">C++ Programming Language</data>
  <data key="d1">other</data>
  <data key="d2">C++ Programming Language is the programming language used for the mid-1990s embedded real-time timer program described in the text.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Standard Timing Functions">
  <data key="d0">Standard Timing Functions</data>
  <data key="d1">method</data>
  <data key="d2">Standard Timing Functions are operating system timing functions that production code may call directly or that unit tests may mock to control time.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Keyboard">
  <data key="d0">Keyboard</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">Keyboard is the input device listened to by the driver program, where typed characters were used to schedule delayed commands.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-000</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="No Tests">
  <data key="d0">No Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The absence of a test suite, removing assurance that code changes work as expected.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Clean Tests">
  <data key="d0">Clean Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Unit tests kept readable, simple, and maintained to the same standard as production code.&lt;SEP&gt;Clean Tests are kept clean, expressive, and succinct, and they preserve and enhance the flexibility, maintainability, and reusability of production code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Coverage">
  <data key="d0">Test Coverage</data>
  <data key="d1">data</data>
  <data key="d2">A measure of production code exercised by tests; higher coverage reduces fear of change.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Automated Suite of Unit Tests">
  <data key="d0">Automated Suite of Unit Tests</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">A machine-executable collection of unit tests covering production code.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
