<node id="Testing API">
  <data key="d0">Testing API</data>
  <data key="d1">artifact</data>
  <data key="d2">An API whose code is described as having a different set of engineering standards than production code while still requiring simplicity, succinctness, and expressiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Testing API is an invented interface that helps write tests and acts as a domain-specific language.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-005</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Test Environment">
  <data key="d0">Test Environment</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The execution environment for test code, described as having needs that differ from a production environment and as less likely to be constrained.&lt;SEP&gt;Test Environment is an environment where operations involving inefficiency may be acceptable, as part of the dual standard.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Dual Standard">
  <data key="d0">Dual Standard</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The principle that test code and production code may follow different engineering standards, especially regarding efficiency while not compromising cleanliness.&lt;SEP&gt;Dual Standard is the difference in acceptable practices between production and test environments, involving memory or CPU efficiency but not cleanliness.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606382</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="One Assert Per Test">
  <data key="d0">One Assert Per Test</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A proposed rule that every test function in a JUnit test should contain one and only one assert statement.&lt;SEP&gt;One Assert Per Test is a school of thought stating that every JUnit test function should have one and only one assert statement, and the author treats the rule as a guideline while recommending that assertions be minimized.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-003&lt;SEP&gt;doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-004</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606383</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Assert Statement">
  <data key="d0">Assert Statement</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A statement used in a JUnit test function, referenced in the rule limiting each test function to one assert statement.</data>
  <data key="d3">doc-e6b59836a05af0c112c0d24b77e2c123-chunk-003</data>
  <data key="d4">chapter-09.txt</data>
  <data key="d5">1790606383</data>
