</edge>
<edge source="Automatic Code Completion" target="Meaningful Distinctions">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Automatic code completion is more helpful when similar names sort together and their differences are obvious, supporting meaningful distinctions.</data>
  <data key="d9">benefits from</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Information" target="Disinformation">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Consistent spelling and naming produce information, while inconsistent spelling and naming produce disinformation.</data>
  <data key="d9">contrast,opposition</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Information" target="Meaningful Distinctions">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Meaningful distinctions make names informative by aligning spelling and naming with meaning.</data>
  <data key="d9">supports</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Disinformation" target="Lower-Case L">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Lower-case l as a variable name is an example of disinformative naming.</data>
