  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Disinformation" target="Uppercase O">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Uppercase O as a variable name is an example of disinformative naming.</data>
  <data key="d9">visual disinformation</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Disinformation" target="Name">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Using Name for a floating-point number would break the rule against disinformation.</data>
  <data key="d9">disinformation,unexpected type</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Disinformation" target="Renaming">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Renaming is presented as a simple and final way to conquer problems that otherwise create disinformation.</data>
  <data key="d9">counters,solves</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lower-Case L" target="One">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Lower-case l can look almost entirely like the numeric constant one.</data>
  <data key="d9">visual similarity</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lower-Case L" target="Uppercase O">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Using lower-case l and uppercase O together creates particularly confusing variable names.</data>
  <data key="d9">ambiguous combination</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Lower-Case L" target="Renaming">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Renaming can resolve ambiguity caused by using lower-case l as a variable name.</data>
  <data key="d9">resolves</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Uppercase O" target="Zero">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Uppercase O can look almost entirely like the numeric constant zero.</data>
  <data key="d9">visual similarity</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Uppercase O" target="O1">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">O1 begins with uppercase O, which can be mistaken for zero.</data>
  <data key="d9">contains,visual similarity</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Uppercase O" target="Renaming">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Renaming can resolve ambiguity caused by using uppercase O as a variable name.</data>
  <data key="d9">resolves</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Interpreter" target="Number-Series Naming">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">An interpreter can be satisfied by number-series names, but that satisfaction is not enough for meaningful naming.</data>
  <data key="d9">satisfies,syntactic acceptance</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Meaningful Distinctions" target="Noise Words">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Noise words make names different without making them mean something different, undermining meaningful distinctions.</data>
  <data key="d9">meaningless distinction,opposes</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Meaningful Distinctions" target="Prefix Conventions">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Prefix conventions such as a and the are acceptable when they make a meaningful distinction, such as local variables versus function arguments.</data>
  <data key="d9">compatible,meaningful distinction</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
  <data key="d12">1790603358</data>
  <data key="d13"></data>
</edge>
<edge source="Meaningful Distinctions" target="Number-Series Naming">
  <data key="d7">1.0</data>
  <data key="d8">Number-series naming opposes meaningful distinctions because it provides no clue to the author's intention.</data>
  <data key="d9">opposes</data>
  <data key="d10">doc-f209a7a16ee116e66dcaca0799c06baa-chunk-001</data>
  <data key="d11">chapter-02.txt</data>
